/avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/281068747?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs","id":281068747,"login":"wakir-labs","node_id":"U_kgDOEMDEyw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/396f4bf76f1bfaa2898485bfd5faf6d9a7b6c8f9/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-23T08:45:09Z","email":"xypu4r8e@addy.io","name":"wakir-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-23T08:45:09Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Give the identity substrate a root that outlives a day, and an anchor that exists (#561)\n\n* Give the identity substrate a root that outlives a day, and an anchor that exists\n\nADR-0076 steps 1-6. Step 0 (bind_address) landed with #553, step 7\n(healthcheck exec form) with #556; both verified at main before this\nbranch started.\n\nThe substrate stopped attesting on 2026-05-17 and nobody noticed for\n127 days. Two properties produced that, and each looks harmless alone:\n\n  * the agents'' `trust_bundle_path` pointed at a volume the bootstrap\n    created and never wrote. Measured on both pilot VMs 2026-09-22:\n    empty since May.\n  * the servers signed without an UpstreamAuthority, minting a fresh\n    self-signed root every `ca_ttl` and pruning the expired one. A\n    staged anchor would have been worthless within a day anyway.\n\nSo both halves, in that order.\n\nThe root (`UpstreamAuthority \"disk\"`, three federation configs). The\nserver now mints intermediates against a self-signed EC P-256 root the\nbootstrap creates into its own volume - 0600, uid 1000, read-only\nmount, not in this repository and never to be. It is created once. A\nre-run keeps it; a half-present pair aborts rather than heals, because\nrolling the root invalidates every SVID and bundle on both federation\nsides and that is a bilateral maintenance window, not a side effect of\nrunning the bootstrap again.\n\nThe anchor (`bin/wakir-spire-stage-bootstrap-anchor`, new step between\nserver start and agent start). Source is `spire-server bundle show\n-format spiffe` against the running server via podman exec - SPIRE''s\nown CLI, no local crypto. Validated before anything is written, temp\nfile in the target directory then rename, read back and stat-verified.\nEvery failure path exits 2 with a diagnosis and leaves the previous\nanchor untouched; there is no branch on which it exits 0 without a\nverified file on disk. A timer re-runs the same script every 30\nminutes, because `ca_ttl` stays at 24h by decision and a once-staged\nanchor would reproduce the May half-life.\n\n`ca_ttl` stays at 24h and the comment above it is corrected in both\nplaces it stood. It claimed we had shortened a thirty-day production\ndefault for test purposes. We had not: 24h is SPIRE''s documented\ndefault and we left it standing and wrote a rationale next to it.\n\n`rebootstrap_mode = \"auto\"` in both agent configs. The default is\n`never` and it applied, so the agent was not only without an anchor but\nforbidden to fetch one. It is inert until the NodeAttestor can\nre-attest - `join_token` cannot - and both files say so rather than\nletting the next reader believe the switch does something. That is\nwave 2 and it is Reza''s.\n\nThe two `spire-fed-bundle` CLIs are marked fixture-only in their\ndocstrings and in the README, above the fold in both. They emit JWKS\nwhose keys are not SPIRE CA keys; wired into staging they produce a\nfile at the right path, a green check, and an agent that still cannot\nattest.\n\nTests: 28 vectors, of which eight are negative controls on the stager\n(empty, whitespace, non-JSON, truncated, zero keys, keys-not-a-list,\nexec failure, and the refresh path preserving a good anchor across all\nof them) and four on the root (half-present, unparseable, mismatched\nkey, no regeneration on re-run). One mutation control cuts the\nkey-count guard out of a copy of the stager and asserts the zero-key\nvector then goes green - without it, a passing negative control could\nequally be scaffolding that rejects everything.\n\nThe module is named in the required hermetic-invariants lane rather\nthan left in the observation lane. A module whose whole point is that\nits checks can fail does not belong behind a non-blocking gate.\n\nWhat this does not do: no cutover. The root change is bilateral and\noperator-hand. And it does not establish that the agent''s SELinux\ncontext may read the staged anchor - the bundles volume carries a\nprivate relabel on both the server and the agent mount, which wants a\nmeasurement on an enforcing node and has not had one. Written into the\nstager, the README and the PR rather than left to be discovered.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Drop the persona signature the archaeology lint enforces against\n\nThe vocabulary lint flags persona names in the tree and it is right to.\nBoth new files now sign the way the neighbouring hermetic modules do.\nCaught by tests/ci/test_archaeology_lint.py on the full local run, not\nin review.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Guard the one property the acceptance window has no second signal for\n\nADR-0076 errata E3 names the stager''s unconditional write as\nload-bearing: the seven-day harness in #562 measures the re-staging\ncadence at the anchor''s mtime, and under UpstreamAuthority \"disk\" the\npublished bundle is the long-lived root, so the staged document is\nbyte-identical from one run to the next for years. Z4''s overlap half\nis constant-true under that configuration; the mtime is the only thing\nleft that can tell a running timer from a stopped one.\n\nA later \"skip the rename when nothing changed\" -- the obvious\noptimisation, and a correct-looking one -- would therefore freeze the\nmtime on a healthy substrate and turn an improvement into a red\nwindow. Measured on this branch today: three runs against a mock\nserver returning byte-identical output produce three distinct mtimes\nand three distinct inodes. The property holds; it was nowhere\nasserted.\n\nTV-0076-18 asserts it. TV-0076-19 splices exactly that short-circuit\ninto a copy of the script and requires the mtime to stand still, so\nthe vector above is shown to measure the write rather than the\nscaffolding.\n\nNo lane count change: two vectors in a module that is already required.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Measure the rewrite on any filesystem, not on the runner I happened to use\n\nMy own TV-0076-18 went red in CI on the assertion I added as a bonus:\nthe anchor''s inode must change across a re-run. It does not have to.\n`mv -f` unlinks the previous file and the next temp file is free to\nland on the recycled inode number, which is exactly what the hermetic\nlane observed (8942448 twice). Inode reuse is a property of the\nfilesystem, not of the write.\n\nThe sleep-and-compare for the mtime had the same weakness from the\nother side: it assumes sub-second timestamp granularity.\n\nBoth replaced by back-dating the anchor between runs and requiring the\nstager to move it forward again. That is what Z4 actually reads --\n\"the file was written again\" -- and it holds on any filesystem and at\nany timestamp resolution. The mutation control back-dates identically,\nso the spliced-in short-circuit still has to leave the stale stamp in\nplace.\n\nSelf-repair of a fail I put in one commit earlier; no scope beyond the\ntwo vectors from 682124f.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* The stager counted keys; the agent needs certificates\n\nZone-L cross-review, BL-1, measured against a real SPIRE-Agent v1.14.6:\na bundle holding exactly one jwt-svid key and no X.509 authority passed\nthe old `\"kty\"` count, staged green -- and the agent answered\n`no certificates found in trust bundle` in a retry loop that never ends.\nFile at the right path, right mode, right owner, stager reporting\nsuccess, substrate dead. That is the defect class this ADR was written\nagainst, produced by the one check whose entire job is to decide whether\nan anchor is an anchor.\n\nThe invariant is now the sharp one Reza recommended: at least one entry\nwith `use == \"x509-svid\"` and a non-empty `x5c`, and among those the\ncertificate of our own upstream CA root. `x5c` is base64 DER and a PEM\nfile is base64 DER between two armour lines, so the comparison is a\nstring comparison -- no openssl, no crypto, no new dependency. It closes\nthree things at once: the JWT-only anchor, a fixture JWKS from the\nspire-fed-bundle CLIs in this same directory, and the peer''s bundle\nstaged into our volume by swapped overrides. \"At least one\" and not\n\"all\", because a bilateral root cutover legitimately puts both roots in\nthe bundle at the same time.\n\nAn unreachable root is exit 3 and nothing staged -- explicitly not a\nquiet fall-back to the weaker check.\n\nNM-2, the parser. The decider is now a real JSON parser, with the\ndependency-free shape check demoted to a pre-check. python3 when it is\non PATH, otherwise jq in --slurp mode, and exit 3 when neither is. The\nslurp is not decoration: plain jq reads a *stream* of JSON values, so a\ndocument emitted twice parses cleanly, which is one of the two holes\nmeasured in review. The other was a trailing comma sailing through on\nany node without jq.\n\nA correction to the order that carried this fix: python3 is NOT on the\nlive nodes. SRE measured both VMs on 2026-09-22 -- Fedora CoreOS 44 with\nbash, jq, curl, openssl, podman, and no python3, no .venv. So on today''s\nsubstrate the jq back end is the one that runs, which is exactly why it\nhad to stop being the weaker one. TV-0076-37 runs the same payload table\nthrough both back ends and requires the verdicts to match, because \"the\nstrictness no longer depends on the node\" is a claim, and a table is a\nmeasurement.\n\nNM-1: the diagnosis podman and SPIRE produce is quoted back instead of\nbeing sent to /dev/null. Both noise sources emit exactly one line, so\nthere was never any noise to suppress -- only the sentence that says\nwhether the container is gone or the admin socket moved. The restage\ntimer runs this path every 30 minutes with nobody watching.\n\nNM-4: `Persistent=true` only has an effect on timers configured with\n`OnCalendar=` (systemd.timer(5)). The unit set it next to\n`OnUnitActiveSec=`, where it did nothing, and TV-0076-07 certified the\ncatch-up behaviour it was supposed to provide -- our own test suite\nbearing witness to a property the setting cannot have. The timer now has\n`OnCalendar=*:00/30`, which also makes the setting load-bearing, and the\nvector asserts the general rule instead of the string.\n\nNegative and mutation controls, because a check that cannot fail is the\nbug: TV-0076-30..34 (JWT-only, empty x5c, foreign root, the repo''s own\nfixture CLI driven for real, three documents a parser must refuse),\nTV-0076-38/39 (undecided is not permissive), TV-0076-40 (the foreign\ndiagnosis reaches the operator), and two mutation controls -- cut both\ncertificate guards and the JWT-only document is accepted again while the\nempty key set is still rejected by its own guard; cut only the root\nguard and the foreign root is accepted.\n\nNM-5 (the two private SELinux relabels on the bundles volume) is\ndeliberately NOT in here. Reza''s podman-doc citation turns it from a\nsuspicion into a derivation, and it belongs with the bilateral cutover,\nwhere the node can be measured. Named in the module''s limits section.\n\nNo lane count change: every vector lands in a module that is already\nrequired.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"f7427479b87f9c2201d249635a518eded7f46b84","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/trees/f7427479b87f9c2201d249635a518eded7f46b84"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/commits/396f4bf76f1bfaa2898485bfd5faf6d9a7b6c8f9","verification":{"payload":"tree f7427479b87f9c2201d249635a518eded7f46b84\nparent 9d7b2edf2611895312256ad485e67de845cb1dca\nauthor wakir-labs <xypu4r8e@addy.io> 1790153109 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790153109 +0200\n\nGive the identity substrate a root that outlives a day, and an anchor that exists (#561)\n\n* Give the identity substrate a root that outlives a day, and an anchor that exists\n\nADR-0076 steps 1-6. Step 0 (bind_address) landed with #553, step 7\n(healthcheck exec form) with #556; both verified at main before this\nbranch started.\n\nThe substrate stopped attesting on 2026-05-17 and nobody noticed for\n127 days. Two properties produced that, and each looks harmless alone:\n\n  * the agents'' `trust_bundle_path` pointed at a volume the bootstrap\n    created and never wrote. Measured on both pilot VMs 2026-09-22:\n    empty since May.\n  * the servers signed without an UpstreamAuthority, minting a fresh\n    self-signed root every `ca_ttl` and pruning the expired one. A\n    staged anchor would have been worthless within a day anyway.\n\nSo both halves, in that order.\n\nThe root (`UpstreamAuthority \"disk\"`, three federation configs). The\nserver now mints intermediates against a self-signed EC P-256 root the\nbootstrap creates into its own volume - 0600, uid 1000, read-only\nmount, not in this repository and never to be. It is created once. A\nre-run keeps it; a half-present pair aborts rather than heals, because\nrolling the root invalidates every SVID and bundle on both federation\nsides and that is a bilateral maintenance window, not a side effect of\nrunning the bootstrap again.\n\nThe anchor (`bin/wakir-spire-stage-bootstrap-anchor`, new step between\nserver start and agent start). Source is `spire-server bundle show\n-format spiffe` against the running server via podman exec - SPIRE''s\nown CLI, no local crypto. Validated before anything is written, temp\nfile in the target directory then rename, read back and stat-verified.\nEvery failure path exits 2 with a diagnosis and leaves the previous\nanchor untouched; there is no branch on which it exits 0 without a\nverified file on disk. A timer re-runs the same script every 30\nminutes, because `ca_ttl` stays at 24h by decision and a once-staged\nanchor would reproduce the May half-life.\n\n`ca_ttl` stays at 24h and the comment above it is corrected in both\nplaces it stood. It claimed we had shortened a thirty-day production\ndefault for test purposes. We had not: 24h is SPIRE''s documented\ndefault and we left it standing and wrote a rationale next to it.\n\n`rebootstrap_mode = \"auto\"` in both agent configs. The default is\n`never` and it applied, so the agent was not only without an anchor but\nforbidden to fetch one. It is inert until the NodeAttestor can\nre-attest - `join_token` cannot - and both files say so rather than\nletting the next reader believe the switch does something. That is\nwave 2 and it is Reza''s.\n\nThe two `spire-fed-bundle` CLIs are marked fixture-only in their\ndocstrings and in the README, above the fold in both. They emit JWKS\nwhose keys are not SPIRE CA keys; wired into staging they produce a\nfile at the right path, a green check, and an agent that still cannot\nattest.\n\nTests: 28 vectors, of which eight are negative controls on the stager\n(empty, whitespace, non-JSON, truncated, zero keys, keys-not-a-list,\nexec failure, and the refresh path preserving a good anchor across all\nof them) and four on the root (half-present, unparseable, mismatched\nkey, no regeneration on re-run). One mutation control cuts the\nkey-count guard out of a copy of the stager and asserts the zero-key\nvector then goes green - without it, a passing negative control could\nequally be scaffolding that rejects everything.\n\nThe module is named in the required hermetic-invariants lane rather\nthan left in the observation lane. A module whose whole point is that\nits checks can fail does not belong behind a non-blocking gate.\n\nWhat this does not do: no cutover. The root change is bilateral and\noperator-hand. And it does not establish that the agent''s SELinux\ncontext may read the staged anchor - the bundles volume carries a\nprivate relabel on both the server and the agent mount, which wants a\nmeasurement on an enforcing node and has not had one. Written into the\nstager, the README and the PR rather than left to be discovered.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Drop the persona signature the archaeology lint enforces against\n\nThe vocabulary lint flags persona names in the tree and it is right to.\nBoth new files now sign the way the neighbouring hermetic modules do.\nCaught by tests/ci/test_archaeology_lint.py on the full local run, not\nin review.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Guard the one property the acceptance window has no second signal for\n\nADR-0076 errata E3 names the stager''s unconditional write as\nload-bearing: the seven-day harness in #562 measures the re-staging\ncadence at the anchor''s mtime, and under UpstreamAuthority \"disk\" the\npublished bundle is the long-lived root, so the staged document is\nbyte-identical from one run to the next for years. Z4''s overlap half\nis constant-true under that configuration; the mtime is the only thing\nleft that can tell a running timer from a stopped one.\n\nA later \"skip the rename when nothing changed\" -- the obvious\noptimisation, and a correct-looking one -- would therefore freeze the\nmtime on a healthy substrate and turn an improvement into a red\nwindow. Measured on this branch today: three runs against a mock\nserver returning byte-identical output produce three distinct mtimes\nand three distinct inodes. The property holds; it was nowhere\nasserted.\n\nTV-0076-18 asserts it. TV-0076-19 splices exactly that short-circuit\ninto a copy of the script and requires the mtime to stand still, so\nthe vector above is shown to measure the write rather than the\nscaffolding.\n\nNo lane count change: two vectors in a module that is already required.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Measure the rewrite on any filesystem, not on the runner I happened to use\n\nMy own TV-0076-18 went red in CI on the assertion I added as a bonus:\nthe anchor''s inode must change across a re-run. It does not have to.\n`mv -f` unlinks the previous file and the next temp file is free to\nland on the recycled inode number, which is exactly what the hermetic\nlane observed (8942448 twice). Inode reuse is a property of the\nfilesystem, not of the write.\n\nThe sleep-and-compare for the mtime had the same weakness from the\nother side: it assumes sub-second timestamp granularity.\n\nBoth replaced by back-dating the anchor between runs and requiring the\nstager to move it forward again. That is what Z4 actually reads --\n\"the file was written again\" -- and it holds on any filesystem and at\nany timestamp resolution. The mutation control back-dates identically,\nso the spliced-in short-circuit still has to leave the stale stamp in\nplace.\n\nSelf-repair of a fail I put in one commit earlier; no scope beyond the\ntwo vectors from 682124f.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* The stager counted keys; the agent needs certificates\n\nZone-L cross-review, BL-1, measured against a real SPIRE-Agent v1.14.6:\na bundle holding exactly one jwt-svid key and no X.509 authority passed\nthe old `\"kty\"` count, staged green -- and the agent answered\n`no certificates found in trust bundle` in a retry loop that never ends.\nFile at the right path, right mode, right owner, stager reporting\nsuccess, substrate dead. That is the defect class this ADR was written\nagainst, produced by the one check whose entire job is to decide whether\nan anchor is an anchor.\n\nThe invariant is now the sharp one Reza recommended: at least one entry\nwith `use == \"x509-svid\"` and a non-empty `x5c`, and among those the\ncertificate of our own upstream CA root. `x5c` is base64 DER and a PEM\nfile is base64 DER between two armour lines, so the comparison is a\nstring comparison -- no openssl, no crypto, no new dependency. It closes\nthree things at once: the JWT-only anchor, a fixture JWKS from the\nspire-fed-bundle CLIs in this same directory, and the peer''s bundle\nstaged into our volume by swapped overrides. \"At least one\" and not\n\"all\", because a bilateral root cutover legitimately puts both roots in\nthe bundle at the same time.\n\nAn unreachable root is exit 3 and nothing staged -- explicitly not a\nquiet fall-back to the weaker check.\n\nNM-2, the parser. The decider is now a real JSON parser, with the\ndependency-free shape check demoted to a pre-check. python3 when it is\non PATH, otherwise jq in --slurp mode, and exit 3 when neither is. The\nslurp is not decoration: plain jq reads a *stream* of JSON values, so a\ndocument emitted twice parses cleanly, which is one of the two holes\nmeasured in review. The other was a trailing comma sailing through on\nany node without jq.\n\nA correction to