 turns it from a\nsuspicion into a derivation, and it belongs with the bilateral cutover,\nwhere the node can be measured. Named in the module''s limits section.\n\nNo lane count change: every vector lands in a module that is already\nrequired.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqs5GVCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAHPgQAFMTtubfdghFDg3YKjxPSTCY\no9YmKGHcZQWhpDDrcjZGo56PFf3UNi64hIAkdt/W7MJEWImTC4PBkw8RBSn88kX9\n9PMRkX03sqVpIqwFBLeYZcITRcBhgsG/k0VcaRRKz4wo2cTPPBWOhWOb/lfnHHu4\noMhko5eYXzk4JejYC5/X+lbCE1bhQm3ug4pQlQywT584Ffq/J7flkdAIfZqM7TKc\naSYuHaOEWcUKD6urQ1VBlKAfZrVHUTwGoErVdY5FQcgFqoDQ5CluBXb8g32jWm/T\nzql5qI1Ls9UsSa4vlFT15Ntbrrj7ToKLElRTEHm0yJ1rl9qebXCbNUEZyrQY1bqz\nmzS3toVzCpen7aBG7ZirLCRQPLL5S3+K6gXUJm45yovqGljZDzMHQonJVNroiraF\n4OgMxVUNqkXzjSheWLj8eiSP0/C68jY8N+Wx/35H+QRSkrqvQ7qlBNZkXLrLbZK4\nj0Whqwkx9N/yV+aPLdJrCFu2WrACmCpORhByXN/4IDkDR2o651CSj27mhDaTBH1e\nI7U0Fc9ys5vWVAuQz/cNul5oxqyacvsgG+ULuayERHe/rOhvsHiXDM4HIBKARelI\noiH6uGEDcUAP802rZHfZBpPauCHeE3CJkh3TLQkmrVOK2k6hWtJkw4PdWTBvALd6\ns23bZ1Y6kji80yS19owq\n=Q2Mv\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-23T08:45:10Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/396f4bf76f1bfaa2898485bfd5faf6d9a7b6c8f9","node_id":"C_kwDOSVv36NoAKDM5NmY0YmY3NmYxYmZhYTI4OTg0ODViZmQ1ZmFmNmQ5YTdiNmM4Zjk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/9d7b2edf2611895312256ad485e67de845cb1dca","sha":"9d7b2edf2611895312256ad485e67de845cb1dca","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/9d7b2edf2611895312256ad485e67de845cb1dca"}],"sha":"396f4bf76f1bfaa2898485bfd5faf6d9a7b6c8f9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/396f4bf76f1bfaa2898485bfd5faf6d9a7b6c8f9"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/281068747?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs","id":281068747,"login":"wakir-labs","node_id":"U_kgDOEMDEyw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/9d7b2edf2611895312256ad485e67de845cb1dca/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-22T11:47:19Z","email":"xypu4r8e@addy.io","name":"wakir-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-22T11:47:19Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"The acceptance lane can only measure what is already merged; give it the tree, and make it name the tree (#560)\n\n* fix(bootstrap): let step 4 fail when the fetch fails, instead of resetting to a stale ref\n\nStep 4 updated an existing checkout with three git calls in one\nsubshell, each redirected to /dev/null and none of them checked:\n\n    ( cd \"$root\"\n      git fetch  --depth 1 origin \"$branch\" >/dev/null 2>&1\n      git checkout \"$branch\"                >/dev/null 2>&1\n      git reset --hard \"origin/$branch\"     >/dev/null 2>&1\n    ) || { log_err \"git fetch/reset failed\"; return 2; }\n    log_ok \"repo updated to origin/$branch\"\n\nThe subshell returns the status of the last command only. `reset --hard\norigin/<branch>` reads a remote-tracking ref that is already on disk; it\ndoes not talk to the remote and does not care whether the fetch that was\nsupposed to refresh that ref succeeded. With no route to the forge,\nexpired credentials or a moved remote, the step reset the checkout to\nwhatever an earlier run had left behind - possibly weeks old - and\nlogged `repo updated to origin/<branch>`.\n\nSame class as the other defects found in this file today: a step that\nreports success for something that did not happen. It is worse than a\nloud failure in two directions at once. The tree is silently older than\nthe log says, and every later step, every smoke check and every\nacceptance verdict downstream is computed against it.\n\nOne call per invocation now, via a `_git_repo` helper that keeps both\nthe output and the exit status. A failed fetch stops the step and the\ncheckout is left untouched, because resetting to a remote-tracking ref\nof unknown age is worse than not updating at all - it looks like an\nupdate. git''s own diagnostic reaches the operator instead of /dev/null.\n\nBehaviour coverage, against a real git and a local bare remote made\nunreachable by renaming it - which is exactly the condition the defect\nneeds, since the stale refs/remotes/origin/<branch> stays resettable:\n\n  TV-S4F-1..4  a failed fetch returns non-zero, leaves the checkout\n               alone, does not emit the success line, and surfaces the\n               reason. All four are red against the pre-fix form.\n  TV-S4F-5     negative control: a reachable remote still updates the\n               checkout to the tip and returns 0.\n  TV-S4F-6     mutation control: with the pre-fix subshell appended to a\n               copy of the bootstrap, the scenario returns 0 and logs\n               `repo updated to origin/main` - proof the vectors measure\n               the guard and not the scaffolding.\n\nStep 4 had no behavioural coverage before this; the only mention of it\nin the tree was its name in a step-order list.\n\nThe module goes into the required hermetic-invariants lane rather than\nthe non-blocking observation lane, because it guards the step every\nlater step is computed against.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(acceptance): let the lane measure the tree on the node, and make it say which tree that was\n\nStep 4 of the bootstrap ended with `reset --hard origin/<branch>` on\nevery run. The live-VM acceptance lane calls the full bootstrap, so the\nlane could measure exactly one thing: what was already on the branch.\n\nMeasured 2026-09-22: a branch state placed on a node to verify a\nconvergence fix was reset away by step 4 before the step under test\nran. The run then exercised baseline code, logged `already active` for\nthe thing it was supposed to prove, and hung twenty minutes later on a\nservice the reverted unit had put back into a crash loop - while the\nsame log reported that service as started. No evidence in either\ndirection. The order this imposes is backwards: believe, merge, then\nmeasure.\n\nWAKIR_REPO_REF_MODE now selects what step 4 does:\n\n  track-remote  (default, unchanged) fetch and reset to the branch tip\n  pin           fetch WAKIR_REPO_REF - a branch, a tag,\n                refs/pull/<n>/head - and check out at it, detached\n  keep          leave the checkout exactly as it is\n\nThat switch is the smaller half. An acceptance report you cannot tell\napart from a canonical one is worse than the state it replaces, because\nbefore it, everyone at least knew it was always the branch. So:\n\n  * step 4 MEASURES the tree it ends up with - HEAD against the freshly\n    fetched origin/<branch>, plus worktree cleanliness - and writes a\n    provenance record. The verdict is never taken from the mode flag: a\n    pin that lands on the branch tip is canonical, a track-remote run\n    whose comparison could not be made is not. Reading it off the flag\n    would have been one more check that reports what it was told.\n  * the record carries a per-run nonce, so a record left behind by an\n    earlier run cannot be read as a statement about this one. Missing,\n    stale and unknown-schema all resolve to UNKNOWN, which is not\n    evidence: the absence of a measurement is not a passing one.\n  * both acceptance entry points print which tree the run used - in the\n    summary and on every failure path - and end a non-canonical run as\n    `NOT EVIDENCE` with exit 10 rather than `PASS`, with the seven\n    `regression-tested: clean` lines omitted, because that list is what\n    gets quoted afterwards.\n\nExit 10 is deliberately a third outcome. Collapsing \"passed, but not\nagainst the branch\" into either PASS or FAIL is the ambiguity this\nchange exists to remove.\n\nOne writer (bootstrap step 4), one reader\n(scripts/lib/repo-provenance.sh), and a vector that drives a real record\nthrough the real reader so the two cannot drift into a reader that\nsilently finds nothing and calls it missing.\n\nCoverage, hermetic - real git against a bare remote in tmp_path, no\nnetwork, no root, no substrate:\n\n  TV-PROV-1..9   step 4: track-remote lands on the tip; keep leaves an\n                 injected commit alone; pin moves to the requested ref;\n                 pin onto the tip is canonical (the verdict is measured);\n                 a dirty worktree on the tip is not; an unmeasurable tree\n                 is never canonical; the nonce round-trips; an unwritable\n                 record fails the step; four misconfigurations - including\n                 a ref that would be silently ignored - are refused up\n                 front.\n  TV-PROV-10..16 the reader: canonical is evidence, stale and missing and\n                 unknown-schema are not, reasons survive into the printed\n                 line, and every field the writer emits is consumed.\n  TV-PROV-15     control for the nonce: the same stale record loaded\n                 without one reads as canonical. That is what the nonce\n                 buys, stated as behaviour.\n  TV-PROV-17..18 lane wiring drift guards.\n  TV-PROV-19     mutation control: with the pre-fix step 4 restored,\n                 keep-mode resets the node anyway and leaves no record -\n                 the 2026-09-22 state, reproduced.\n\nFound while writing TV-PROV-6, in code added by this change: `rev-parse\n<ref>` echoes the ref back and exits non-zero when it cannot resolve it,\nso taking its stdout without its status recorded the literal string\n`origin/main` as a commit. Same class as the defect being repaired, two\nhours older. Now `rev-parse --verify --quiet`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(acceptance): refresh the reference the verdict compares against, in the run that compares\n\nZone-M review found the defect this change was written against, sitting\nin this change. `_record_repo_provenance` compared HEAD with\n`refs/remotes/origin/<branch>` - a file on the node, left there by some\nearlier run - and nothing checked whether this run had refreshed it.\n\n  track-remote  refreshed by construction (checked fetch, step aborts\n                otherwise). There the comparison is also tautological:\n                after `reset --hard origin/<branch>` the two are equal\n                by definition, and the weight is carried by the fetch\n                status check from the first commit.\n  keep          the comparison fetch failing was a log_warn and appeared\n                in no field of the record.\n  pin           not fetched at all. Only WAKIR_REPO_REF is.\n\nSo the measurement was strongest where it was redundant and weakest\nwhere it was the only thing holding.\n\nReproduced on a completely healthy network, no exotic condition: pin a\ntag pointing at yesterday''s commit - the commit this node''s tracking ref\nstill names - and the record came back\n\n    CANONICAL : yes\n    REASON    : HEAD is the origin/main tip and the worktree is clean\n    recorded tip 257952d5   real main tip bc554864\n\nThe lane would have printed PASS, `canonical main tip (ref-mode=pin)`\nand seven `regression-tested: clean` lines for a tree that is not the\nbranch tip. That is the defect this PR repairs, one level up: not \"reset\nto a stale tracking ref and call it an update\" but \"compare against a\nstale tracking ref and call it canonical\" - precisely the report\nrepo-provenance.sh says in its own header would be worse than the state\nit replaces.\n\nClosed:\n\n  * the comparison reference is refreshed in the run that compares, with\n    an explicit refspec rather than relying on git''s opportunistic\n    remote-tracking update - a measurement should not depend on a side\n    effect. track-remote passes its already-checked freshness in, so it\n    fetches once, not twice.\n  * WAKIR_PROVENANCE_REMOTE_TIP_FRESH is a recorded field and\n    canonical=yes is bound to it.\n  * a failed refresh yields `unmeasured`, not `no`. \"the tree is not the\n    branch tip\" and \"nobody compared the tree to anything\" are different\n    statements; the reader carries `unmeasured` as its own state and\n    prints NOT MEASURED. Same distinction as UNKNOWN, one layer down.\n  * schema @1 -> @2. An @1 record''s `canonical=yes` did not carry the\n    freshness guarantee that `canonical=yes` now claims, so it reads as\n    schema-mismatch rather than being re-interpreted. Treating a weaker\n    statement as the stronger one because it has the same name is the\n    defect that forced the bump.\n\nSecond finding, and the more important one: TV-PROV-6 could not see the\ncase it was named for. It called `update-ref -d refs/remotes/origin/main`\nfirst - deleting the stale ref whose *presence* is the entire condition.\nOn a real node that ref is always there. A negative control that clears\naway the state it exists to test is the class we spent the day finding,\nthis time in the control itself.\n\n  TV-PROV-6   rewritten: unreachable remote, stale ref left in place,\n              branch moved on -> `unmeasured`.\n  TV-PROV-6b  the deletion case kept as its own vector.\n  TV-PROV-20  pin to yesterday on a healthy network -> not canonical.\n  TV-PROV-4   now pins a TAG. Fetching a branch name refreshes the\n              comparison reference incidentally, so the old branch-name\n              form passed on a comparison nobody arranged.\n  TV-PROV-14b an @1 record is not re-interpreted.\n  TV-PROV-14c `unmeasured` is its own state, and prints as such.\n\nAll six are red against the reviewed head.\n\nAlso taken from the review, not blocking: step 4 writes\n`status --porcelain` and `diff --stat` into the bring-up log *before*\ndiscarding local changes, in both destructive arms. The pin arm\nintroduces no new loss class - the default arm has discarded tracked\nmodifications since the lane exists - and refusing on a dirty checkout\nwould be worse, because the lane is unattended and a node carrying an\noperator-hand change is the normal case. Silent is the part that was\nwrong, not destructive.\n\nAnd one sentence each in the test plan: \"clean worktree\" is measured at\nthe end of step 4, before step 5 runs the resolver with --apply against\nthe repo root; and what a non-keep run discards.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Fred <fabian@callandor.gmbh>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"df24760109590649fd4854fbe82f1265670e30af","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/trees/df24760109590649fd4854fbe82f1265670e30af"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/commits/9d7b2edf2611895312256ad485e67de845cb1dca","verification":{"payload":"tree df24760109590649fd4854fbe82f1265670e30af\nparent 4c24aad0b9c6cae12b0c8d7e679e33a1af79947c\nauthor wakir-labs <xypu4r8e@addy.io> 1790077639 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790077639 +0200\n\nThe acceptance lane can only measure what is already merged; give it the tree, and make it name the tree (#560)\n\n* fix(bootstrap): let step 4 fail when the fetch fails, instead of resetting to a stale ref\n\nStep 4 updated an existing checkout with three git calls in one\nsubshell, each redirected to /dev/null and none of them checked:\n\n    ( cd \"$root\"\n      git fetch  --depth 1 origin \"$branch\" >/dev/null 2>&1\n      git checkout \"$branch\"                >/dev/null 2>&1\n      git reset --hard \"origin/$branch\"     >/dev/null 2>&1\n    ) || { log_err \"git fetch/reset failed\"; return 2; }\n    log_ok \"repo updated to origin/$branch\"\n\nThe subshell returns the status of the last command only. `reset --hard\norigin/<branch>` reads a remote-tracking ref that is already on disk; it\ndoes not talk to the remote and does not care whether the fetch that was\nsupposed to refresh that ref succeeded. With no route to the forge,\nexpired credentials or a moved remote, the step reset the checkout to\nwhatever an earlier run had left behind - possibly weeks old - and\nlogged `repo updated to origin/<branch>`.\n\nSame class as the other defects found in this file today: a step that\nreports success for something that did not happen. It is worse than a\nloud failure in two directions at once. The tree is silently older than\nthe log says, and every later step, every smoke check and every\nacceptance verdict downstream is computed against it.\n\nOne call per invocation now, via a `_git_repo` helper that keeps both\nthe output and the exit status. A failed fetch stops the step and the\ncheckout is left untouched, because resetting to a remote-tracking ref\nof unknown age is worse than not updating at all - it looks like an\nupdate. git''s own diagnostic reaches the operator instead of /dev/null.\n\nBehaviour coverage, against a real git and a local bare remote made\nunreachable by renaming it - which is exactly the condition the defect\nneeds, since the stale refs/remotes/origin/<branch> stays resettable:\n\n  TV-S4F-1..4  a failed fetch returns non-zero, leaves the checkout\n               alone, does not emit the success line, and surfaces the\n               reason. All four are red against the pre-fix form.\n  TV-S4F-5     negative control: a reachable remote still updates the\n               checkout to the tip and returns 0.\n  TV-S4F-6     mutation control: with the pre-fix subshell appended to a\n               copy of the bootstrap, the scenario returns 0 and logs\n               `repo updated to origin/main` - proof the vectors measure\n               the guard and not the scaffolding.\n\nStep 4 had no behavioural coverage before this; the only mention of it\nin the tree was its name in a step-order list.\n\nThe module goes into the required hermetic-invariants lane rather than\nthe non-blocking observation lane, because it guards the step every\nlater step is computed against.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(acceptance): let the lane measure the tree on the node, and make it say which tree that was\n\nStep 4 of the bootstrap ended with `reset --hard origin/<branch>` on\nevery run. The live-VM acceptance lane calls the full bootstrap, so the\nlane could measure exactly one thing: what was already on the branch.\n\nMeasured 2026-09-22: a branch state placed on a node to verify a\nconvergence fix was reset away by step 4 before the step under test\nran. The run then exercised baseline code, logged `already active` for\nthe thing it was supposed to prove, and hung twenty minutes later on a\nservice the reverted unit had put back into a crash loop - while the\nsame log reported that service as started. No evidence in either\ndirection. The order this imposes is backwards: believe, merge, then\nmeasure.\n\nWAKIR_REPO_REF_MODE now selects what step 4 does:\n\n  track-remote  (default, unchanged) fetch and reset to the branch tip\n  pin           fetch WAKIR_REPO_REF - a branch, a tag,\n                refs/pull/<n>/head - and check out at it, detached\n  keep          leave the checkout exactly as it is\n\nThat switch is the smaller half. An acceptance report you cannot tell\napart from a canonical one is worse than the state it replaces, because\nbefore it, everyone at least knew it was always the branch. So:\n\n  * step 4 MEASURES the tree it ends up with - HEAD against the freshly\n    fetched origin/<branch>, plus worktree cleanliness - and writes a\n    provenance record. The verdict is never taken from the mode flag: a\n    pin that lands on the branch tip is canonical, a track-remote run\n    whose comparison could not be made is not. Reading it off the flag\n    would have been one more check that reports what it was told.\n  * the record carries a per-run nonce, so a record left behind by an\n    earlier run cannot be read as a statement about this one. Missing,\n    stale and unknown-schema all resolve to UNKNOWN, which is not\n    evidence: the absence of a measurement is not a passing one.\n  * both acceptance entry points print which tree the run used - in the\n    summary and on every failure path - and end a non-canonical run as\n    `NOT EVIDENCE` with exit 10 rather than `PASS`, with the seven\n    `regression-tested: clean` lines omitted, because that list is what\n    gets quoted afterwards.\n\nExit 10 is deliberately a third outcome. Collapsing \"passed, but not\nagainst the branch\" into either PASS or FAIL is the ambiguity this\nchange exists to remove.\n\nOne writer (bootstrap step 4), one reader\n(scripts/lib/repo-provenance.sh), and a vector that drives a real record\nthrough the real reader so the two cannot drift into a reader that\nsilently finds nothing and calls it missing.\n\nCoverage, hermetic - real git against a bare remote in tmp_path, no\nnetwork, no root, no substrate:\n\n  TV-PROV-1..9   step 4: track-remote lands on the tip; keep leaves an\n                 injected commit alone; pin moves to the requested ref;\n                 pin onto the tip is canonical (the verdict is measured);\n                 a dirty worktree on the tip is not; an unmeasurable tree\n                 is never canonical; the nonce round-trips; an unwritable\n                 record fails the step; four misconfigurations - including\n                 a ref that would be silently ignored - are refused up\n                 front.\n  TV-PROV-10..16 the reader: canonical is evidence, stale and missing and\n                 unknown-schema are not, reasons survive into the printed\n                 line, and every field the writer emits is consumed.\n  TV-PROV-15     control for the nonce: the same stale record loaded\n                 without one reads as canonical. That is what the nonce\n                 buys, stated as behaviour.\n  TV-PROV-17..18 lane wiring drift guards.\n  TV-PROV-19     mutation control: with the pre-fix step 4 restored,\n                 keep-mode resets the node anyway and leaves no record -\n                 the 2026-09-22 state, reproduced.\n\nFound while writing TV-PROV-6, in code added by this change: `rev-parse\n<ref>` echoes the ref back and exits non-zero when it cannot resolve it,\nso taking its stdout without its status recorded the literal string\n`origin/main` as a commit. Same class as the defect being repaired, two\nhours older. Now `rev-parse --verify --quiet`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(acceptance): refresh the reference the verdict compares against, in the run that compares\n\nZone-M review found the defect this change was written against, sitting\nin this change. `_record_repo_provenance` compared HEAD with\n`refs/remotes/origin/<branch>` - a file on the node, left there by some\nearlier run - and nothing checked whether this run had refreshed it.\n\n  track-remote  refreshed by construction (checked fetch, step aborts\n                otherwise). There the comparison is also tautological:\n                after `reset --hard origin/<branch>` the two are equal\n                by definition, and the weight is carried by the fetch\n                status check from the first commit.\n  keep          the comparison fetch failing was a log_warn and appeared\n                in no field of the record.\n  pin           not fetched at all. Only WAKIR_REPO_REF is.\n\nSo the measurement was strongest where it was redundant and weakest\nwhere it was the only thing holding.\n\nReproduced on a completely healthy network, no exotic condition: pin a\ntag pointing at yesterday''s commit - the commit this node''s tracking ref\nstill names - and the record came back\n\n    CANONICAL : yes\n    REASON    : HEAD is the origin/main tip and the worktree is clean\n    recorded tip 257952d5   real main tip bc554864\n\nThe lane would have printed PASS, `canonical main tip (ref-mode=pin)`\nand seven `regression-tested: clean` lines for a tree that is not the\nbranch tip. That is the defect this PR repairs, one level up: not \"reset\nto a stale tracking ref and call it an update\" but \"compare against a\nstale tracking ref and call it canonical\" - precisely the report\nrepo-provenance.sh says in its own header would be worse than the state\nit replaces.\n\nClosed:\n\n  * the comparison reference is refreshed in the run that compares, with\n    an explicit refspec rather than relying on git''s opportunistic\n    remote-tracking update - a measurement should not depend on a side\n    effect. track-remote passes its already-checked freshness in, so it\n    fetches once, not twice.\n  * WAKIR_PROVENANCE_REMOTE_TIP_FRESH is a recorded field and\n    canonical=yes is bound to it.\n  * a failed refresh yields `unmeasured`, not `no`. \"the tree is not the\n    branch tip\" and \"nobody compared the tree to anything\" are different\n    statements; the reader carries `unmeasured` as its own state and\n    prints NOT MEASURED. Same distinction as UNKNOWN, one layer down.\n  * schema @1 -> @2. An @1 record''s `canonical=yes` did not carry the\n    freshness guarantee that `canonical=yes` now claims, so it reads as\n    schema-mismatch rather than being re-interpreted. Treating a weaker\n    statement as the stronger one because it has the same name is the\n    defect that forced the bump.\n\nSecond finding, and the more important one: TV-PROV-6 could not see the\ncase it was named for. It called `update-ref -d refs/remotes/origin/main`\nfirst - deleting the stale ref whose *presence* is the entire condition.\nOn a real node that ref is always there. A negative control that clears\naway the state it exists to test is the class we spent the day finding,\nthis time in the control itself.\n\n  TV-PROV-6   rewritten: unreachable remote, stale ref left in place,\n              branch moved on -> `unmeasured`.\n  TV-PROV-6b  the deletion case kept as its own vector.\n  TV-PROV-20  pin to yesterday on a healthy network -> not canonical.\n  TV-PROV-4   now pins a TAG. Fetching a branch name refreshes the\n              comparison reference incidentally, so the old branch-name\n              form passed on a comparison nobody arranged.\n  TV-PROV-14b an @1 record is not re-interpreted.\n  TV-PROV-14c `unmeasured` is its own state, and prints as such.\n\nAll six are red against the reviewed head.\n\nAlso taken from the review, not blocking: step 4 writes\n`status --porcelain` and `diff --stat` into the bring-up log *before*\ndiscarding local changes, in both destructive arms. The pin arm\nintroduces no new loss class - the default arm has discarded tracked\nmodifications since the lane exists - and refusing on a dirty checkout\nwould be worse, because the lane is unattended and a node carrying an\noperator-hand change is the normal case. Silent is the part that was\nwrong, not destructive.\n\nAnd one sentence each in the test plan: \"clean worktree\" is measured at\nthe end of step 4, before step 5 runs the resolver with --apply against\nthe repo root; and what a non-keep run discards.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Fred <fabian@callandor.gmbh>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqsmrHCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAZAEQAJYFarxacQGDNwmz2WJCFwKR\nEDvxdeUalXmNgSmVC7fzaR/urFjEpFTNubd3x3egXLBn1mJf5bI/Mh1ioEcxkw4e\nq3cY3XJo5qVAxJT03uweCX7uB96+yA7MjSCGtLmWcTDtrDdhz8UJPXTnwsPakfz9\nmK/iCzxw6C0RA9HuHyiz7fVkcxPiUS0KdeEV0YQ6BfL8VF44G31aZD0ijphZrKj8\n3Nmb6h/IzyvCdrxBtVvj0YRRo6Q00lhZqtU2ndoZQxn1XaPwvNFm2ka4UbqWMVDY\nVb6QW6f4aXNo4zcvoWFkfqEZM2I0vJNtIUp7p3MCC7xZbyJe85OYg7sZ6OCVN/MR\nYsePjy/06yayb95HMc3PEFQGwfbjky2FqEjMHtx3cNUc4y9B81rXt2iopjtn4CF1\nBqVW74G/Fx8m9MXLwIiduMQJnodeD3NBX5Bvcxk3MSv2jm49YUDEfPzKy4GBlOLO\nhYscnxgXiTY+KA9x8UsonCUCPHS80UwTHYGYW9FQ1dIbBgLwVP+oSO/tZ3V3vu62\nRJLlMrK4w3/dg0U1J6hauPbh11C53ymf/TRWPI2EHIkoM+trun+633NW9SDGsbDQ\nNzctOT5hKLLzkzp4uqZnlBzB/BcTbmCAU70n8GIHTkrG38+b/8ej9ewA0ZfVzVwM\n9pMDyceiOfQCdbgatHkF\n=zuAx\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-22T11:47:20Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/9d7b2edf2611895312256ad485e67de845cb1dca","node_id":"C_kwDOSVv36NoAKDlkN2IyZWRmMjYxMTg5NTMxMjI1NmFkNDg1ZTY3ZGU4NDVjYjFkY2E","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/4c24aad0b9c6cae12b0c8d7e679e33a1af79947c","sha":"4c24aad0b9c6cae12b0c8d7e679e33a1af79947c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/4c24aad0b9c6cae12b0c8d7e679e33a1af79947c"}],"sha":"9d7b2edf2611895312256ad485e67de845cb1dca","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/9d7b2edf2611895312256ad485e67de845cb1dca"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/281068747?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs","id":281068747,"login":"wakir-labs","node_id":"U_kgDOEMDEyw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/4c24aad0b9c6cae12b0c8d7e679e33a1af79947c/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-22T11:05:59Z","email":"xypu4r8e@addy.io","name":"wakir-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-22T11:05:59Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Remove the operator''s LAN addresses, and make the rule against them run (#559)\n\n* Remove the operator''s own LAN addresses from the published tree\n\nSixty occurrences of the operator''s pilot-VM addresses stood in 21\nfiles, and another 24 RFC1918 addresses stood in eleven more. They are\nnon-routable, so this is topology disclosure rather than access: no\nincident, hygiene. It has been a standing directive since 2026-05-03\nand it has not been held since 2026-05-03.\n\nDecided per site rather than substituted mechanically:\n\n* Defaults in scripts are gone, not renamed. `WAKIR_PEER_HOST` in\n  wakir-pilot-acceptance.sh and federation-live-vm-acceptance.sh and\n  `--peer-host` in ci-live-vm-acceptance-wrapper.sh had a concrete\n  address baked in; all three now abort naming the variable and the\n  remedy. Removing a working default changes operator behaviour, so\n  the abort has to be the thing that explains it — a script that\n  silently stops working is worse than the address was. The wrapper''s\n  own header already promised it \"does NOT hard-code\" an address\n  while line 139 did exactly that.\n\n* Test fixtures need an address, not a real one. They use the RFC 5737\n  documentation ranges: 192.0.2.0/24 for hosts on the operator\n  network, 198.51.100.0/24 for the Proxmox-internal federation bridge,\n  203.0.113.0/24 for the operator-managed /etc/hosts fixture. Distinct\n  values stay distinct, which is what those fixtures assert.\n\n* The live-VM fixture in tests/live_vm/ skips when WAKIR_PEER_HOST is\n  unset instead of defaulting. Its docstring justified the old default\n  as \"intentionally an RFC1918 address so a careless live-run cannot\n  exfiltrate\", which answers a different question: non-routable\n  protects the run, not the address, and the address was public.\n\n* The seven archive reports under docs/archive/reports/live-vm/ are\n  protocols. Redacted with visible [redacted] markers and a dated\n  notice in each file saying what was removed and why. Changing a\n  record silently would have been worse than what is being fixed.\n\n* Comments and prose that only needed to say \"not reachable from the\n  sandbox\" now say that, without an address.\n\nThe history is deliberately not rewritten: a force-rewrite of a public\nrepository with 550+ pull requests is a larger operational risk than a\nnon-routable address in the log.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Make the no-internal-addresses rule something that runs\n\nThe scrub in the previous commit is a one-off. Without a gate it\nrepeats: the directive it enforces has existed since 2026-05-03, and\nsixty occurrences accumulated under it because nothing ever compared\nthe rule to the tree.\n\nThere is already a secret-scanning lane, and it did not help. It runs\ngitleaks over the full history with `useDefault = true`; default rules\nlook for credentials, and an internal IP address is not a credential.\nSo the rule existed only in prose -- the same class this repository\nfound in itself three times today, this time at a directive instead of\na test.\n\n`tooling/ci/private_address_lint.py`:\n\n* Scans the tracked working tree via `git ls-files`. The history is\n  deliberately out of scope, because rewriting a public repository\n  with 550+ pull requests is a larger operational risk than a\n  non-routable address in the log. A test pins that: an address still\n  in the log but gone from the tree is green.\n* Enforces all of RFC1918, not the one prefix this scrub found. A lint\n  that knew only 192.168.178.0/24 would be guarding the past.\n* Also catches the prose wildcard form (`192.168.178.*`,\n  `.x`, `.<peer>`). Eleven of the 85 baseline findings were written\n  that way, and it is the obvious way around a four-octet matcher\n  while still disclosing the /24.\n* Allow-list entries carry `path`, `addresses`, `reason`, `owner`,\n  `until` and `tracking`, all mandatory. `until` is compared against\n  the calendar: an expired entry turns the lane red rather than\n  quietly ceasing to apply, per ADR-0075 §3, and the addresses it\n  covered become violations again. Same semantics as\n  tooling/ci/exemption_expiry.py and the same shape as\n  tooling/compat/compat-allowlist.json, rather than a fourth pattern.\n  `addresses` is mandatory so a waiver cannot cover a whole file.\n* Ships empty, which is the intended state.\n\nIt blocks. The test module lands in `tests/ci/`, which `lane-ci-meta`\nruns wholesale, and that job is in the acceptance-gate aggregator''s\n`needs:` and `GATE_RESULTS` -- so it inherits a required context\ninstead of adding one. A new context would need a branch-protection\nchange and an exact display name, which is the PR #102\nforever-pending class. The CLI also runs as its own step so the first\nthing an engineer reads is the per-site guidance, not an assertion\ndiff.\n\nThe tests are built around negative controls, because a clean tree is\ngreen under a working scanner and under a broken one alike: an\ninjected address must go red, each enforced block is exercised\nseparately, addresses outside the set (including the RFC 5737 ranges\nused as replacements) must not fire, and an allow-listed address is\ngreen with a live date and red with an expired one. Both halves were\nmutation-checked -- disabling the scanner fails five tests, and making\nexpired entries keep applying fails exactly the one that names it.\n\nWhat it does not catch is written down in the module docstring rather\nthan left to be discovered: the history, non-RFC1918 private space\n(CGNAT, link-local, IPv6 ULA), public addresses, hostnames and\nusernames, non-literal forms, and untracked files. Gap 4 is populated\ntoday -- 32 occurrences of an operator home-directory path across 19\nfiles, including functional quadlet mounts -- and is left for a change\nthat owns the behaviour, not bundled in here.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Mira Kessler <mira@wakir.local>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"dda12be3a8b643c1741875b71ed68be868b00a9a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/trees/dda12be3a8b643c1741875b71ed68be868b00a9a"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/commits/4c24aad0b9c6cae12b0c8d7e679e33a1af79947c","verification":{"payload":"tree dda12be3a8b643c1741875b71ed68be868b00a9a\nparent c907e632ea3389cbd8bd5ebda592f7a5bb4749af\nauthor wakir-labs <xypu4r8e@addy.io> 1790075159 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790075159 +0200\n\nRemove the operator''s LAN addresses, and make the rule against them run (#559)\n\n* Remove the operator''s own LAN addresses from the published tree\n\nSixty occurrences of the operator''s pilot-VM addresses stood in 21\nfiles, and another 24 RFC1918 addresses stood in eleven more. They are\nnon-routable, so this is topology disclosure rather than access: no\nincident, hygiene. It has been a standing directive since 2026-05-03\nand it 