0c8d7e679e33a1af79947c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/4c24aad0b9c6cae12b0c8d7e679e33a1af79947c"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/281068747?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs","id":281068747,"login":"wakir-labs","node_id":"U_kgDOEMDEyw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/c907e632ea3389cbd8bd5ebda592f7a5bb4749af/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-22T10:37:24Z","email":"xypu4r8e@addy.io","name":"wakir-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-22T10:37:24Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Withdraw the two live-VM lanes that cannot produce evidence, and name the one that does (#558)\n\n* Withdraw the real-VM job: its runner label matches no runner that exists\n\nThe second job of e2e-vm-acceptance-gate.yml selected\n`[self-hosted, wakir-nested-kvm]`. Measured 2026-09-22 against the\nActions API: the repository has zero self-hosted runners, and the label\nappears nowhere else in the tree than in that one `runs-on` line. A\ndispatch would not have failed — it would have queued indefinitely.\nOn every pull request the job read `skipping` while the workflow\nreported `success` from the hermetic job beside it.\n\nThat is a gate reporting success for something that never happened.\nWithdrawn under ADR-0077, together with the three `workflow_dispatch`\ninputs that existed only to steer it.\n\nThe hermetic `harness-logic` job is untouched and still runs on every\npull request. The workflow file stays.\n\nThe tests that pinned the withdrawn job''s shape leave with it. Two\nsurvive in generalised form: the job set is now asserted to be the\nhermetic lane alone, and the PR #38 `runner.*`-at-job-level pin now\ncovers every job instead of the one that happened to carry the defect.\nA new assertion refuses a self-hosted `runs-on` anywhere in this file,\nso re-introducing one is a deliberate act with a runner behind it\nrather than an edit back into a shape that grades nothing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Withdraw live-vm-acceptance.yml: a hosted runner, a LAN target, no secrets\n\nThe workflow was the named CI path to live virtual-machine evidence.\nMeasured 2026-09-22: it has never run — zero dispatches since it was\nadded on 2026-05-16 — and two structural reasons say it could not have.\nIt executed on a hosted runner and reached its target over SSH at a\nprivate-network address that no hosted runner can route to, and it read\nthat target and its key from Actions secrets in a repository that holds\nzero secrets. The first step needing a credential would have found\nnothing to read.\n\nWithdrawn under ADR-0077 and registered in docs/ci/retired-workflows.md\nwith the change that removed it, so the historical prose in the trust\ndocuments stays legal and stays checkable.\n\ntests/infra/test_live_vm_acceptance_phase_3b.py leaves with it. Its\nsubject was the workflow''s own YAML — matrix axes, the per-cell\nshould_run filter, the aggregate verdict state machine. A test that pins\na file has to leave with the file it pins, not after it.\n\nOne consequence was not decided here, only made visible:\ntests/live_vm/test_phase_2_doppelbetrieb_live.py drops from profile\n`operator-hand` to `opt-in-marker`. In this taxonomy `operator-hand`\nmeans reachable via workflow_dispatch, and the dispatch surface it\nreferred to was this lane. What remains is a pytest marker an operator\nswitches on by hand on the node — which is what the module always\nactually was. The group''s review date, renewal author and renewal\nreason are untouched; only the profile label and the measured counts\nmove, and both are re-derived by the lane suite rather than asserted.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Pull the references through, so nothing points at a lane that is gone\n\nRemoving two lanes and leaving their mentions standing would produce\ndocumentation that cites something which does not exist — the same\nclass the withdrawal is about, in miniature. Every live reference is\neither removed or rewritten onto the operator-hand path.\n\nRewritten:\n\n- docs/architecture/layers.md — layer 5 no longer lists live bring-up\n  lanes as gates. It says there is no standing gate, names the\n  withdrawal, and calls the remaining path evidence with a date on it.\n- docs/quality-gates/test-lane-assignment.md — the standing-exception\n  table''s historical row is preserved as history and followed by what\n  has happened since: the acceptance ran by hand and failed, the\n  workflow half was withdrawn. The blanket \"all 22 have never executed\"\n  moves into the past tense, because one of them has.\n- infra/test-e2e/vm-lifecycle-harness/README.md — the CI section\n  describes the one hermetic lane and stops there. The \"future work:\n  self-hosted runner with nested KVM\" paragraph is replaced by the\n  reason that option was weighed and declined.\n- docs/operations/live-vm-acceptance-phase-3b.md — a new section 0\n  states what the document now is. Topology, inputs, aggregation and\n  runbook are rewritten from \"jobs do this\" to \"an operator does this\",\n  including shell for what the matrix job used to expand and what the\n  aggregate job used to check. The document also now says plainly that\n  neither the matrix nor the driver has ever been run.\n- docs/test-plans/phase-2-live-vm-acceptance.md — a status note ahead\n  of everything else: the \"CI-triggerable\" column means unattended-safe,\n  not \"something runs it\". Nothing does, and the cadences in that table\n  are targets nobody is meeting.\n- scripts/ci-live-vm-acceptance-wrapper.sh and\n  scripts/ci-live-vm-phase-3b-driver.sh — headers say what calls them\n  (nothing), what has ever run them (nothing), and why the `ci-` in\n  their names is a leftover rather than a claim.\n- tooling/ci/lane_inventory.py, tests/ci/test_evidence_citations.py and\n  the two script test modules — docstrings that cited the workflow as a\n  present fact now cite it as a withdrawn one, and say what that leaves\n  the test proving.\n\nKept, deliberately, with the reasoning stated rather than assumed: both\nscripts, their tests, and the phase-3b companion document. They are\noperator tooling driven over SSH from an operator host; neither needs a\nworkflow to be invoked, and the wrapper drives the very script that\nproduced the 2026-09-21/22 evidence. Withdrawing a gate that reports\nwithout measuring is this decision; deleting a capability that has never\nbeen exercised is a different one, and it deserves its own.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Make the exemption name a hand, not a file that no longer exists\n\n`live-vm-operator.execution_evidence.enabled_by` named\n`.github/workflows/live-vm-acceptance.yml` as the thing that would run\nthis lane. The file is gone, so the field would otherwise cite something\nthat does not exist — an exemption pointing at a missing file is a small\nedition of the defect the exemption is documenting.\n\nIt now names what actually enables the run: an operator opening a root\nsession on the substrate node. The `reason` field''s closing paragraph,\nwhich described both lanes as \"the subject of a separate decision\",\nrecords the outcome of that decision instead. `runs_total_via_workflow`\nkeeps its 0 and gains the date the workflow half ceased to exist, so the\nzero stays readable as a measurement rather than as a pending state.\n\nUntouched on purpose: `review_by`, `renewed_on`, `renewed_by` and\n`renewal_reason`. The date was extended on 2026-09-22 with a reason and\nan author; moving it here would make the renewal check demand a fresh\njustification, and this change is not one. It records what already\nhappened.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* STABILITY.md: say one way, and say why it is not three\n\nThe maturity matrix recorded that the live-VM lanes were not running and\nattributed it to missing dispatches. That was the charitable half of the\nexplanation and not the operative one. The runner finding was missing:\none lane targeted a private network from a hosted runner with no secrets\nto authenticate with, the other selected a self-hosted runner label that\nno runner in this repository carries. Neither was idle. Neither could\nhave run.\n\nThree rows change. The SPIFFE/SPIRE row no longer names a withdrawn\nworkflow as \"the live path\". The container-and-deployment row says what\nhappened to the real-VM leg and why its silence read as a skip beside a\npassing workflow. The live virtual-machine row is rewritten to state the\nposition after ADR-0077: one way, the operator hand on the substrate\nnode; it has been exercised, it failed there, and it is bounded by a\nreview date that the calendar is checked against rather than by a gate.\n\nThe row now reads exercised, failing, dated — which is a weaker claim\nthan the one it replaces and a true one.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Correct the one sentence that still promised a live half somewhere else\n\nThe header of the acceptance-gates workflow explained its own hermetic\nboundary by pointing at another workflow: the live-VM acceptance gate\n\"stays an operator-hand lane\". After ADR-0077 that sentence would send a\nreader to a workflow which is now hermetic in its entirety, and imply a\nlive half that no job supplies. It now says where live acceptance\nactually happens and what bounds it.\n\nComment only; no job, name or required context is touched.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Mira Kessler <mira@wakir.local>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"3c7dd4f506f2a5bb830fe52536e3e06e76d7960f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/trees/3c7dd4f506f2a5bb830fe52536e3e06e76d7960f"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/commits/c907e632ea3389cbd8bd5ebda592f7a5bb4749af","verification":{"payload":"tree 3c7dd4f506f2a5bb830fe52536e3e06e76d7960f\nparent 391d0538e05493daafc42655115a4f6b18f0fb9a\nauthor wakir-labs <xypu4r8e@addy.io> 1790073444 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790073444 +0200\n\nWithdraw the two live-VM lanes that cannot produce evidence, and name the one that does (#558)\n\n* Withdraw the real-VM job: its runner label matches no runner that exists\n\nThe second job of e2e-vm-acceptance-gate.yml selected\n`[self-hosted, wakir-nested-kvm]`. Measured 2026-09-22 against the\nActions API: the repository has zero self-hosted runners, and the label\nappears nowhere else in the tree than in that one `runs-on` line. A\ndispatch would not have failed — it would have queued indefinitely.\nOn every pull request the job read `skipping` while the workflow\nreported `success` from the hermetic job beside it.\n\nThat is a gate reporting success for something that never happened.\nWithdrawn under ADR-0077, together with the three `workflow_dispatch`\ninputs that existed only to steer it.\n\nThe hermetic `harness-logic` job is untouched and still runs on every\npull request. The workflow file stays.\n\nThe tests that pinned the withdrawn job''s shape leave with it. Two\nsurvive in generalised form: the job set is now asserted to be the\nhermetic lane alone, and the PR #38 `runner.*`-at-job-level pin now\ncovers every job instead of the one that happened to carry the defect.\nA new assertion refuses a self-hosted `runs-on` anywhere in this file,\nso re-introducing one is a deliberate act with a runner behind it\nrather than an edit back into a shape that grades nothing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Withdraw live-vm-acceptance.yml: a hosted runner, a LAN target, no secrets\n\nThe workflow was the named CI path to live virtual-machine evidence.\nMeasured 2026-09-22: it has never run — zero dispatches since it was\nadded on 2026-05-16 — and two structural reasons say it could not have.\nIt executed on a hosted runner and reached its target over SSH at a\nprivate-network address that no hosted runner can route to, and it read\nthat target and its key from Actions secrets in a repository that holds\nzero secrets. The first step needing a credential would have found\nnothing to read.\n\nWithdrawn under ADR-0077 and registered in docs/ci/retired-workflows.md\nwith the change that removed it, so the historical prose in the trust\ndocuments stays legal and stays checkable.\n\ntests/infra/test_live_vm_acceptance_phase_3b.py leaves with it. Its\nsubject was the workflow''s own YAML — matrix axes, the per-cell\nshould_run filter, the aggregate verdict state machine. A test that pins\na file has to leave with the file it pins, not after it.\n\nOne consequence was not decided here, only made visible:\ntests/live_vm/test_phase_2_doppelbetrieb_live.py drops from profile\n`operator-hand` to `opt-in-marker`. In this taxonomy `operator-hand`\nmeans reachable via workflow_dispatch, and the dispatch surface it\nreferred to was this lane. What remains is a pytest marker an operator\nswitches on by hand on the node — which is what the module always\nactually was. The group''s review date, renewal author and renewal\nreason are untouched; only the profile label and the measured counts\nmove, and both are re-derived by the lane suite rather than asserted.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Pull the references through, so nothing points at a lane that is gone\n\nRemoving two lanes and leaving their mentions standing would produce\ndocumentation that cites something which does not exist — the same\nclass the withdrawal is about, in miniature. Every live reference is\neither removed or rewritten onto the operator-hand path.\n\nRewritten:\n\n- docs/architecture/layers.md — layer 5 no longer lists live bring-up\n  lanes as gates. It says there is no standing gate, names the\n  withdrawal, and calls the remaining path evidence with a date on it.\n- docs/quality-gates/test-lane-assignment.md — the standing-exception\n  table''s historical row is preserved as history and followed by what\n  has happened since: the acceptance ran by hand and failed, the\n  workflow half was withdrawn. The blanket \"all 22 have never executed\"\n  moves into the past tense, because one of them has.\n- infra/test-e2e/vm-lifecycle-harness/README.md — the CI section\n  describes the one hermetic lane and stops there. The \"future work:\n  self-hosted runner with nested KVM\" paragraph is replaced by the\n  reason that option was weighed and declined.\n- docs/operations/live-vm-acceptance-phase-3b.md — a new section 0\n  states what the document now is. Topology, inputs, aggregation and\n  runbook are rewritten from \"jobs do this\" to \"an operator does this\",\n  including shell for what the matrix job used to expand and what the\n  aggregate job used to check. The document also now says plainly that\n  neither the matrix nor the driver has ever been run.\n- docs/test-plans/phase-2-live-vm-acceptance.md — a status note ahead\n  of everything else: the \"CI-triggerable\" column means unattended-safe,\n  not \"something runs it\". Nothing does, and the cadences in that table\n  are targets nobody is meeting.\n- scripts/ci-live-vm-acceptance-wrapper.sh and\n  scripts/ci-live-vm-phase-3b-driver.sh — headers say what calls them\n  (nothing), what has ever run them (nothing), and why the `ci-` in\n  their names is a leftover rather than a claim.\n- tooling/ci/lane_inventory.py, tests/ci/test_evidence_citations.py and\n  the two script test modules — docstrings that cited the workflow as a\n  present fact now cite it as a withdrawn one, and say what that leaves\n  the test proving.\n\nKept, deliberately, with the reasoning stated rather than assumed: both\nscripts, their tests, and the phase-3b companion document. They are\noperator tooling driven over SSH from an operator host; neither needs a\nworkflow to be invoked, and the wrapper drives the very script that\nproduced the 2026-09-21/22 evidence. Withdrawing a gate that reports\nwithout measuring is this decision; deleting a capability that has never\nbeen exercised is a different one, and it deserves its own.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Make the exemption name a hand, not a file that no longer exists\n\n`live-vm-operator.execution_evidence.enabled_by` named\n`.github/workflows/live-vm-acceptance.yml` as the thing that would run\nthis lane. The file is gone, so the field would otherwise cite something\nthat does not exist — an exemption pointing at a missing file is a small\nedition of the defect the exemption is documenting.\n\nIt now names what actually enables the run: an operator opening a root\nsession on the substrate node. The `reason` field''s closing paragraph,\nwhich described both lanes as \"the subject of a separate decision\",\nrecords the outcome of that decision instead. `runs_total_via_workflow`\nkeeps its 0 and gains the date the workflow half ceased to exist, so the\nzero stays readable as a measurement rather than as a pending state.\n\nUntouched on purpose: `review_by`, `renewed_on`, `renewed_by` and\n`renewal_reason`. The date was extended on 2026-09-22 with a reason and\nan author; moving it here would make the renewal check demand a fresh\njustification, and this change is not one. It records what already\nhappened.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* STABILITY.md: say one way, and say why it is not three\n\nThe maturity matrix recorded that the live-VM lanes were not running and\nattributed it to missing dispatches. That was the charitable half of the\nexplanation and not the operative one. The runner finding was missing:\none lane targeted a private network from a hosted runner with no secrets\nto authenticate with, the other selected a self-hosted runner label that\nno runner in this repository carries. Neither was idle. Neither could\nhave run.\n\nThree rows change. The SPIFFE/SPIRE row no longer names a withdrawn\nworkflow as \"the live path\". The container-and-deployment row says what\nhappened to the real-VM leg and why its silence read as a skip beside a\npassing workflow. The live virtual-machine row is rewritten to state the\nposition after ADR-0077: one way, the operator hand on the substrate\nnode; it has been exercised, it failed there, and it is bounded by a\nreview date that the calendar is checked against rather than by a gate.\n\nThe row now reads exercised, failing, dated — which is a weaker claim\nthan the one it replaces and a true one.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Correct the one sentence that still promised a live half somewhere else\n\nThe header of the acceptance-gates workflow explained its own hermetic\nboundary by pointing at another workflow: the live-VM acceptance gate\n\"stays an operator-hand lane\". After ADR-0077 that sentence would send a\nreader to a workflow which is now hermetic in its entirety, and imply a\nlive half that no job supplies. It now says where live acceptance\nactually happens and what bounds it.\n\nComment only; no job, name or required context is touched.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Mira Kessler <mira@wakir.local>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqslpkCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAgRsQACk33wbCWjdrhGsFeFRffrZ9\nblEvAGI3I8wpoXsX25M14xh4blXg8nr2jTtfpTIy42JqaB1Ev3jZbNWHA4+7Rk83\n2YTu5HAqmOUHb3xHWHGFWfIiwQS584br8TBCRS2gffVf809CSSbzNW27PaIwZZTk\nRvZK7zzk8Hj7zT5nbsYOeTZBYIrPOabehATc3vNeJGYWJ7zgWSZaZWOAC015MMGr\njMXEBar5962ZUMmkkKaUtQJxolCQNNUiIACYEXYp634ODcGvIMuNBQVeKtooiuDL\nyDvtXD+QhUQ7vB8WvWbv/LMH0cRt5fR78qtqhjfbPDPYbxU4bbmnD5BZmmQbVRRS\nEiIqEHW/+efD27qtDW3PXAEaTPr7BqZjexf3rmE0Fr3usgQffvJKLC7jwmKn4Mka\nNvP4vH1nkcKTsFw8uPhEjPG4ukDHgaBB+OEz+ye726gl7gdt4bc+uBB4w4fJqudF\n+7FpwtY0C90+oNpZYaxYs7Ff61VmzFQtHhuHV6/01l3mCbJ4hpSe0aVjHuQFIsUO\n0vl43WrSgLjaM1sVYkk+xpVb/zTjH5awyslc6OqYguLc7SpyNd+sYpTh9342HfjP\n5Oz+S5qGydO29chXgUndqIx5BLQUAvFdxMc/pWzMcc4DE3ASjtfULyrKqB+f5sVA\nLmduqyGvqLAnzBc8S5pl\n=6cXp\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-22T10:37:25Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/c907e632ea3389cbd8bd5ebda592f7a5bb4749af","node_id":"C_kwDOSVv36NoAKGM5MDdlNjMyZWEzMzg5Y2JkOGJkNWViZGE1OTJmN2E1YmI0NzQ5YWY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/391d0538e05493daafc42655115a4f6b18f0fb9a","sha":"391d0538e05493daafc42655115a4f6b18f0fb9a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/391d0538e05493daafc42655115a4f6b18f0fb9a"}],"sha":"c907e632ea3389cbd8bd5ebda592f7a5bb4749af","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/c907e632ea3389cbd8bd5ebda592f7a5bb4749af"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/281068747?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs","id":281068747,"login":"wakir-labs","node_id":"U_kgDOEMDEyw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/391d0538e05493daafc42655115a4f6b18f0fb9a/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-22T10:32:03Z","email":"xypu4r8e@addy.io","name":"wakir-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-22T10:32:03Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(observability): declare an absent node, and ship the unit that actually runs (#557)\n\nTwo additions from arming one node of two on the live substrate.\n\n1. A node that is knowingly not reporting is declared, not omitted.\n\n   Only one federation node is armed; the other is untouched evidence\n   for ADR-0076 and gets armed as part of its rebuild. The tempting form\n   was a shorter --expect list, and that is precisely the gap this\n   instrument exists to close: a roster silently smaller than the\n   installation is green on a silence nobody declared.\n\n   --known-absent \"label=YYYY-MM-DD=reason\" takes three mandatory\n   fields. Before the date the node is reported and does not fail; on\n   the date it turns red, because an absence without an end is a node\n   quietly dropped from the roster. A reason under 40 characters is\n   rejected as a shrug.\n\n   Three edges, all tested: a roster of only absent nodes is refused (it\n   is the empty expectation list with extra steps); a label declared\n   both expected and absent is refused (a roster that contradicts itself\n   does not get to give the convenient answer); and an absent node that\n   starts reporting is flagged loudly but does not fail, because the\n   date already forces the revisit and making good news red as well is\n   the alert fatigue this thing is supposed to avoid.\n\n2. The systemd units, and a defect the instrument caught on day one.\n\n   The first timer-driven heartbeat carried\n   unmeasurable/agent_data_volume_absent instead of the real verdict.\n   The service unit shipped with ProtectSystem=strict, which hides /var,\n   and the probe resolves the agent volume through podman. The unit was\n   wrong and it was mine.\n\n   The other half is worth stating plainly: the probe reported that it\n   could not measure rather than reporting green, the evaluator counted\n   that as red, and the lane said so. The rule earned its place on its\n   first live encounter with a real defect.\n\n   Corrected to ProtectSystem=full and committed, because a unit that\n   exists only on a node is the drift class this repository keeps\n   finding — measured the same day: zero Wakir timers installed on\n   either node, and the existing health units invoke a python that is\n   not there.\n\nMeasured end to end on real infrastructure, not in a fixture: emitter\nunder timer exit 0, heartbeat read at age 12 s, signature verified with\nzero rejections, verdict red/bundle_expired because the node genuinely\ndoes not attest. The receiver opened one issue on a real failure and\nappended a comment rather than a second issue on the next one.\n\nAnd the green path, which a red-only history leaves unproven: a freshly\nminted state file through the real probe, emitter, broker and evaluator\n— exit 0 — under a throwaway label, never under the armed node''s label,\nso no green was recorded for a node that is not. Drill state removed\nafterwards.\n\nCo-authored-by: Noa Bergstroem <noa@wakir.dev>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"c3f2f49758298d93d67622b7678e0b2028e270d6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/trees/c3f2f49758298d93d67622b7678e0b2028e270d6"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/commits/391d0538e05493daafc42655115a4f6b18f0fb9a","verification":{"payload":"tree c3f2f49758298d93d67622b7678e0b2028e270d6\nparent 69f6cd253c30f990dd289d091c5a242d8616a794\nauthor wakir-labs <xypu4r8e@addy.io> 1790073123 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790073123 +0200\n\nfeat(observability): declare an absent node, and ship the unit that actually runs (#557)\n\nTwo additions from arming one node of two on the live substrate.\n\n1. A node that is knowingly not reporting is declared, not omitted.\n\n   Only one federation node is armed; the other is untouched evidence\n   for ADR-0076 and gets armed as part of its rebuild. The tempting form\n   was a shorter --expect list, and that is precisely the gap this\n   instrument exists to close: a roster silently smaller than the\n   installation is green on a silence nobody declared.\n\n   --known-absent \"label=YYYY-MM-DD=reason\" takes three mandatory\n   fields. Before the date the node is reported and does not fail; on\n   the date it turns red, because an absence without an end is a node\n   quietly dropped from the roster. A reason under 40 characters is\n   rejected as a shrug.\n\n   Three edges, all tested: a roster of only absent nodes is refused (it\n   is the empty expectation list with extra steps); a label declared\n   both expected and absent is refused (a roster that contradicts itself\n   does not get to give the convenient answer); and an absent node that\n   starts reporting is flagged loudly but does not fail, because the\n   date already forces the revisit and making good news red as well is\n   the alert fatigue this thing is supposed to avoid.\n\n2. The systemd units, and a defect the instrument caught on day one.\n\n   The first timer-driven heartbeat carried\n   unmeasurable/agent_data_volume_absent instead of the real verdict.\n   The service unit shipped with ProtectSystem=strict, which hides /var,\n   and the probe resolves the agent volume through podman. The unit was\n   wrong and it was mine.\n\n   The other half is worth stating plainly: the probe reported that it\n   could not measure rather than reporting green, the evaluator counted\n   that as red, and the lane said so. The rule earned its place on its\n   first live encounter with a real defect.\n\n   Corrected to ProtectSystem=full and committed, because a unit that\n   exists only on a node is the drift class this repository keeps\n   finding — measured the same day: zero Wakir timers installed on\n   either node, and the existing health units invoke a python that is\n   not there.\n\nMeasured end to end on real infrastructure, not in a fixture: emitter\nunder timer exit 0, heartbeat read at age 12 s, signature verified with\nzero rejections, verdict red/bundle_expired because the node genuinely\ndoes not attest. The receiver opened one issue on a real failure and\nappended a comment rather than a second issue on the next one.\n\nAnd the green path, which a red-only history leaves unproven: a freshly\nminted state file through the real probe, emitter, broker and evaluator\n— exit 0 — under a throwaway label, never under the armed node''s label,\nso no green was recorded for a node that is not. Drill state removed\nafterwards.\n\nCo-authored-by: Noa Bergstroem <noa@wakir.dev>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqslkjCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAANTwQAJhgKld4jTSj/Dq9JozveT+6\n2dmztKEvCWeE3ZosHOegSGnpCPNEirzo7UGaC9Bm80pCUS0FUiS+TzqC+Q+72obS\n5ooEuNv/8RpIfqSiF9MBRVtiVc/JPq/cj6Jzl2HhXYZYlh93Cgb1iVphU7tE7j6M\nepodvlAyb86oG+cOtCh9NQGdJrziRuKFYK0goOtwjjUuGuPKR7pyJBmW8qNYVSGg\nGlxBv8BMTtk6dFeMpa+AVYxuLCKh+i318KE64zMjP/1oR49HCCZm+uKt2Gz6OjH9\nLOMn3GWYNOSRYq4hyqYjzGH7dq3HJIlLT/jZl0v2TmkyIwYSFxyjXDgph15nYK/o\n8LlRpiKaBMXJoqv+1IQFoNt+19AOloBMm5vmF0HcghmcY2wN6I2gDwHDdpuWiX4F\nyxgjTSVsvaHFc2+qvnx+G7mS/SVnX+JkogmlS+oWun9KK12PcGLdcYDkFf4dN236\nvak2caYniFIztAV76f7vzoa+oYuH03Vf23DhUn/LxqHvq5/Kii+fx/NYDToaKIIv\nqsHETWRbfCRMrtVYUb//QMXHrBurfVf/oHuYEp1wx/wMc612SdWPQtVdghyLgBW3\nqNW+F8h6eCUitC+qxbxl5TgG4cYY1PlKrszL8mhZrmFEzgsxer3WMm1EEcIulxyq\negZZo+7Q8IfYJJP8Qkb/\n=h6jN\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-22T10:32:03Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/391d0538e05493daafc42655115a4f6b18f0fb9a","node_id":"C_kwDOSVv36NoAKDM5MWQwNTM4ZTA1NDkzZGFhZmM0MjY1NTExNWE0ZjZiMThmMGZiOWE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/69f6cd253c30f990dd289d091c5a242d8616a794","sha":"69f6cd253c30f990dd289d091c5a242d8616a794","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/69f6cd253c30f990dd289d091c5a242d8616a794"}],"sha":"391d0538e05493daafc42655115a4f6b18f0fb9a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/391d0538e05493daafc42655115a4f6b18f0fb9a"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/281068747?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs","id":281068747,"login":"wakir-labs","node_id":"U_kgDOEMDEyw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/69f6cd253c30f990dd289d091c5a242d8616a794/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-22T10:31:10Z","email":"xypu4r8e@addy.io","name":"wakir-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-22T10:31:10Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Four healthchecks have been publishing a verdict they never computed; the missing measurement is now made (#556)\n\n* fix(quadlet): stop four healthchecks from publishing a verdict they never computed\n\nMeasured on the live node 2026-09-22 08:32 UTC, and independently on\nboth nodes:\n\n    .Config.Healthcheck.Test = [\"CMD-SHELL\", \".../spire-server healthcheck\"]\n    podman exec <server> /bin/sh -c ''echo ok''\n      -> crun: executable file `/bin/sh` not found\n    podman exec <server> /opt/spire/bin/spire-server healthcheck\n      -> Server is healthy.\n    .State.Health.Log -> ExitCode 1, Output \"\", every 10 s, since May\n\nPodman wraps a *string* HealthCmd in /bin/sh -c. The SPIRE images have\nno shell. So the probe never started, failed with empty output every\nten seconds for four months, and the container published `unhealthy` on\nthe strength of it — while the same command without the wrapper reported\nthe server healthy. The label was not uninformative, it was the inverse\nof the truth.\n\nThe semantics are now measured rather than cited. On the target podman\n(5.8.1, on the live node, 2026-09-22 10:05 UTC), with a throwaway\ncontainer created, inspected and removed, leaving the container list\nbyte-identical to before:\n\n    --health-cmd \"/opt/spire/bin/spire-server healthcheck\"\n      -> [\"CMD-SHELL\",\"/opt/spire/bin/spire-server healthcheck\"]\n    --health-cmd ''[\"/opt/spire/bin/spire-server\", \"healthcheck\"]''\n      -> [\"CMD\",\"/opt/spire/bin/spire-server\",\"healthcheck\"]\n\nand Quadlet passes the array through verbatim (quadlet -dryrun, podman\n5.8.4). That was the one link that was documented but unexecuted last\ntime, which is why the fix was described and not built then.\n\nFour quadlets target images measured to have no /bin/sh\n(ghcr.io/spiffe/spire-server, ghcr.io/spiffe/spire-agent) and are\nconverted. A fifth, wakir-persona-tomas, is converted too — but it was\nNOT broken: its image was measured to have a shell. It is converted\nbecause a plain command gains nothing from a shell and loses the ability\nto run in an image that later drops one. Stated separately so this does\nnot read as five bug fixes.\n\nwakir-nats keeps its string form and earns it: its command uses || and a\nredirect, so it genuinely needs sh, and its image has one.\n\nThe test enforces a rule rather than a list: a HealthCmd that uses no\nshell feature must be in exec form. A list of blessed files would have\nto be maintained by the same attention that missed this for four months.\nnats therefore passes on its merits, not by exemption. Verified against\na deliberately reintroduced regression: putting one line back into\nstring form turns two tests red.\n\nNot checked, and named so it is not mistaken for covered: that the\nbinary in the exec form exists in the image. That is an image-content\nquestion for a lane that pulls images.\n\nTouches container quadlets, which is Kai''s substrate — flagged for\nZone H rather than assumed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(quadlet): two assertions were pinning the defect, not the contract\n\nThe observation lane went red on the previous commit, and it was right\nto: two tests asserted the broken spelling literally.\n\n    infra/spire/agent/tests/...:  re.search(r\"HealthCmd=/opt/spire/bin/spire-agent\\s+healthcheck\")\n    infra/spire/federation/tests/...: \"HealthCmd=/opt/spire/bin/spire-server healthcheck\" in text\n\nThat is the string form — the one podman wraps in /bin/sh -c, in an\nimage measured to have no shell. Both tests have been green since May\nwhile the thing they guard has been returning ExitCode 1 with empty\noutput every ten seconds, and the container has been publishing\n`unhealthy` on the strength of it.\n\nSo they were not merely out of date. They were holding the defect in\nplace: a change to the working form would have turned them red, and did.\n\nRewritten to assert the property rather than a spelling — exec form,\nright binary, right subcommand — so they cannot pin a broken spelling\nagain. The literal-substring style is what made this possible, and it is\nwhat is removed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Noa Bergstroem <noa@wakir.dev>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"f913e8c93c8fb8048b8faaaeb8c38189fc4ba015","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/trees/f913e8c93c8fb8048b8faaaeb8c38189fc4ba015"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/commits/69f6cd253c30f990dd289d091c5a242d8616a794","verification":{"payload":"tree f913e8c93c8fb8048b8faaaeb8c38189fc4ba015\nparent 0844eb950b974b8e7540575cf8f3b1649e97c719\nauthor wakir-labs <xypu4r8e@addy.io> 1790073070 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790073070 +0200\n\nFour healthchecks have been publishing a verdict they never computed; the missing measurement is now made (#556)\n\n* fix(quadlet): stop four healthchecks from publishing a verdict they never computed\n\nMeasured on the live node 2026-09-22 08:32 UTC, and independently on\nboth nodes:\n\n    .Config.Healthcheck.Test = [\"CMD-SHELL\", \".../spire-server healthcheck\"]\n    podman exec <server> /bin/sh -c ''echo ok''\n      -> crun: executable file `/bin/sh` not found\n    podman exec <server> /opt/spire/bin/spire-server healthcheck\n      -> Server is healthy.\n    .State.Health.Log -> ExitCode 1, Output \"\", every 10 s, since May\n\nPodman wraps a *string* HealthCmd in /bin/sh -c. The SPIRE images have\nno shell. So the probe never started, failed with empty output every\nten seconds for four months, and the container published `unhealthy` on\nthe strength of it — while the same command without the wrapper reported\nthe server healthy. The label was not uninformative, it was the inverse\nof the truth.\n\nThe semantics are now measured rather than cited. On the target podman\n(5.8.1, on the live node, 2026-09-22 10:05 UTC), with a throwaway\ncontainer created, inspected and removed, leaving the container list\nbyte-identical to before:\n\n    --health-cmd \"/opt/spire/bin/spire-server healthcheck\"\n      -> [\"CMD-SHELL\",\"/opt/spire/bin/spire-server healthcheck\"]\n    --health-cmd ''[\"/opt/spire/bin/spire-server\", \"healthcheck\"]''\n      -> [\"CMD\",\"/opt/spire/bin/spire-server\",\"healthcheck\"]\n\nand Quadlet passes the array through verbatim (quadlet -dryrun, podman\n5.8.4). That was the one link that was documented but unexecuted last\ntime, which is why the fix was described and not built then.\n\nFour quadlets target images measured to have no /bin/sh\n(ghcr.io/spiffe/spire-server, ghcr.io/spiffe/spire-agent) and are\nconverted. A fifth, wakir-persona-tomas, is converted too — but it was\nNOT broken: its image was measured to have a shell. It is converted\nbecause a plain command gains nothing from a shell and loses the ability\nto run in an image that later drops one. Stated separately so this does\nnot read as five bug fixes.\n\nwakir-nats keeps its string form and earns it: its command uses || and a\nredirect, so it genuinely needs sh, and its image has one.\n\nThe test enforces a rule rather than a list: a HealthCmd that uses no\nshell feature must be in exec form. A list of blessed files would have\nto be maintained by the same attention that missed this for four months.\nnats therefore passes on its merits, not by exemption. Verified against\na deliberately reintroduced regression: putting one line back into\nstring form turns two tests red.\n\nNot checked, and named so it is not mistaken for covered: that the\nbinary in the exec form exists in the image. That is an image-content\nquestion for a lane that pulls images.\n\nTouches container quadlets, which is Kai''s substrate — flagged for\nZone H rather than assumed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(quadlet): two assertions were pinning the defect, not the contract\n\nThe observation lane went red on the previous commit, and it was right\nto: two tests asserted the broken spelling literally.\n\n    infra/spire/agent/tests/...:  re.search(r\"HealthCmd=/opt/spire/bin/spire-agent\\s+healthcheck\")\n    infra/spire/federation/tests/...: \"HealthCmd=/opt/spire/bin/spire-server healthcheck\" in text\n\nThat is the string form — the one podman wraps in /bin/sh -c, in an\nimage measured to have no shell. Both tests have been green since May\nwhile the thing they guard has been returning ExitCode 1 with empty\noutput every ten seconds, and the container has been publishing\n`unhealthy` on the strength of it.\n\nSo they were not merely out of date. They were holding the defect in\nplace: a change to the working form would have turned them red, and did.\n\nRewritten to assert the property rather than a spelling — exec form,\nright binary, right subcommand — so they cannot pin a broken spelling\nagain. The literal-substring style is what made this possible, and it is\nwhat is removed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Noa Bergstroem <noa@wakir.dev>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqsljuCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAiosQAGt1Q+rvvrmGvVRyQ53cncVW\nT7yFuWEpabNuFyPEvIuKqEd8mSqjZ9WExysJE2BsM7BmGVXVEZ/UkM1P+qLabgbC\nybRiXufS+zKc++CXoBA2vQT6addWoMuS0DUMgakwq8U0AeidJujMUpK1Pj69Ncvb\nQRTui2GOj5yI21ckg1HskHjtsh4RiyXkjeiRK11v9clK6vhDNSIu7lk6a0QyOKLQ\nj+4uxebyq6567v2lc57g2N9OGB1b7nLMlD1X5NiduIqeuMcjEErZeaK8VUYTukSC\nxxMLYi3ujI9AeCvpsTmUVk8Jwt8d1Sc6BDQcMqEDg/zSzMxiwTwPyQ53GR9M5eVp\nw4aBn5uM5khpE3R30vcnt77gG5MNit/mTHawMJHZz2s1AMATUEgdc+CDYB7IgkAh\nqzU6Wj7p0kC80WbbfOni3aea+TkeH1nx1dNNPIDDPUdCPbUya1MAN9A4jdmsrVGk\nIOgfcPeQT5WbRsNly4W5pxYCMXGua6SovzN75+ZAQu0ph7ZM9d3qV7FigM/I4JYR\nXtO41f+yU5KArJkb7ToXFxZ4CQhquaBJxZkMEXRZiNKf7wkC2UH+Ipg7wEgidvF+\nZzoimYI30AHINsF2Bzxj68+zf4vEBOKb6EkZq2kO//Ril5e2IV3L/UQbXSM+Pbhn\nRKO7V8wQG3FlQ8RvmKFE\n=W+BZ\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-22T10:31:10Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/69f6cd253c30f990dd289d091c5a242d8616a794","node_id":"C_kwDOSVv36NoAKDY5ZjZjZDI1M2MzMGY5OTBkZDI4OWQwOTFjNWEyNDJkODYxNmE3OTQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/0844eb950b974b8e7540575cf8f3b1649e97c719","sha":"0844eb950b974b8e7540575cf8f3b1649e97c719","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/0844eb950b974b8e7540575cf8f3b1649e97c719"}],"sha":"69f6cd253c30f990dd289d091c5a242d8616a794","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/69f6cd253c30f990dd289d091c5a242d8616a794"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/281068747?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs","id":281068747,"login":"wakir-labs","node_id":"U_kgDOEMDEyw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/0844eb950b974b8e7540575cf8f3b1649e97c719/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-22T09:36:49Z","email":"xypu4r8e@addy.io","name":"wakir-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-22T09:36:49Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Name the author of the renewal, since the commit metadata names the wrong one (#555)\n\nADR-0075 §3 trades a silent lapse for a visible decision that has an\nauthor. The renewal in #552 has one, but the only place it was recorded\nwas the git author of the commit, and that field is wrong: the working\ncopy it was written in carried a different local git identity, so blame\non this file points at the wrong hand.\n\nAn author recorded only in metadata that is wrong is not an author. The\nfield states the deciding role in the document the mechanism actually\nreads, where moving a working copy cannot change it. The decision record\nitself lives outside this repository, and the field says so rather than\nimporting internal names the archaeology lint rightly keeps out - the\nfirst draft of this change named them and the lint refused it, which is\nthe gate doing its job on its own author.\n\nNo date moved, so the renewal gate has nothing to compare; the expiry\ngate, the lane suite and the archaeology lint are green.\n\nCo-authored-by: Mira Kessler <mira@wakir.local>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"b24b720485eaeed30e20ed4a9cf4c6f9f8647922","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/trees/b24b720485eaeed30e20ed4a9cf4c6f9f8647922"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/commits/0844eb950b974b8e7540575cf8f3b1649e97c719","verification":{"payload":"tree b24b720485eaeed30e20ed4a9cf4c6f9f8647922\nparent 482bd41982042d45d99b19bd87db2a47f93e97f6\nauthor wakir-labs <xypu4r8e@addy.io> 1790069809 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790069809 +0200\n\nName the author of the renewal, since the commit metadata names the wrong one (#555)\n\nADR-0075 §3 trades a silent lapse for a visible decision that has an\nauthor. The renewal in #552 has one, but the only place it was recorded\nwas the git author of the commit, and that field is wrong: the working\ncopy it was written in carried a different local git identity, so blame\non this file points at the wrong hand.\n\nAn author recorded only in metadata that is wrong is not an author. The\nfield states the deciding role in the document the mechanism actually\nreads, where moving a working copy cannot change it. The decision record\nitself lives outside this repository, and the field says so rather than\nimporting internal names the archaeology lint rightly keeps out - the\nfirst draft of this change named them and the lint refused it, which is\nthe gate doing its job on its own author.\n\nNo date moved, so the renewal gate has nothing to compare; the expiry\ngate, the lane suite and the archaeology lint are green.\n\nCo-authored-by: Mira Kessler <mira@wakir.local>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqskwxCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA7CMQABkthTtRCiYPndgDh8HgZ/YE\ntYcRtnw1VM4YmhEP56QmVh89tdOMplrw+Q5sE5ezIZ4KhzJAQR/cSJuYeoKXD4tG\nYiqHigPgSkuX3lqyqPhMCkkm765QfwMKdvgv4M9iUqvd5SDHdn1whTeQAh7yPXEA\nERiL6IdszTWqF1yE4ceyfkGL7YK4SMLAwek825XfttrzKNcksDwuWtcI3q2hwk6r\nW7EOgqO8kWw9Yyb20jn4mrckJssiGwbjEko6Q/Tg/gsEX2WFMGyibcJAOhq6lUif\nYfu7Y6aA6XozUwW3ibk9Tjx4kUOiz9fwhGwnIe95eseErSxu6SO3LU4X+0O8K4qU\nnY6KSWY7mZdVfIFNoL/OcxlcgzdPrN9sZfRXaPoYcefkAyk+UFbeqK82S35K2URp\ntl0cl7ypxZJitBxT/WTmqsyR0Vo4n9VNhgNIDMHuw+nTysuMorHUN4On869sEkA2\n3htj3nnkHfVo40D7A/UPZv9DCnMLIDpIcXVqKXXB7AaAx2228C5XEtw6gm6ifo4u\nzn0dUFSP5hkoD69OukbDv0E+TyQ4ujjuRwF3aVAf4d6jEv3N+VooMvmVdq/rkfTR\nmtiBItMcoCCEBApw21eyV6F7r+tW2BPZYh1mNk7GniiKjVGDX4Xebky6BerV/UaY\n/koUFaTQhisepsHgc2++\n=fn+v\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-22T09:36:50Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/0844eb950b974b8e7540575cf8f3b1649e97c719","node_id":"C_kwDOSVv36NoAKDA4NDRlYjk1MGI5NzRiOGU3NTQwNTc1Y2Y4ZjNiMTY0OWU5N2M3MTk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/482bd41982042d45d99b19bd87db2a47f93e97f6","sha":"482bd41982042d45d99b19bd87db2a47f93e97f6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/482bd41982042d45d99b19bd87db2a47f93e97f6"}],"sha":"0844eb950b974b8e7540575cf8f3b1649e97c719","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/0844eb950b974b8e7540575cf8f3b1649e97c719"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/281068747?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs","id":281068747,"login":"wakir-labs","node_id":"U_kgDOEMDEyw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/482bd41982042d45d99b19bd87db2a47f93e97f6/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-22T09:31:08Z","email":"xypu4r8e@addy.io","name":"wakir-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-22T09:31:08Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Make step 6 converge: apply the unit it installs, instead of installing it and hoping (#553)\n\n* Apply what step 6 installs, instead of installing it and hoping\n\nMeasured on the live pilot VM, 2026-09-21/22 (Operator-Hand):\n\n  unit file written    2026-09-21 20:11:44\n  container running    since 2026-09-20 19:59:46\n  unit says            PublishPort=0.0.0.0:8443:8443\n  actual listener      127.0.0.1:8443\n  bootstrap log        OK  ...-server-federation-wakir.service already\n                           active\n\nStep 6 rendered the unit, wrote it, ran daemon-reload, saw the service\nactive and skipped the start. The container had been created two runs\nearlier from an older unit. A systemctl restart made the installed\ndirective live in under a second -- and, as a side effect, dissolved\nthe federation asymmetry that was filed in May as \"Bug-39, asymmetric\ntopology by design\". It was not a design. It was a unit that had never\nbeen applied.\n\nGeneralised: any Quadlet change -- image pin, mount, env, resource\nlimit, PublishPort -- could be installed, logged OK and never applied.\nThe bootstrap was idempotent, not convergent.\n\nWhat changes\n\nTwo independent change-signals per service unit, both feeding one\ndecision in step 6h/6j:\n\n1. In-run signal. Every step-6 install path now goes through\n   _install_if_changed. Where the cmp -s guard fails and install runs,\n   the owning unit is marked (_note_config_change -> _mark_unit_changed).\n   Precise, state-free, blind to earlier runs.\n2. Applied-state fingerprint. After a successful start/restart,\n   _record_unit_applied writes the SHA-256 of every file that defines\n   the unit -- .container, the bind-mounted config, the referenced\n   .volume/.network units -- to /var/lib/wakir/bootstrap/applied/.\n   The next run compares. This is the signal that catches the\n   2026-09-21 case, where the unit came from run N-1 and the container\n   from run N-2.\n\nContent hash rather than mtime: mtime says nothing about content (an\noperator touch, a re-render producing identical bytes), and a container\nthat systemd restarted on its own after the file changed but before the\ndaemon-reload would look newer than a unit it never applied.\n\n_converge_service_unit then starts a stopped unit, restarts a running\nunit whose installed definition is not the one it was created from, and\nleaves an in-sync unit alone. Order stays server -> agent: the agent\npulls its join-token from a running server. The Bug-25 wait points\n(is-active, agent Workload-API socket) now run on the restart path too\n-- a restart re-opens the same race as a cold start.\n\nStated cost: on the first run after this lands, no unit has a record,\nso every running unit counts as \"convergence state unknown\" and is\nrestarted once. On a substrate where an unapplied unit may have sat\nthere for months, \"assume it is fine\" is the assumption that produced\nthis bug.\n\nJoin-token round-trip (necessary, not incidental)\n\nStep 6i seds the issued join-token into the installed agent unit, so\nthe installed file could never equal a freshly rendered template (which\ncarries WAKIR_JOIN_TOKEN_PLACEHOLDER). Under \"start only if stopped\"\nthat was invisible: every re-run rewrote the unit back to the\nplaceholder and the placeholder version was never applied -- until the\nnext VM reboot, where systemd would have started the agent with\n-joinToken WAKIR_JOIN_TOKEN_PLACEHOLDER (the Bug-37 crash-loop, armed\nand waiting). With convergence the same rewrite would restart the agent\ninto that unit on every run. _carried_join_token carries the live token\ninto the render, so a re-run produces a byte-identical file.\n\nTests\n\ntests/infra/test_pilot_bootstrap_step6_convergence.py drives behaviour,\nnot source text: it sources the bootstrap (main-gate), points the path\noverrides at a tmp tree and injects a recording systemctl.\n\n  TV-CONV-1   changed definition + running service -> restart. Red when\n              the restart is skipped.\n  TV-CONV-2   negative control: unchanged -> no start, no restart.\n  TV-CONV-4   no applied-state record -> one convergence pass, then\n              quiet.\n  TV-CONV-5   changed bind-mounted config -> restart (the ca_ttl class).\n  TV-CONV-8   the Bug-25 waits run on the restart path.\n  TV-CONV-9   mutation control: roll the drift check back to \"skip when\n              active\" and TV-CONV-1''s assertion goes red.\n\nThree existing source-discipline guards are re-pointed at the new call\nsite, with the invariant they protect stated in the docstring:\nBug-22''s server pre-start sweep, Bug-25''s step-6h wait, and\nTV-BRINGUP-04, which now additionally requires that the is-active guard\nreconsider a unit whose definition changed. test_pilot_bootstrap''s\nidempotency-marker list keeps its entry via the replacement line.\n\n\"OK <unit> already active\" is gone. The line that replaces it says\nwhether the running configuration matches the installed one.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Bind the federation gRPC API where the agent can reach it\n\nMeasured 2026-09-22, both pilot VMs: the SPIRE-Agent fails with\n``connection refused`` on 8081. Not ``x509: certificate signed by\nunknown authority`` -- that was the symptom on 2026-09-21, from a\ncontainer still running an older, reachable configuration.\n\nAll three federation server configs declared:\n\n    bind_address = \"127.0.0.1\"\n\nThat is the loopback of the SERVER CONTAINER''s network namespace. The\nSPIRE-Agent runs in its own container and dials the server over the\n``wakir-federation`` bridge by NetworkAlias, so it never reached that\nsocket. Unchanged since the configs landed -- the federation path never\nworked for an agent in its own container. ``spire-server-pilot-single-\norg.conf`` has bound 0.0.0.0 since the 2026-05-14 bring-up, for exactly\nthis reason, which is why the single-org path did work.\n\nThe comment above the line said the bind kept the gRPC API \"loopback-\nonly inside the container\", presenting a containment intent that its\nmechanism does not produce. The rewritten comment says what is true:\nthe agent container must reach this port over the container network,\nand what keeps the gRPC control-plane off the network is the Quadlet,\nwhich publishes 8081 to the HOST loopback only\n(``PublishPort=127.0.0.1:<HOST_GRPC_PORT>:8081``). No PublishPort is\nadded for 8081; host exposure stays exactly where it was.\n\n``spire-server-partner.conf`` carries the same line and the same\ntopology (hermetic same-host pair, agent in its own container), so it\nis pulled along rather than left as a second latent copy.\n\nT-FED-HCL-06 pins both halves: bind_address must be 0.0.0.0 on every\nfederation server config, and the server Quadlet must not publish 8081\nbeyond host loopback. One without the other is either an unreachable\nserver or an exposed control-plane.\n\nSeparate from the step-6 convergence commit on purpose: two defects,\ntwo commits, independently reviewable. They do interact -- once step 6\nconverges, it replaces the accidentally-working container on the pilot\nVM with the installed, correct, unreachable-until-this-commit\nconfiguration.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Refuse to apply an agent unit that still holds the token placeholder\n\nTwo halves of the same defect, both of which only become dangerous\nonce the bootstrap actually applies what it installs.\n\nStep 6d re-renders the agent unit from the template, which carries\n``-joinToken WAKIR_JOIN_TOKEN_PLACEHOLDER``. Step 6i replaced the\nplaceholder only when the agent was NOT yet attested. So on every\nre-run against an attested agent the installed unit went back to the\nplaceholder and stayed there -- invisible, because nothing ever\nrestarted the agent. Armed, though: the next VM reboot starts the agent\nfrom that unit, with the literal string as its join-token, and it\ncrash-loops (Bug-37).\n\n  * Step 6i now keys on the placeholder being present, not on the\n    attestation state. A token that goes unused costs one datastore row\n    and expires in an hour; a placeholder in an applied unit costs the\n    agent.\n  * Step 6h/6j refuse to start or restart an agent unit that still\n    carries the placeholder, with the file and the grep in the\n    diagnostic. Failing loudly beats applying a unit known to be\n    incoherent.\n\nTV-CONV-13 drives the refusal (exit 2, no systemctl start, no restart)\nand pins the 6i condition.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Make the NATS unit runnable: it ran as root against a 1000-owned store\n\nFound by the first converged step-6 run on the pilot VM, 2026-09-22\n08:34 UTC. NATS had been up 37 hours, healthy. The converged run\napplied the installed unit, and NATS died:\n\n    [FTL] Can''t start JetStream: storage directory is not writable\n\nDiagnosis, measured on the box:\n\n  * nats:2.11-alpine declares no USER, and the Quadlet declared no\n    User=, so the container runs as root.\n  * DropCapability=ALL takes CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE away from that root.\n  * The JetStream volume is owned 1000:1000, mode 755 -- the bootstrap\n    chowns it there in _pre_start_chown_sweep (kind=nats).\n  * Probe with the unit''s own settings: uid=0, touch\n    /data/jetstream/.probe -> Permission denied.\n\nSo the installed unit could not start at all. It survived because\nnothing had restarted it since the chown -- the same \"installed is not\napplied\" pattern this PR exists for, third instance, this time a unit\nthat was dead on arrival and looked healthy for four months.\n\nThe comment in _pre_start_chown_sweep claimed \"NATS runs as uid:1000\ntoo (compose parity)\". It does not: compose/nats.yaml sets no user\neither, and its volume is root-owned, which is why the compose track\nworks. The chown belongs to the Quadlet track alone. Comment corrected\nto say that.\n\nFix: the Quadlet now pins User=1000/Group=1000 -- the side the SPIRE\nunits already stand on and the side the on-disk JetStream data is\nalready owned by -- and the volume line carries :U so podman chowns the\nnamed volume to the container user at start (the Bug-26 discipline that\ntests/infra/test_quadlet_selinux_relabel.py enforces repo-wide, and\nwhich the NATS unit was exempt from only because it had no User=).\n\nRunning NATS as non-root is also the better posture: root with all\ncapabilities dropped is a root that cannot do its job.\n\ntest_quadlet_nats.py''s compose-parity vector is pulled along, with the\nreason the two tracks differ on this line spelled out.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Give the new convergence module a lane and refresh the counts\n\ntests/lanes/test_lane_assignment.py turns red on any test module without\na lane entry -- \"which gate runs this?\" is part of adding a test. The\nconvergence module joins the infra-substrate group on the optional-ci\nprofile, alongside the other pilot-bootstrap modules, and the measured\ncounts move 419 -> 420 modules / 100 -> 101 optional-ci.\n\nSelf-repair of my own PR: the production-suite failure and the\ndownstream drift-envelope failure both trace back to this one missing\nentry.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Kai Hoffmann (DevOps) <kai@wakir-labs.dev>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"0d4be6266b014acfb31b8074fe04beccc635a792","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/trees/0d4be6266b014acfb31b8074fe04beccc635a792"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/commits/482bd41982042d45d99b19bd87db2a47f93e97f6","verification":{"payload":"tree 0d4be6266b014acfb31b8074fe04beccc635a792\nparent ea5afc39d190709f3a3f11ceea219e80ce7f85c0\nauthor wakir-labs <xypu4r8e@addy.io> 1790069468 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790069468 +0200\n\nMake step 6 converge: apply the unit it installs, instead of installing it and hoping (#553)\n\n* Apply what step 6 installs, instead of installing it and hoping\n\nMeasured on the live pilot VM, 2026-09-21/22 (Operator-Hand):\n\n  unit file written    2026-09-21 20:11:44\n  container running    since 2026-09-20 19:59:46\n  unit says            PublishPort=0.0.0.0:8443:8443\n  actual listener      127.0.0.1:8443\n  bootstrap log        OK  ...-server-federation-wakir.service already\n                           active\n\nStep 6 rendered the unit, wrote it, ran daemon-reload, saw the service\nactive and skipped the start. The container had been created two runs\nearlier from an older unit. A systemctl restart made the installed\ndirective live in under a second -- and, as a side effect, dissolved\nthe federation asymmetry that was filed in May as \"Bug-39, asymmetric\ntopology by design\". It was not a design. It was a unit that had never\nbeen applied.\n\nGeneralised: any Quadlet change -- image pin, mount, env, resource\nlimit, PublishPort -- could be installed, logged OK and never applied.\nThe bootstrap was idempotent, not convergent.\n\nWhat changes\n\nTwo independent change-signals per service unit, both feeding one\ndecision in step 6h/6j:\n\n1. In-run signal. Every step-6 install path now goes through\n   _install_if_changed. Where the cmp -s guard fails and install runs,\n   the owning unit is marked (_note_config_change -> _mark_unit_changed).\n   Precise, state-free, blind to earlier runs.\n2. Applied-state fingerprint. After a successful start/restart,\n   _record_unit_applied writes the SHA-256 of every file that defines\n   the unit -- .container, the bind-mounted config, the referenced\n   .volume/.network units -- to /var/lib/wakir/bootstrap/applied/.\n   The next run compares. This is the signal that catches the\n   2026-09-21 case, where the unit came from run N-1 and the container\n   from run N-2.\n\nContent hash rather than mtime: mtime says nothing about content (an\noperator touch, a re-render producing identical bytes), and a container\nthat systemd restarted on its own after the file changed but before the\ndaemon-reload would look newer than a unit it never applied.\n\n_converge_service_unit then starts a stopped unit, restarts a running\nunit whose installed definition is not the one it was created from, and\nleaves an in-sync unit alone. Order stays server -> agent: the agent\npulls its join-token from a running server. The Bug-25 wait points\n(is-active, agent Workload-API socket) now run on the restart path too\n-- a restart re-opens the same race as a cold start.\n\nStated cost: on the first run after this lands, no unit has a record,\nso every running unit counts as \"convergence state unknown\" and is\nrestarted once. On a substrate where an unapplied unit may have sat\nthere for months, \"assume it is fine\" is the assumption that produced\nthis bug.\n\nJoin-token round-trip (necessary, not incidental)\n\nStep 6i seds the issued join-token into the installed agent unit, so\nthe installed file could never equal a freshly rendered template (which\ncarries WAKIR_JOIN_TOKEN_PLACEHOLDER). Under \"start only if stopped\"\nthat was invisible: every re-run rewrote the unit back to the\nplaceholder and the placeholder version was never applied -- until the\nnext VM reboot, where systemd would have started the agent with\n-joinToken WAKIR_JOIN_TOKEN_PLACEHOLDER (the Bug-37 crash-loop, armed\nand waiting). With convergence the same rewrite would restart the agent\ninto that unit on every run. _carried_join_token carries the live token\ninto the render, so a re-run produces a byte-identical file.\n\nTests\n\ntests/infra/test_pilot_bootstrap_step6_convergence.py drives behaviour,\nnot source text: it sources the bootstrap (main-gate), points the path\noverrides at a tmp tree and injects a recording systemctl.\n\n  TV-CONV-1   changed definition + running service -> restart. Red when\n              the restart is skipped.\n  TV-CONV-2   negative control: unchanged -> no start, no restart.\n  TV-CONV-4   no applied-state record -> one convergence pass, then\n              quiet.\n  TV-CONV-5   changed bind-mounted config -> restart (the ca_ttl class).\n  TV-CONV-8   the Bug-25 waits run on the restart path.\n  TV-CONV-9   mutation control: roll the drift check back to \"skip when\n              active\" and TV-CONV-1''s assertion goes red.\n\nThree existing source-discipline guards are re-pointed at the new call\nsite, with the invariant they protect stated in the docstring:\nBug-22''s server pre-start sweep, Bug-25''s step-6h wait, and\nTV-BRINGUP-04, which now additionally requires that the is-active guard\nreconsider a unit whose definition changed. test_pilot_bootstrap''s\nidempotency-marker list keeps its entry via the replacement line.\n\n\"OK <unit> already active\" is gone. The line that replaces it says\nwhether the running configuration matches the installed one.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Bind the federation gRPC API where the agent can reach it\n\nMeasured 2026-09-22, both pilot VMs: the SPIRE-Agent fails with\n``connection refused`` on 8081. Not ``x509: certificate signed by\nunknown authority`` -- that was the symptom on 2026-09-21, from a\ncontainer still running an older, reachable configuration.\n\nAll three federation server configs declared:\n\n    bind_address = \"127.0.0.1\"\n\nThat is the loopback of the SERVER CONTAINER''s network namespace. The\nSPIRE-Agent runs in its own container and dials the server over the\n``wakir-federation`` bridge by NetworkAlias, so it never reached that\nsocket. Unchanged since the configs landed -- the federation path never\nworked for an agent in its own container. ``spire-server-pilot-single-\norg.conf`` has bound 0.0.0.0 since the 2026-05-14 bring-up, for exactly\nthis reason, which is why the single-org path did work.\n\nThe comment above the line said the bind kept the gRPC API \"loopback-\nonly inside the container\", presenting a containment intent that its\nmechanism does not produce. The rewritten comment says what is true:\nthe agent container must reach this port over the container network,\nand what keeps the gRPC control-plane off the network is the Quadlet,\nwhich publishes 8081 to the HOST loopback only\n(``PublishPort=127.0.0.1:<HOST_GRPC_PORT>:8081``). No PublishPort is\nadded for 8081; host exposure stays exactly where it was.\n\n``spire-server-partner.conf`` carries the same line and the same\ntopology (hermetic same-host pair, agent in its own container), so it\nis pulled along rather than left as a second latent copy.\n\nT-FED-HCL-06 pins both halves: bind_address must be 0.0.0.0 on every\nfederation server config, and the server Quadlet must not publish 8081\nbeyond host loopback. One without the other is either an unreachable\nserver or an exposed control-plane.\n\nSeparate from the step-6 convergence commit on purpose: two defects,\ntwo commits, independently reviewable. They do interact -- once step 6\nconverges, it replaces the accidentally-working container on the pilot\nVM with the installed, correct, unreachable-until-this-commit\nconfiguration.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Refuse to apply an agent unit that still holds the token placeholder\n\nTwo halves of the same defect, both of which only become dangerous\nonce the bootstrap actually applies what it installs.\n\nStep 6d re-renders the agent unit from the template, which carries\n``-joinToken WAKIR_JOIN_TOKEN_PLACEHOLDER``. Step 6i replaced the\nplaceholder only when the agent was NOT yet attested. So on every\nre-run against an attested agent the installed unit went back to the\nplaceholder and stayed there -- invisible, because nothing ever\nrestarted the agent. Armed, though: the next VM reboot starts the agent\nfrom that unit, with the literal string as its join-token, and it\ncrash-loops (Bug-37).\n\n  * Step 6i now keys on the placeholder being present, not on the\n    attestation state. A token that goes unused costs one datastore row\n    and expires in an hour; a placeholder in an applied unit costs the\n    agent.\n  * Step 6h/6j refuse to start or restart an agent unit that still\n    carries the placeholder, with the file and the grep in the\n    diagnostic. Failing loudly beats applying a unit known to be\n    incoherent.\n\nTV-CONV-13 drives the refusal (exit 2, no systemctl start, no restart)\nand pins the 6i condition.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Make the NATS unit runnable: it ran as root against a 1000-owned store\n\nFound by the first converged step-6 run on the pilot VM, 2026-09-22\n08:34 UTC. NATS had been up 37 hours, healthy. The converged run\napplied the installed unit, and NATS died:\n\n    [FTL] Can''t start JetStream: storage directory is not writable\n\nDiagnosis, measured on the box:\n\n  * nats:2.11-alpine declares no USER, and the Quadlet declared no\n    User=, so the container runs as root.\n  * DropCapability=ALL takes CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE away from that root.\n  * The JetStream volume is owned 1000:1000, mode 755 -- the bootstrap\n    chowns it there in _pre_start_chown_sweep (kind=nats).\n  * Probe with the unit''s own settings: uid=0, touch\n    /data/jetstream/.probe -> Permission denied.\n\nSo the installed unit could not start at all. It survived because\nnothing had restarted it since the chown -- the same \"installed is not\napplied\" pattern this PR exists for, third instance, this time a unit\nthat was dead on arrival and looked healthy for four months.\n\nThe comment in _pre_start_chown_sweep claimed \"NATS runs as uid:1000\ntoo (compose parity)\". It does not: compose/nats.yaml sets no user\neither, and its volume is root-owned, which is why the compose track\nworks. The chown belongs to the Quadlet track alone. Comment corrected\nto say that.\n\nFix: the Quadlet now pins User=1000/Group=1000 -- the side the SPIRE\nunits already stand on and the side the on-disk JetStream data is\nalready owned by -- and the volume line carries :U so podman chowns the\nnamed volume to the container user at start (the Bug-26 discipline that\ntests/infra/test_quadlet_selinux_relabel.py enforces repo-wide, and\nwhich the NATS unit was exempt from only because it had no User=).\n\nRunning NATS as non-root is also the better posture: root with all\ncapabilities dropped is a root that cannot do its job.\n\ntest_quadlet_nats.py''s compose-parity vector is pulled along, with the\nreason the two tracks differ on this line spelled out.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Give the new convergence module a lane and refresh the counts\n\ntests/lanes/test_lane_assignment.py turns red on any test module without\na lane entry -- \"which gate runs this?\" is part of adding a test. The\nconvergence module joins the infra-substrate group on the optional-ci\nprofile, alongside the other pilot-bootstrap modules, and the measured\ncounts move 419 -> 420 modules / 100 -> 101 optional-ci.\n\nSelf-repair of my own PR: the production-suite failure and the\ndownstream drift-envelope failure both trace back to this one missing\nentry.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Kai Hoffmann (DevOps) <kai@wakir-labs.dev>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqskrcCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAATc8QAFoGWBUMOrbLwrbLAMM2eQPb\ngEj6WM7vxtrw+LIyFymiz/953Q5ZsOMu2ndpsswutskDeXW8lTtdXF10AnBb++Xe\nBEXGLO3M1wqFcHY8JV88PiDzvBetwygUl45JUZDXP4LFyBB8gpLs/n081yTa4os+\nFb/od4mZIwtNlCFHbo/6XwyLQ7kGLoIIDrPwBkgj8AdXoKJwW2XwF07Bjw7OM8pO\ntlOxQCmVJx0T7ltgKJg2EQOybLFiSoVI09pvOwOQZ34P/SeNSsM1/kA+XnOqmSrX\naCfea5LsleMeE99kvsO8t2JIbAZFL/IMj8n49AfWGaQQQavMsfaAJ11CnelTOkhP\nZv6fEY93qooukn5UvGX0WNqxQSEluV7EMgLQDx3scmo+0MsDn1aNcbgmj5/m+sL9\n0h+2cyNdRyY8xJMBR2i0hdWJfE8U0gHwAIiKW0gNI6XZ3ZtucSlVzSC2I75uj0Z/\n3jnGNMMDTZpiLoZ/0VA/fDenzz7FTMdjz+QCdsPX3cOnSkoYcztfBwneKy4ZptL+\noRyjSWiAXpcrXG4935Uh5RFpCJuOLTZjpUF3RX9QuZVLfkIFOjFgm55xOYjbmeml\nGjL9DMSL75tYCLYFiefAFFUq+rPmIahEhhDIfbCLlnHu9J3sFyD5+LldbwaJaibN\nqTd5vn4cqNsDs3djWYzC\n=1y1C\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-22T09:31:09Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/482bd41982042d45d99b19bd87db2a47f93e97f6","node_id":"C_kwDOSVv36NoAKDQ4MmJkNDE5ODIwNDJkNDVkOTliMTliZDg3ZGIyYTQ3ZjkzZTk3ZjY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/ea5afc39d190709f3a3f11ceea219e80ce7f85c0","sha":"ea5afc39d190709f3a3f11ceea219e80ce7f85c0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/ea5afc39d190709f3a3f11ceea219e80ce7f85c0"}],"sha":"482bd41982042d45d99b19bd87db2a47f93e97f6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/482bd41982042d45d99b19bd87db2a47f93e97f6"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/281068747?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs","id":281068747,"login":"wakir-labs","node_id":"U_kgDOEMDEyw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/ea5afc39d190709f3a3f11ceea219e80ce7f85c0/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-22T09:19:32Z","email":"xypu4r8e@addy.io","name":"wakir-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-22T09:19:32Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(observability): build the expectation window, and wire \"unmeasurable is red\" (#554)\n\nThe probe from the companion change produces a verdict and cannot\ndeliver one. This is the other half: an emitter that pushes the verdict\nout of the private network, and an evaluator that notices when it stops\narriving.\n\nDirection is push, and not for elegance. Pull needs a route into the\nprivate network; three named live-evidence lanes died on exactly that\nassumption, and a melder built the same way would have been the fourth.\nOutbound HTTPS from both nodes is measured to work. So the node speaks\nand the outside listens, and nothing here opens a port, holds a GitHub\ntoken, or grants anyone a way in.\n\nWhat each half refuses to do, all of it under test:\n\n* The emitter sends bad news too. Green, red and unmeasurable all go on\n  the wire and all exit 0. If it suppressed anything, silence would\n  carry two meanings — \"all well\" and \"badly broken\" — which is the\n  incident in one word.\n* The evaluator treats unmeasurable as red. Wired, not commented: the\n  substrate already showed what the alternative looks like, with a\n  container healthcheck returning ExitCode 1 and empty output every ten\n  seconds since May while publishing `unhealthy` as though it were a\n  finding. A probe that could not measure is not a sign of life.\n* The evaluator applies that rule to itself. No key, no topic, an\n  unreachable broker, an unreadable source: exit 2, never exit 0.\n* An empty --expect list is refused rather than defaulted. A window that\n  expects no node is satisfied by silence — the defect dressed as\n  configuration.\n* A forged heartbeat cannot silence the alarm. Anyone who learns the\n  topic can post to it, so every message carries HMAC-SHA256 over the\n  exact probe bytes. Two tests: a forgery cannot supply a missing node,\n  and a forgery cannot outrank a genuine red.\n* Recency is the probe''s own timestamp, never the broker''s arrival\n  order, so a replayed message still reads as old.\n\nNumbers, derived rather than chosen. Window 2 h = eight consecutive\nmissed pushes at the 15-minute probe cadence: one lost push must not\nfire, eight in a row is not chance. Broker lookback 4× the window, so a\nmessage that arrived just outside is seen and judged stale rather than\nnot seen — same verdict, different report, and the report is what\nsomebody reads at 9am.\n\nOne design ambiguity was caught by its own test rather than by review:\nlocal sources were read as raw heartbeat lines while broker sources were\nunwrapped from envelopes, so the envelope path had no coverage. Reading\nis now explicit (--source-format) instead of sniffed. A reader that\nguesses wrong here fails as \"found no heartbeats\", which is\nindistinguishable from the incident.\n\nThe level-3 drill is a dated obligation, not a runbook sentence.\ndocs/observability/substrate-liveness-drill-evidence.json carries the\ndates and a test compares them to the calendar: the seven-day window\nmust start by activate_by, the first drill is due on day eight, and it\nrecurs. A run recorded as anything but red-as-expected does not satisfy\nit — a drill that came out green did not prove the melder works, it\nproved it does not. Deferral has an end, because \"we have not started\nyet\" would otherwise be a permanently green answer.\n\nThe evaluator half of the drill runs hermetically on every pass, using\nthe two payloads the live nodes actually emitted on 2026-09-22 08:24:45\nUTC. They are recorded, not invented. There is deliberately no recorded\ngreen payload: no node has been green since May, so one would be a\nfabrication, and the green cases say they are synthesised.\n\nThe 6-hour staleness threshold stays marked as derived. It is computed\nfrom the measured SVID TTL and has never been calibrated against a\nhealthy agent, because there has not been one since May. The\ncalibration is owed at the seven-day run and is recorded in the evidence\nfile rather than remembered.\n\nThree new test modules, all assigned to the required hermetic-invariants\nlane. An instrument for noticing that a check stopped running does not\nget to have unassigned tests.\n\nCo-authored-by: Noa Bergstroem <noa@wakir.dev>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"fb98bc57108c992ac4e39ee9f8945fbc8fc15827","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/trees/fb98bc57108c992ac4e39ee9f8945fbc8fc15827"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/commits/ea5afc39d190709f3a3f11ceea219e80ce7f85c0","verification":{"payload":"tree fb98bc57108c992ac4e39ee9f8945fbc8fc15827\nparent 50da707e55730099dbb65943a9adc424904dcd5b\nauthor wakir-labs <xypu4r8e@addy.io> 1790068772 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790068772 +0200\n\nfeat(observability): build the expectation window, and wire \"unmeasurable is red\" (#554)\n\nThe probe from the companion change produces a verdict and cannot\ndeliver one. This is the other half: an emitter that pushes the verdict\nout of the private network, and an evaluator that notices when it stops\narriving.\n\nDirection is push, and not for elegance. Pull needs a route into the\nprivate network; three named live-evidence lanes died on exactly that\nassumption, and a melder built the same way would have been the fourth.\nOutbound HTTPS from both nodes is measured to work. So the node speaks\nand the outside listens, and nothing here opens a port, holds a GitHub\ntoken, or grants anyone a way in.\n\nWhat each half refuses to do, all of it under test:\n\n* The emitter sends bad news too. Green, red and unmeasurable all go on\n  the wire and all exit 0. If it suppressed anything, silence would\n  carry two meanings — \"all well\" and \"badly broken\" — which is the\n  incident in one word.\n* The evaluator treats unmeasurable as red. Wired, not commented: the\n  substrate already showed what the alternative looks like, with a\n  container healthcheck returning ExitCode 1 and empty output every ten\n  seconds since May while publishing `unhealthy` as though it were a\n  finding. A probe that could not measure is not a sign of life.\n* The evaluator applies that rule to itself. No key, no topic, an\n  unreachable broker, an unreadable source: exit 2, never exit 0.\n* An empty --expect list is refused rather than defaulted. A window that\n  expects no node is satisfied by silence — the defect dressed as\n  configuration.\n* A forged heartbeat cannot silence the alarm. Anyone who learns the\n  topic can post to it, so every message carries HMAC-SHA256 over the\n  exact probe bytes. Two tests: a forgery cannot supply a missing node,\n  and a forgery cannot outrank a genuine red.\n* Recency is the probe''s own timestamp, never the broker''s arrival\n  order, so a replayed message still reads as old.\n\nNumbers, derived rather than chosen. Window 2 h = eight consecutive\nmissed pushes at the 15-minute probe cadence: one lost push must not\nfire, eight in a row is not chance. Broker lookback 4× the window, so a\nmessage that arrived just outside is seen and judged stale rather than\nnot seen — same verdict, different report, and the report is what\nsomebody reads at 9am.\n\nOne design ambiguity was caught by its own test rather than by review:\nlocal sources were read as raw heartbeat lines while broker sources were\nunwrapped from envelopes, so the envelope path had no coverage. Reading\nis now explicit (--source-format) instead of sniffed. A reader that\nguesses wrong here fails as \"found no heartbeats\", which is\nindistinguishable from the incident.\n\nThe level-3 drill is a dated obligation, not a runbook sentence.\ndocs/observability/substrate-liveness-drill-evidence.json carries the\ndates and a test compares them to the calendar: the seven-day window\nmust start by activate_by, the first drill is due on day eight, and it\nrecurs. A run recorded as anything but red-as-expected does not satisfy\nit — a drill that came out green did not prove the melder works, it\nproved it does not. Deferral has an end, because \"we have not started\nyet\" would otherwise be a permanently green answer.\n\nThe evaluator half of the drill runs hermetically on every pass, using\nthe two payloads the live nodes actually emitted on 2026-09-22 08:24:45\nUTC. They are recorded, not invented. There is deliberately no recorded\ngreen payload: no node has been green since May, so one would be a\nfabrication, and the green cases say they are synthesised.\n\nThe 6-hour staleness threshold stays marked as derived. It is computed\nfrom the measured SVID TTL and has never been calibrated against a\nhealthy agent, because there has not been one since May. The\ncalibration is owed at the seven-day run and is recorded in the evidence\nfile rather than remembered.\n\nThree new test modules, all assigned to the required hermetic-invariants\nlane. An instrument for noticing that a check stopped running does not\nget to have unassigned tests.\n\nCo-authored-by: Noa Bergstroem <noa@wakir.dev>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqskgkCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAu8QQAFgJKDGeHcq7K70PpZPUyHtW\n6St7CWUGLrp4GgWlnADBKinsoMQ1H/B4WOcID482BVJL7Bv34nmWWuGjeVcuaGMz\nF/sQfoUFBnRv4HfvU1jO2XvdKa3cX10jHlG9qN8FymH/AdmU1TegHOx0StP/X325\nCcvso6AqjUNND3IvtVY7TFDfuac+vhGBfXMTtECcthl7pfFmsDMVqufRuFdpQ95B\nIavdkHxEVMNDKjbgY4SUaVJ/ejlsfej3DdDpF8R16DQaBMDqku9sfNepf3QyJC2q\nTwhMoEnMmoLbMkMr9RoEOBrl2KaNPDP9RZZb5mlxNCOFD+oNK7zPIEho/lYFXAS2\naAE5MSr2T4KapcnF6Klclixh7joUnfX8oAtQwvP0pbnZTbGvBjGd3kn3UJox4yJp\n9kjeJX17Z1RRVL9+V59W31pkMIOaln43N8zQDY6LrESjO71PJgrkkU+J1afvQoto\nCXaB7pVvu6Ey1RbEgI8EpqBz19SotRJgaWLP/a3WZZwV5W71vXnnTIhTBdIlIgfo\n+UdINNyRUmdPtzI7pOvGSjZMZk9dOqlGAKbvVZVH/BGmxSUD0EKnfbbfNigjftB+\nHL8O670qTqAl5OuKSeA+ZfMGG6UAQ60GWiULI2lgDwmb80fEJYqN2AUEdg6AnJwH\nGcuciSAgwfQ2ovVB/kaA\n=JRg4\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-22T09:19:32Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/ea5afc39d190709f3a3f11ceea219e80ce7f85c0","node_id":"C_kwDOSVv36NoAKGVhNWFmYzM5ZDE5MDcwOWYzYTNmMTFjZWVhMjE5ZTgwY2U3Zjg1YzA","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/50da707e55730099dbb65943a9adc424904dcd5b","sha":"50da707e55730099dbb65943a9adc424904dcd5b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/50da707e55730099dbb65943a9adc424904dcd5b"}],"sha":"ea5afc39d190709f3a3f11ceea219e80ce7f85c0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/ea5afc39d190709f3a3f11ceea219e80ce7f85c0"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/281068747?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs","id":281068747,"login":"wakir-labs","node_id":"U_kgDOEMDEyw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/50da707e55730099dbb65943a9adc424904dcd5b/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-22T09:07:54Z","email":"xypu4r8e@addy.io","name":"wakir-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-22T09:07:54Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Measure whether the agent still attests, since everything cheaper was true for four months (#551)\n\n* feat(observability): probe a SPIRE agent for the one signal that was not true\n\nTwo federation nodes did not attest for about 127 days and reached\nnobody. Measured read-only on both nodes on 2026-09-22 ~08:15 UTC,\nevery cheap signal a monitor would reasonably have used was true the\nwhole time:\n\n  * container state: `Up` — continuously, for four months\n  * container uptime: seconds, because systemd recreates the unit, so\n    a dead agent reads as a fresh healthy start\n  * `podman inspect .RestartCount`: 0, for the same reason — podman\n    never sees the restart that systemd performs\n  * workload-API socket file: present, dated May, nothing listening\n  * server container health: `unhealthy`, FailingStreak 636 — correct,\n    and read by nobody for four months\n\nThe one unambiguous reading was the expiry of the credential the agent\npersisted the last time it successfully attested: notAfter\n2026-05-15T08:32:35Z on one node, 2026-05-17T04:59:19Z on the other.\nThat is what this probe reads — offline, read-only, from the agent''s\nown state file.\n\nPrimary signal is `.svid[0]`''s notAfter and the newest notAfter in the\ncached `.bundle`. `bundle_expired` outranks `svid_expired` when both\nhold: an expired SVID can be renewed, an expired cached bundle cannot\nbe recovered from by the agent alone. The state file''s mtime is a\nsecond, independent opinion and deliberately not the primary one —\n`keys.json` in the same directory was rewritten on 2026-09-21 by a\nbootstrap re-run while `agent-data.json` kept its May timestamp, so a\nwatcher on the directory would have reported freshness.\n\nExit 2 (`unmeasurable`) is not exit 0. Missing tool, missing target,\nundecodable SVID and a state file of the wrong shape each get their own\nreason code and none of them reads green.\n\nThresholds are derived from measurement, not taste: grace = 3600 s =\none full `default_x509_svid_ttl` as read from the live server config,\nso red means at least two consecutive renewal windows were missed.\nA negative control asserts that an SVID expired for 50 minutes stays\ngreen; alert fatigue is a reliability risk, not a style question.\n\nNo jq and no python on the executable lines, asserted by a test. Both\nnodes run Fedora CoreOS 44 and have no python3 at all — the repo''s\nexisting systemd health units invoke a `.venv/bin/python3` that exists\non neither node. A probe whose job is to notice that something does\nnot run must not itself be unable to run.\n\n14 hermetic tests, certificates minted in-process, `now` and the state\npath injected, no network. Among them the replay of the originating\nincident: node A''s exact measured state evaluated at the hour it was\nmeasured must be red on the first sample — and the same state\nevaluated on the day of the May bring-up must be green, because the\nMay report of 6/6 PASS was not wrong, it was early.\n\nExecuted against both live nodes on 2026-09-22 08:24 UTC over stdin,\nnothing installed, nothing written: red / exit 1 on both, 11 231 530 s\nand 11 071 526 s of expiry respectively.\n\nThe probe produces a verdict; it does not deliver one. Transport,\ndead-man''s window and receiver are an open decision, stated as a limit\nin the runbook rather than left to be discovered.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(observability): a probe that cannot run must not look like one that ran\n\nMeasured on the substrate 2026-09-22 08:32 UTC, independently of the\nprobe''s own subject: the SPIRE containers carry\n\n    [\"CMD-SHELL\", \"/opt/spire/bin/spire-server healthcheck\"]\n\npodman runs a string HealthCmd through /bin/sh -c, and the SPIRE image\nhas no shell — `crun: executable file /bin/sh not found`. Every probe\nsince May returned `ExitCode 1, Output \"\"` at a ten-second interval,\nand the container has published `unhealthy` on the strength of it. The\nsame command run directly answers `Server is healthy.`\n\nThat is a check that never started, published as a judgement. It is\nthe defect class this probe exists for, one level down, and it lands\non the probe itself as a requirement: a probe that cannot run must be\ndistinguishable from a probe that ran and found nothing, and from a\nprobe that ran and found something. Empty output is not a finding.\n\nChanges:\n\n* `date` joins the required-tool precheck. It was used before it was\n  checked; with no `date` the payload would have been emitted with a\n  null epoch rather than a reason.\n* A test strips PATH to an empty directory and asserts the probe still\n  answers — valid JSON, exit 2, `verdict: unmeasurable`, reason\n  `missing_tool_<tool>` — and that it invents no measurement it did not\n  take (`svid_not_after_epoch` stays null).\n* The runbook''s signal table is corrected. It previously called the\n  `unhealthy` reading \"correct, and read by nobody\". It is not correct;\n  it happened to point the same way as the truth for unrelated reasons.\n\nThe five quadlets carrying the shell-form HealthCmd are NOT changed\nhere. Quadlet passthrough of the JSON-array form is measured\n(`/usr/libexec/podman/quadlet -dryrun`, podman 5.8.4, 2026-09-22), and\npodman-run.1 documents \"Multiple options can be passed in the form of a\nJSON array; otherwise, the command is interpreted as an argument to\n/bin/sh -c\" (retrieved 2026-09-22, HTTP 200) — but the resulting\n`[\"CMD\", ...]` form could not be executed and observed from this\nsandbox, and observing it on the substrate would mean creating a\ncontainer, which the read-only mandate for this run forbids. Half-\nverified is worse than openly named, so the fix is written up for a\nseparate task with the one missing step stated.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ci(lanes): answer \"which gate runs this?\" for the substrate-liveness probe\n\nThe lane-assignment gate turned red on the previous commit, exactly as\nits own comment in runtime-acceptance-gates.yml says it should:\n\n    A new module in these directories therefore lands `unassigned` and\n    turns tests/lanes/test_lane_assignment.py red until someone answers\n    \"which gate runs this?\". That is the intended cost.\n\nAnswered: `Lane — hermetic invariants (spec freeze, parity, routing)`,\nprofile `required`. The module is hermetic (certificates minted\nin-process, `now` and the state path injected, no network, no podman),\nand `cryptography>=42` is already among that lane''s dependencies.\n\nCounts re-derived: 420 modules, 317 required, unassigned stays 0.\n\nAdding this one to an optional lane would have been the joke telling\nitself — a check about checks that no gate runs.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Noa Bergstroem <noa@wakir.dev>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"5234debc7f929ab6f39abd0556c084cba838ca77","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/trees/5234debc7f929ab6f39abd0556c084cba838ca77"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/commits/50da707e55730099dbb65943a9adc424904dcd5b","verification":{"payload":"tree 5234debc7f929ab6f39abd0556c084cba838ca77\nparent 21e66f67e63d81d7ffdbf817ce77522c37243248\nauthor wakir-labs <xypu4r8e@addy.io> 1790068074 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790068074 +0200\n\nMeasure whether the agent still attests, since everything cheaper was true for four months (#551)\n\n* feat(observability): probe a SPIRE agent for the one signal that was not true\n\nTwo federation nodes did not attest for about 127 days and reached\nnobody. Measured read-only on both nodes on 2026-09-22 ~08:15 UTC,\nevery cheap signal a monitor would reasonably have used was true the\nwhole time:\n\n  * container state: `Up` — continuously, for four months\n  * container uptime: seconds, because systemd recreates the unit, so\n    a dead agent reads as a fresh healthy start\n  * `podman inspect .RestartCount`: 0, for the same reason — podman\n    never sees the restart that systemd performs\n  * workload-API socket file: present, dated May, nothing listening\n  * server container health: `unhealthy`, FailingStreak 636 — correct,\n    and read by nobody for four months\n\nThe one unambiguous reading was the expiry of the credential the agent\npersisted the last time it successfully attested: notAfter\n2026-05-15T08:32:35Z on one node, 2026-05-17T04:59:19Z on the other.\nThat is what this probe reads — offline, read-only, from the agent''s\nown state file.\n\nPrimary signal is `.svid[0]`''s notAfter and the newest notAfter in the\ncached `.bundle`. `bundle_expired` outranks `svid_expired` when both\nhold: an expired SVID can be renewed, an expired cached bundle cannot\nbe recovered from by the agent alone. The state file''s mtime is a\nsecond, independent opinion and deliberately not the primary one —\n`keys.json` in the same directory was rewritten on 2026-09-21 by a\nbootstrap re-run while `agent-data.json` kept its May timestamp, so a\nwatcher on the directory would have reported freshness.\n\nExit 2 (`unmeasurable`) is not exit 0. Missing tool, missing target,\nundecodable SVID and a state file of the wrong shape each get their own\nreason code and none of them reads green.\n\nThresholds are derived from measurement, not taste: grace = 3600 s =\none full `default_x509_svid_ttl` as read from the live server config,\nso red means at least two consecutive renewal windows were missed.\nA negative control asserts that an SVID expired for 50 minutes stays\ngreen; alert fatigue is a reliability risk, not a style question.\n\nNo jq and no python on the executable lines, asserted by a test. Both\nnodes run Fedora CoreOS 44 and have no python3 at all — the repo''s\nexisting systemd health units invoke a `.venv/bin/python3` that exists\non neither node. A probe whose job is to notice that something does\nnot run must not itself be unable to run.\n\n14 hermetic tests, certificates minted in-process, `now` and the state\npath injected, no network. Among them the replay of the originating\nincident: node A''s exact measured state evaluated at the hour it was\nmeasured must be red on the first sample — and the same state\nevaluated on the day of the May bring-up must be green, because the\nMay report of 6/6 PASS was not wrong, it was early.\n\nExecuted against both live nodes on 2026-09-22 08:24 UTC over stdin,\nnothing installed, nothing written: red / exit 1 on both, 11 231 530 s\nand 11 071 526 s of expiry respectively.\n\nThe probe produces a verdict; it does not deliver one. Transport,\ndead-man''s window and receiver are an open decision, stated as a limit\nin the runbook rather than left to be discovered.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(observability): a probe that cannot run must not look like one that ran\n\nMeasured on the substrate 2026-09-22 08:32 UTC, independently of the\nprobe''s own subject: the SPIRE containers carry\n\n    [\"CMD-SHELL\", \"/opt/spire/bin/spire-server healthcheck\"]\n\npodman runs a string HealthCmd through /bin/sh -c, and the SPIRE image\nhas no shell — `crun: executable file /bin/sh not found`. Every probe\nsince May returned `ExitCode 1, Output \"\"` at a ten-second interval,\nand the container has published `unhealthy` on the strength of it. The\nsame command run directly answers `Server is healthy.`\n\nThat is a check that never started, published as a judgement. It is\nthe defect class this probe exists for, one level down, and it lands\non the probe itself as a requirement: a probe that cannot run must be\ndistinguishable from a probe that ran and found nothing, and from a\nprobe that ran and found something. Empty output is not a finding.\n\nChanges:\n\n* `date` joins the required-tool precheck. It was used before it was\n  checked; with no `date` the payload would have been emitted with a\n  null epoch rather than a reason.\n* A test strips PATH to an empty directory and asserts the probe still\n  answers — valid JSON, exit 2, `verdict: unmeasurable`, reason\n  `missing_tool_<tool>` — and that it invents no measurement it did not\n  take (`svid_not_after_epoch` stays null).\n* The runbook''s signal table is corrected. It previously called the\n  `unhealthy` reading \"correct, and read by nobody\". It is not correct;\n  it happened to point the same way as the truth for unrelated reasons.\n\nThe five quadlets carrying the shell-form HealthCmd are NOT changed\nhere. Quadlet passthrough of the JSON-array form is measured\n(`/usr/libexec/podman/quadlet -dryrun`, podman 5.8.4, 2026-09-22), and\npodman-run.1 documents \"Multiple options can be passed in the form of a\nJSON array; otherwise, the command is interpreted as an argument to\n/bin/sh -c\" (retrieved 2026-09-22, HTTP 200) — but the resulting\n`[\"CMD\", ...]` form could not be executed and observed from this\nsandbox, and observing it on the substrate would mean creating a\ncontainer, which the read-only mandate for this run forbids. Half-\nverified is worse than openly named, so the fix is written up for a\nseparate task with the one missing step stated.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ci(lanes): answer \"which gate runs this?\" for the substrate-liveness probe\n\nThe lane-assignment gate turned red on the previous commit, exactly as\nits own comment in runtime-acceptance-gates.yml says it should:\n\n    A new module in these directories therefore lands `unassigned` and\n    turns tests/lanes/test_lane_assignment.py red until someone answers\n    \"which gate runs this?\". That is the intended cost.\n\nAnswered: `Lane — hermetic invariants (spec freeze, parity, routing)`,\nprofile `required`. The module is hermetic (certificates minted\nin-process, `now` and the state path injected, no network, no podman),\nand `cryptography>=42` is already among that lane''s dependencies.\n\nCounts re-derived: 420 modules, 317 required, unassigned stays 0.\n\nAdding this one to an optional lane would have been the joke telling\nitself — a check about checks that no gate runs.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Noa Bergstroem <noa@wakir.dev>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqskVqCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAbU0QADNOWqjPe0nnsYP7Kwg8Gk9d\no6zts6v9yOCrKGC8IeZ+Pg0U3jGNwjI4lavDa3PfacIE1ACvjM+mQX0/CQp8BTPd\n7wZDEfLxMgJfTNmDdGDfwl53W7WOYfDpdWTLSzlAlGBS9ZQM80LZHx4BA2L9nrr8\nLlnkyuP5v31g5w7GaUkkkf9DxgRWAXeEcigdzmTodxyshLNMuWN6PairOPs7MRUg\n1Zp+iqZ2gkan9hbYHgAc0hzy4MbqvU22fD3YyDPou5Oqu8DexCzWiWTjxcw5ooWn\ne56jQNoemV0CL0gS06MDEhJkqt7XheDeTvHit8nSmpyjrjB9XB7aQ7/aL2w/ZFEG\nEiiJPXLt+Pvx5xza/G5K6DUhiPteAHHTdQF0SQbU72wwBDNGFgjdSvT1Ojf6/Iv1\n1chk+SxgNQWirbWYrz940LGigWJRv9rq/eTm/BQDJWmIN+UV4TiDh6iS+B+hFfCk\ne9LQb+BU/61dKlTfCMIfmZSpwJuyS69HMgdaeMDPBPqMtc+ncBdWpvnK08e7nPiv\nuiZYfg6vrOtxxu1aYY/Ai20ph2QNf6tEKsIFsS+PlRaqCrfyMO97//j+0LEcJ5NX\nx6WiJf/TtgaHxU7Ld+ghrXThX4d+MEQO/+S/VNlOBCWTj64ByzwtuYvrcyyz/lit\nquOc+lFuscEXtkkCYR1n\n=cpL9\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-22T09:07:55Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/50da707e55730099dbb65943a9adc424904dcd5b","node_id":"C_kwDOSVv36NoAKDUwZGE3MDdlNTU3MzAwOTlkYmI2NTk0M2E5YWRjNDI0OTA0ZGNkNWI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/21e66f67e63d81d7ffdbf817ce77522c37243248","sha":"21e66f67e63d81d7ffdbf817ce77522c37243248","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/21e66f67e63d81d7ffdbf817ce77522c37243248"}],"sha":"50da707e55730099dbb65943a9adc424904dcd5b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/50da707e55730099dbb65943a9adc424904dcd5b"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/281068747?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs","id":281068747,"login":"wakir-labs","node_id":"U_kgDOEMDEyw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/21e66f67e63d81d7ffdbf817ce77522c37243248/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-22T08:47:25Z","email":"xypu4r8e@addy.io","name":"wakir-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-22T08:47:25Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Renew the live-VM exemption because the run happened and failed (#552)\n\nThe exemption was due 2026-09-30 on the strength of \"nobody has run it\".\nThat sentence is no longer true. On 2026-09-21/22 the acceptance ran for\nthe first time, three times, by operator hand against dbdda5a, and the\nsubstrate failed it: 5/8 smoke checks on one side, 4/8 on the other.\n\nSo the renewal rests on a different footing than the original entry, and\nthe file now says which one. The reason no longer claims the module has\nnever executed anywhere this repository can observe; it says it executed,\nwhat it found, and that the named workflow path is still not what\nproduced the evidence. live-vm-acceptance.yml remains dispatch-only with\nzero dispatches since 2026-05-16, and the second lane''s Real-VM job wants\na self-hosted runner label that no runner carries. Both are a separate\ndecision.\n\nexecution_evidence is restated rather than patched: the mechanism that\nactually produces evidence is the operator-hand run on the substrate\nnode, not the workflow, and the workflow''s zero stays visible next to the\nthree real runs instead of being overwritten by them. A last_actual_run\nof 2026-09-22 under the old mechanism string would have claimed a\ndispatch that never happened - the precise move this file exists to\nprevent.\n\nNew date 2026-10-24. ADR-0076 (approved 2026-09-22) settles the trust\nanchor; its acceptance requires seven days of unattended attestation,\nwhich cannot start until the convergence fix lands. The date is the\nearliest by which that window can have closed and been evaluated, and it\nsits apart from the 2026-10-31 cluster on purpose - this one should not\nbe reviewed in a batch.\n\nGates green: exemption_expiry --check, check_exemption_renewal against\nthe base, and 86 tests in tests/lanes/ plus tests/ci/test_evidence_citations.py.\n\nCo-authored-by: Kai Hoffmann (DevOps) <kai@wakir-labs.dev>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"27fb496b044c9c72b7ab2a6c687254dcd4fd52b8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/trees/27fb496b044c9c72b7ab2a6c687254dcd4fd52b8"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/commits/21e66f67e63d81d7ffdbf817ce77522c37243248","verification":{"payload":"tree 27fb496b044c9c72b7ab2a6c687254dcd4fd52b8\nparent dbdda5a5e8265d4b7c2b5fe848d875534bba88b1\nauthor wakir-labs <xypu4r8e@addy.io> 1790066845 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790066845 +0200\n\nRenew the live-VM exemption because the run happened and failed (#552)\n\nThe exemption was due 2026-09-30 on the strength of \"nobody has run it\".\nThat sentence is no longer true. On 2026-09-21/22 the acceptance ran for\nthe first time, three times, by operator hand against dbdda5a, and the\nsubstrate failed it: 5/8 smoke checks on one side, 4/8 on the other.\n\nSo the renewal rests on a different footing than the original entry, and\nthe file now says which one. The reason no longer claims the module has\nnever executed anywhere this repository can observe; it says it executed,\nwhat it found, and that the named workflow path is still not what\nproduced the evidence. live-vm-acceptance.yml remains dispatch-only with\nzero dispatches since 2026-05-16, and the second lane''s Real-VM job wants\na self-hosted runner label that no runner carries. Both are a separate\ndecision.\n\nexecution_evidence is restated rather than patched: the mechanism that\nactually produces evidence is the operator-hand run on the substrate\nnode, not the workflow, and the workflow''s zero stays visible next to the\nthree real runs instead of being overwritten by them. A last_actual_run\nof 2026-09-22 under the old mechanism string would have claimed a\ndispatch that never happened - the precise move this file exists to\nprevent.\n\nNew date 2026-10-24. ADR-0076 (approved 2026-09-22) settles the trust\nanchor; its acceptance requires seven days of unattended attestation,\nwhich cannot start until the convergence fix lands. The date is the\nearliest by which that window can have closed and been evaluated, and it\nsits apart from the 2026-10-31 cluster on purpose - this one should not\nbe reviewed in a batch.\n\nGates green: exemption_expiry --check, check_exemption_renewal against\nthe base, and 86 tests in tests/lanes/ plus tests/ci/test_evidence_citations.py.\n\nCo-authored-by: Kai Hoffmann (DevOps) <kai@wakir-labs.dev>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqskCdCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAANM8QAA0GgEzQNaoz/TMBAY+M+SGJ\nxW4T31VzT4iQzGmLPKad2QnQEhOUuBOrBG1vOzO5ygQEQC6saFWevtepGU8HF3US\ne0H45qykL0ACWSNDFbjs4ISmod0Ttgo8IGx5shiI3MBwnEosj1/sHZJPKkQfB9Ni\nNZAEx2WgM0QJcTGOeBYukbmoeIHTM1Vkg5HkaxeR1IBnEb3xtIty+czPQ7J/7imU\nyWiKUT7tkyF2bjy4J39CuoMQMCos1LJc5r0YY+sI9mARgEl9rT2ifI0yviSqu4Qe\nJlcS6bgSPMbaYMhi1IjUAdQ2G0fG8FnVFWTyBjljEAWj/2Rc7qut5PyjpYMbYR8A\n2zFeJ1XrKgS/bPEeblfY0AtsGT+GA9Ewjwarjd2OpRefxOZkNrx2VFvnr8MdA84U\nvOmFdRIt70UvANW9xFwpWxcf1VESm8wrHHRioQZMGhf3K1HEK15WcuDHwPRHSj4s\nBczvp0Th7LEPpqDBUnqcJWFoqpUebRCv+100IkyqGSI0qDSIsme1idLh6r90v5qa\ni+ca/rSAlK4w24kqhjdRyJyA28jOz0mR37RaN4yn9GxMaWFt3WlG3KWdTf5Z6FKX\nGyellZ6I+awjJ6PBhLVnsZn+ihgkXemPoZH8OGTYbilo8V/UTLZ4c4dnnjSp7/jQ\nCIn7T7pEpMurO8QEsPtl\n=Uq30\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-22T08:47:26Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/21e66f67e63d81d7ffdbf817ce77522c37243248","node_id":"C_kwDOSVv36NoAKDIxZTY2ZjY3ZTYzZDgxZDdmZmRiZjgxN2NlNzc1MjJjMzcyNDMyNDg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/dbdda5a5e8265d4b7c2b5fe848d875534bba88b1","sha":"dbdda5a5e8265d4b7c2b5fe848d875534bba88b1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/dbdda5a5e8265d4b7c2b5fe848d875534bba88b1"}],"sha":"21e66f67e63d81d7ffdbf817ce77522c37243248","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/21e66f67e63d81d7ffdbf817ce77522c37243248"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/281068747?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs","id":281068747,"login":"wakir-labs","node_id":"U_kgDOEMDEyw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/dbdda5a5e8265d4b7c2b5fe848d875534bba88b1/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-22T05:51:21Z","email":"xypu4r8e@addy.io","name":"wakir-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-22T05:51:21Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Make the image pipeline emit a digest that resolves, and the resolver refuse a pin that does not (#550)\n\n* fix(image-pipeline): resolve the pushed digest from the registry, and stop calling a stale pin a no-op\n\nThe 0.1.2 provisioner pin was a digest the registry never served. It\nentered the repo from the 2026-05-20 build, which resolved the pushed\ndigest with `buildah images --format ''{{ .Digest }}''` — the digest of\nthe manifest in the LOCAL build store, not the one GHCR addresses the\nimage under. The two differ whenever the push converts media types.\n\nThree things followed from that one line, and each hid the next:\n\n  - The Quadlet pin never resolved. Nobody noticed for four months,\n    because nothing pulled it: the VM was still running the image it\n    had cached in May.\n  - cosign signed the phantom digest, so the `.sig` tag exists for an\n    image that does not. `cosign verify` was green against nothing.\n  - The bring-up resolver saw a file with no DIGEST_PENDING placeholder,\n    printed \"no placeholder; skip\", and handed it through — while\n    holding the correct digest, resolved twenty lines earlier in the\n    same run.\n\nThe failure finally surfaced on 2026-09-21, live on wakir-pilot, as\n`manifest unknown` inside podman at bootstrap step 7 — two steps and\none process away from the line that caused it.\n\nFixes:\n\n  - The push step resolves the digest via `buildah push --digestfile`\n    and cross-checks it against `crane digest` and `skopeo inspect`,\n    the same pair the base-layer step already uses. A digest the\n    registry does not serve fails the build instead of becoming a pin.\n  - The resolver treats a concrete pin as something to check, not\n    something to skip. Pin equal to the live digest: OK. Pin different:\n    hard failure naming both digests, at resolve time, where the\n    operator is looking. It does not rewrite the pin — the committed\n    pin stays the source of truth (IMAGE_PINS.md).\n\nTested against the live registry state: the three SPIRE/python pins\nmatch their tags and pass; the provisioner pin is the only one that\nfails. Negative control (resolver run with the committed digest as the\nlive one) exits 0, and the placeholder-substitution path is unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* chore(provisioner): pin the bucket-init Quadlet to 0.1.4, built by the corrected pipeline\n\n0.1.2 stays as it is. Its tag still resolves; what never resolved is the\ndigest the repo pinned it at. Overwriting the tag would have destroyed\nthe evidence of that, and re-used a tag for different content — the\nhabit that made the original defect invisible.\n\n0.1.3 was built before the pipeline fix and carries the same phantom\ndigest and the same orphaned signature; it is skipped rather than\nreused, and left in place as the second instance of the defect.\n\n0.1.4 is the first artefact from the corrected pipeline. Verified\nagainst GHCR independently of the build, 2026-09-21:\n\n  digest ce9d1a70…  HTTP 200\n  tag 0.1.4 →       sha256:ce9d1a70…   (same digest, from the registry)\n  signature on it   HTTP 200           (on the real digest, not a phantom)\n\nFor contrast, the pin this replaces:\n\n  digest 9af7ee68…  HTTP 404\n  signature on it   HTTP 200           — a green verification of nothing\n\ntests/infra/test_wakir_provisioner_image_pin_form.py baselines the tag\nand moves with it. The test above it still accepts a placeholder OR a\nreal digest for the pin, which is how the dead one passed for four\nmonths; quadlet/wakir-recovery-drill-anchor.container currently relies\non that tolerance with an unresolved DIGEST_PENDING_REZA_CROSS_REVIEW\nthat no resolver path substitutes. Both are named in the PR body as\nfollow-ups rather than widened into here.\n\ntests/ci + tests/orchestrator + tests/infra green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(cosign-verify): correct the recorded cause of the dangling 0.1.2 pin\n\nThe dangling-pin check and the orphan-signature explanation, both added\nearlier on 2026-09-21, were right about the mechanism and wrong about\nthe cause. \"The tag had been re-pushed\" was a reasonable reading of a\nsingle data point. The live bring-up produced a second one hours later:\nthe 0.1.3 build reproduced the identical mismatch with nothing\nre-pushed anywhere.\n\nThe pin was never in the registry. The build named the digest from the\nlocal buildah store, and cosign signed that name. The check stays as\nit is — it catches the state regardless of how the pin got written —\nbut the comment no longer sends the next reader to the registry''s\nretention behaviour when the fault is in our own build.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(bootstrap,e2e): resolve the tag the Quadlet pins, and keep the new check honest in the hermetic lane\n\nTwo things the first commits got wrong, both found by CI rather than by\nme.\n\n**The bootstrap enumerated 0.1.2 in seven places** while the Quadlet\nnow pins 0.1.4. The resolver would have looked up the digest of one\nimage and compared it against the pin of another — a fresh instance of\nthe defect this branch exists to remove, introduced by the fix for it.\nThe tag lived as a literal in the bootstrap and in three test modules,\nso the rotation broke them one at a time, each failure naming only\nitself.\n\n`test_bootstrap_resolves_the_tag_the_quadlet_actually_pins` now asserts\nthe relationship instead of the value: whatever tag the Quadlet pins is\nthe tag the bootstrap must resolve. Verified by negative control —\nsetting the bootstrap to 0.1.9 fails it, reverting passes.\n\n**The e2e-container lane went red**, and it was right to. It stubs\nskopeo with one fictional digest for every image, so a check that\ncompares a committed pin against the digest it was handed disagrees\nwith every pin by construction. That lane does not assert digest truth;\nits own comment points at cosign-verify-images and resolve-image-pins-ci\nfor that.\n\nSo the lane declares itself: `WAKIR_SYNTHETIC_DIGESTS=1` adds\n`--synthetic-digests`, and the resolver then prints, per file, that it\nis NOT checking and why. An opt-out that stays quiet is how a check\nstops running without anyone deciding it should — which is the shape of\nthis whole branch.\n\n`test_only_the_hermetic_lane_declares_its_digests_synthetic` sweeps the\nrepo and fails if that env var ever appears in a shipped script,\nworkflow or runbook.\n\ntests/ci + tests/orchestrator + tests/infra green, 4 new resolver tests\nincluding the negative control that the check discriminates rather than\nmerely refuses.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(e2e): stop citing a lane that has never run as the reason this one need not check\n\nWhile writing the synthetic-digest opt-out I repeated a claim the file\nalready made: that this lane may skip digest truth because\n`cosign-verify-images` and `resolve-image-pins-ci` validate it\nelsewhere.\n\nHalf of that is true. `cosign-verify-images` runs on every push and PR.\n`resolve-image-pins-ci` is `workflow_dispatch`-only and, measured\n2026-09-21 against the run history, has never been run — not once since\nit was written.\n\nSo the pin lane most specifically named for this job has produced zero\nevidence, and three comments cited it as coverage. Naming a lane that\ndoes not run as the reason another one need not look is how a gap gets\nwritten down as covered — the same shape as the pin that was signed,\nverified, green, and absent from the registry.\n\nComments corrected. Whether that workflow should gain a trigger or be\nwithdrawn is a decision, not a comment fix, and is left to the follow-up\nnamed in the PR body.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Kai Hoffmann <kai@wakir.dev>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"7189160454bd524d7e36a064d669919d1b3acc89","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/trees/7189160454bd524d7e36a064d669919d1b3acc89"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/commits/dbdda5a5e8265d4b7c2b5fe848d875534bba88b1","verification":{"payload":"tree 7189160454bd524d7e36a064d669919d1b3acc89\nparent 64540938d5f124a8a37c1446929ab3fe9f01f877\nauthor wakir-labs <xypu4r8e@addy.io> 1790056281 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790056281 +0200\n\nMake the image pipeline emit a digest that resolves, and the resolver refuse a pin that does not (#550)\n\n* fix(image-pipeline): resolve the pushed digest from the registry, and stop calling a stale pin a no-op\n\nThe 0.1.2 provisioner pin was a digest the registry never served. It\nentered the repo from the 2026-05-20 build, which resolved the pushed\ndigest with `buildah images --format ''{{ .Digest }}''` — the digest of\nthe manifest in the LOCAL build store,