solves; what never resolved is the\ndigest the repo pinned it at. Overwriting the tag would have destroyed\nthe evidence of that, and re-used a tag for different content — the\nhabit that made the original defect invisible.\n\n0.1.3 was built before the pipeline fix and carries the same phantom\ndigest and the same orphaned signature; it is skipped rather than\nreused, and left in place as the second instance of the defect.\n\n0.1.4 is the first artefact from the corrected pipeline. Verified\nagainst GHCR independently of the build, 2026-09-21:\n\n  digest ce9d1a70…  HTTP 200\n  tag 0.1.4 →       sha256:ce9d1a70…   (same digest, from the registry)\n  signature on it   HTTP 200           (on the real digest, not a phantom)\n\nFor contrast, the pin this replaces:\n\n  digest 9af7ee68…  HTTP 404\n  signature on it   HTTP 200           — a green verification of nothing\n\ntests/infra/test_wakir_provisioner_image_pin_form.py baselines the tag\nand moves with it. The test above it still accepts a placeholder OR a\nreal digest for the pin, which is how the dead one passed for four\nmonths; quadlet/wakir-recovery-drill-anchor.container currently relies\non that tolerance with an unresolved DIGEST_PENDING_REZA_CROSS_REVIEW\nthat no resolver path substitutes. Both are named in the PR body as\nfollow-ups rather than widened into here.\n\ntests/ci + tests/orchestrator + tests/infra green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(cosign-verify): correct the recorded cause of the dangling 0.1.2 pin\n\nThe dangling-pin check and the orphan-signature explanation, both added\nearlier on 2026-09-21, were right about the mechanism and wrong about\nthe cause. \"The tag had been re-pushed\" was a reasonable reading of a\nsingle data point. The live bring-up produced a second one hours later:\nthe 0.1.3 build reproduced the identical mismatch with nothing\nre-pushed anywhere.\n\nThe pin was never in the registry. The build named the digest from the\nlocal buildah store, and cosign signed that name. The check stays as\nit is — it catches the state regardless of how the pin got written —\nbut the comment no longer sends the next reader to the registry''s\nretention behaviour when the fault is in our own build.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(bootstrap,e2e): resolve the tag the Quadlet pins, and keep the new check honest in the hermetic lane\n\nTwo things the first commits got wrong, both found by CI rather than by\nme.\n\n**The bootstrap enumerated 0.1.2 in seven places** while the Quadlet\nnow pins 0.1.4. The resolver would have looked up the digest of one\nimage and compared it against the pin of another — a fresh instance of\nthe defect this branch exists to remove, introduced by the fix for it.\nThe tag lived as a literal in the bootstrap and in three test modules,\nso the rotation broke them one at a time, each failure naming only\nitself.\n\n`test_bootstrap_resolves_the_tag_the_quadlet_actually_pins` now asserts\nthe relationship instead of the value: whatever tag the Quadlet pins is\nthe tag the bootstrap must resolve. Verified by negative control —\nsetting the bootstrap to 0.1.9 fails it, reverting passes.\n\n**The e2e-container lane went red**, and it was right to. It stubs\nskopeo with one fictional digest for every image, so a check that\ncompares a committed pin against the digest it was handed disagrees\nwith every pin by construction. That lane does not assert digest truth;\nits own comment points at cosign-verify-images and resolve-image-pins-ci\nfor that.\n\nSo the lane declares itself: `WAKIR_SYNTHETIC_DIGESTS=1` adds\n`--synthetic-digests`, and the resolver then prints, per file, that it\nis NOT checking and why. An opt-out that stays quiet is how a check\nstops running without anyone deciding it should — which is the shape of\nthis whole branch.\n\n`test_only_the_hermetic_lane_declares_its_digests_synthetic` sweeps the\nrepo and fails if that env var ever appears in a shipped script,\nworkflow or runbook.\n\ntests/ci + tests/orchestrator + tests/infra green, 4 new resolver tests\nincluding the negative control that the check discriminates rather than\nmerely refuses.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(e2e): stop citing a lane that has never run as the reason this one need not check\n\nWhile writing the synthetic-digest opt-out I repeated a claim the file\nalready made: that this lane may skip digest truth because\n`cosign-verify-images` and `resolve-image-pins-ci` validate it\nelsewhere.\n\nHalf of that is true. `cosign-verify-images` runs on every push and PR.\n`resolve-image-pins-ci` is `workflow_dispatch`-only and, measured\n2026-09-21 against the run history, has never been run — not once since\nit was written.\n\nSo the pin lane most specifically named for this job has produced zero\nevidence, and three comments cited it as coverage. Naming a lane that\ndoes not run as the reason another one need not look is how a gap gets\nwritten down as covered — the same shape as the pin that was signed,\nverified, green, and absent from the registry.\n\nComments corrected. Whether that workflow should gain a trigger or be\nwithdrawn is a decision, not a comment fix, and is left to the follow-up\nnamed in the PR body.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Kai Hoffmann <kai@wakir.dev>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqshdZCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAGk8QAFTa6aDYS+uFeV3u0kAu73xc\ngPvf1vM7ZE1yOOZVJjG5E0FlwBomGMDOClQxSzRAD9d+7YoOiOpvl6ogSW93ugw/\ndtw0khlSWYVJ3UZfm/HW72SJQMNxJXkb09/bIcre8PkL0iIHhuXgMTC4desx08nP\nE8bPcFPfw6yxQVbx1n0wcid1qWfaMd19koAdXsLw6tkF4zzwJpOK1NKH4xxjb5jf\nfZU9jhqvDqPKTh1nUYwfu34vQRso+C0h4Mqf+omQEgZnGKAi/b3NSLydVCu9O9Qb\neKhFBHBkEKS4PMAbHLETxnB4XqlpUv9OUdea0cGp2tO/nFj3V6h4FZn1HNB85VJ6\n96FGQ/8IjCp+ogVcrkKE9t5DqR+qXJ0JpMTRx7qVgD1lJiwxQkZssOe+OfvmfuJs\nvCeO/PPBJ0fhTSViLQTwaoZu/2+wVBFdJ25spoyTuDu+C2sZ2V0lSQ3EvCmWA4tQ\netxbXA8wxITR3fjYndumKTgRNo4xZZm30ZyrYvff6zPl6hKcJ3R+VgNA62utv852\nrYTPzIBv43WoziQ/WMfIzCfT/r0Z/sORT3Siv6T2Gwb2K9V0pGFOo652KjDNTcf9\n8L1PbgGfaXXUmfz95nDCfCZCWq68WiS8QEJ7OvHJ7jwkB+PzOAbBJrHLCDqCoGzE\n21awWS2Jj9+sAFVohXpz\n=Qi4i\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-22T05:51:22Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/dbdda5a5e8265d4b7c2b5fe848d875534bba88b1","node_id":"C_kwDOSVv36NoAKGRiZGRhNWE1ZTgyNjVkNGI3YzJiNWZlODQ4ZDg3NTUzNGJiYTg4YjE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/64540938d5f124a8a37c1446929ab3fe9f01f877","sha":"64540938d5f124a8a37c1446929ab3fe9f01f877","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/64540938d5f124a8a37c1446929ab3fe9f01f877"}],"sha":"dbdda5a5e8265d4b7c2b5fe848d875534bba88b1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/dbdda5a5e8265d4b7c2b5fe848d875534bba88b1"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/281068747?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs","id":281068747,"login":"wakir-labs","node_id":"U_kgDOEMDEyw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/64540938d5f124a8a37c1446929ab3fe9f01f877/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-21T18:26:20Z","email":"xypu4r8e@addy.io","name":"wakir-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-21T18:26:20Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Build the receiver for the scheduled lanes, and make the readiness check measure its own premises (#547)\n\n* fix(readiness): make gate G6 measure the required-check surface instead of asserting it\n\nThe cosign-strict-mode readiness check carried two premises in its\ndocstring that were both false when measured against main @ 67a62fc\non 2026-09-21:\n\n  * \"cosign-keyless-oidc-drift-probe.yml ... is NOT a required-status-\n    check\" — it IS one of the 13 contexts on main (live-read\n    2026-09-21, and pinned on disk in tests/lanes/lane_assignment.json).\n  * \"cosign-verify-images.yml is workflow_dispatch only — not blocking\n    any PR\" — false since PR #503 added push + pull_request.\n\nPR #546 corrected the same claim in the *document*; the script kept it.\nThis is the tool that evaluates gates G1..G6 before the strict-mode\nflip, it has run daily 124 times, and it judged from a remembered\nstate of a past day — the EXPECTED_CHECK_COUNT class from ADR-0075.\n\nCorrecting the prose alone would rebuild the same defect, so G6 is\nconverted from an assertion into a measurement. It used to check that\na hardcoded tuple was non-empty, i.e. it could not fail for any state\nof the repository. It now resolves each canonical display-name in two\ndirections, both from disk, both hermetic:\n\n  a) exactly one jobs.<id>.name across .github/workflows/ (zero = the\n     forever-pending context of PR #102; two = ambiguous),\n  b) present in the on-disk required-context snapshot, which is what\n     makes S3 measurable rather than assumed.\n\nAn unreadable workflow dir or snapshot yields NOT-CHECKED, which\nblocks the aggregate: a gate that cannot measure must not report GREEN.\n\nMeasured result on this tree: G6 GREEN, both names resolve to exactly\none job each and both are required contexts — green for the true\nreason now, over 40 workflow files.\n\nTests: TV-SM-16 rewritten to the measuring signature, plus TV-SM-29..34\n(no job home / ambiguous / not-required / unmeasurable surface / real\non-disk resolution / the specific premise that was false). No test\ndeleted, no expectation weakened.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(ci): build a receiver for the six lanes that fail where nobody looks\n\nMeasured on main @ 67a62fc, 2026-09-21: 40 workflow files, 0 `gh issue\ncreate`, 0 `actions/github-script`, 0 `if: failure()` handlers, 0 ntfy\ncalls — the two ntfy hits in sbom-verification-daily.yml are comment\nlines describing an intention. Six workflows carry a `schedule:`\ntrigger; all six fire reliably (73-74 runs each, latest today), and the\nlast 30 scheduled runs of all six are green.\n\nThe green history is the problem, not the comfort: nothing has ever\nfailed on that surface, so the repository holds no evidence that a\nfailure would reach anyone. Until this commit, \"move it to `schedule`\nso it fails loudly\" was a contradiction in terms here — `schedule` was\nnot a loud place, it was an unobserved one.\n\nThis adds `.github/actions/report-scheduled-failure`, a composite\naction with two modes:\n\n  * fail    — ensure exactly ONE open issue per workflow under a FIXED\n              title; later failures append a comment. The title is the\n              dedupe key: a probe that fails for 74 days must produce\n              one issue with 74 comments, not 74 issues.\n  * resolve — close that issue on the next successful non-PR run, so an\n              open receiver issue always means \"red right now\".\n\nWired into all six scheduled workflows as one extra job. `issues:\nwrite` is scoped to the receiver job alone; every watched job keeps\n`contents: read`. Neither mode runs on `pull_request` — a PR failure\nalready has a receiver, the PR.\n\nAlso adds a temporary negative-control workflow (deleted later in this\nPR) that produces the actual evidence, and the runbook\ndocs/operations/scheduled-workflow-failure-receiver.md with the\nconsumer catalog per the Receiver-Catalog duty.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(ci): cut only job 2 off the PR surface, and correct the model it was justified with\n\nZone-C cross-review (Tomás Reinhart, 2026-09-21) gave the cut for one\nof the two jobs and refused it for the other. This implements exactly\nthat, no more.\n\nMoved: `digest-verify python:3.13-slim`. DockerHub rebuilds the tag on\nits own calendar; no pull request can cause that drift or cure it. It\nnow runs on `schedule` + `push` + `workflow_dispatch` via\n`if: github.event_name != ''pull_request''`. The structural half it used\nto cover — all pin sites carrying the same digest — is in the\nrequired, hermetic `verify-containerfile-base-image-digest-pins` since\n#546. No coverage is lost.\n\nNot moved: `cosign verify wakir-provisioner`. Our image, our registry,\nour workflow, our Quadlet pin — nothing in it follows a third party''s\ncalendar, so it fails the very test that moves job 2. It is red today\nfor a true reason (the pinned digest 404s), and being red is not an\nargument for belonging somewhere else; otherwise every finding would\njustify its own invisibility. The remedy is the rebuild + pin refresh.\n\nC2 — the model was cited wrongly and the citation is replaced, not\nwaved through. `Cosign-Keyless-OIDC-Drift-Probe (daily)` was quoted as\nprecedent for \"liveness moves to schedule\". Verified against the file\nand the live rule: it runs on schedule AND dispatch AND push AND\npull_request, it IS one of the 13 required contexts, and it is\nhermetic. Its calendar axis is an addition to its PR axis, never a\nreplacement — it is evidence for the opposite of what it was cited\nfor, and it is the model for the *shape* of this change instead.\n\nC3 — form: no new file, no new context name, `schedule:` added to the\nexisting `on:` block, one `if:` on job 2. A split would have left\ntests/infra/test_cosign_drift_coverage_a6.py green while its own\ncomment (\"three places\") turned false.\n\nWhat the cut does NOT do, named because the opposite was easy to\nclaim: it does not take liveness off the PR surface. Job 1 is itself a\nlive network check (cosign verify + crane cross-check, exit 2 on\ndrift) and the only one of the three that can block anything — an\nupstream re-push or a Sigstore outage reds a required context on every\nPR. The cut removes the two exposures that block nothing and leaves\nthe one that blocks everything.\n\nThe receiver is wired into this workflow too, watching all three jobs:\na `push: main` failure had no reader either. Expect exactly one issue\non the first push to main after this lands — job 3''s true red — which\nthe receiver closes by itself once the pin is refreshed.\n\nAlso corrects §1 of docs/operations/cosign-strict-mode-activation.md,\nwhich still carried the same two false premises that #546 fixed in §S1\nand this PR fixes in the readiness script.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(ci): flip the negative control to success to prove the recovery leg\n\nThird and last leg of the receiver proof: run 1 created issue #548,\nrun 2 failed again and created no second issue (comment only), run 3\nsucceeds and must close #548.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* chore(ci): remove the negative-control workflow and record its evidence\n\nThe receiver is proven, so the scaffolding goes. A workflow-dispatch-\nonly or branch-only workflow left lying around is the anti-pattern\nthis PR argues against (resolve-image-pins-ci.yml: 0 runs, ever).\n\nEvidence now lives in the runbook, with the three run URLs:\n  run 1 failure -> issue #548 created\n  run 2 failure -> no second issue, comment appended\n  run 3 success -> #548 closed with a recovery comment\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Fred <fabian@callandor.gmbh>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"81545bc39a23e774b94e506b70a9f8a8d4a35d4d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/trees/81545bc39a23e774b94e506b70a9f8a8d4a35d4d"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/commits/64540938d5f124a8a37c1446929ab3fe9f01f877","verification":{"payload":"tree 81545bc39a23e774b94e506b70a9f8a8d4a35d4d\nparent 67a62fc78bf5dfd5f3b46a7d70b2805a9444c067\nauthor wakir-labs <xypu4r8e@addy.io> 1790015180 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790015180 +0200\n\nBuild the receiver for the scheduled lanes, and make the readiness check measure its own premises (#547)\n\n* fix(readiness): make gate G6 measure the required-check surface instead of asserting it\n\nThe cosign-strict-mode readiness check carried two premises in its\ndocstring that were both false when measured against main @ 67a62fc\non 2026-09-21:\n\n  * \"cosign-keyless-oidc-drift-probe.yml ... is NOT a required-status-\n    check\" — it IS one of the 13 contexts on main (live-read\n    2026-09-21, and pinned on disk in tests/lanes/lane_assignment.json).\n  * \"cosign-verify-images.yml is workflow_dispatch only — not blocking\n    any PR\" — false since PR #503 added push + pull_request.\n\nPR #546 corrected the same claim in the *document*; the script kept it.\nThis is the tool that evaluates gates G1..G6 before the strict-mode\nflip, it has run daily 124 times, and it judged from a remembered\nstate of a past day — the EXPECTED_CHECK_COUNT class from ADR-0075.\n\nCorrecting the prose alone would rebuild the same defect, so G6 is\nconverted from an assertion into a measurement. It used to check that\na hardcoded tuple was non-empty, i.e. it could not fail for any state\nof the repository. It now resolves each canonical display-name in two\ndirections, both from disk, both hermetic:\n\n  a) exactly one jobs.<id>.name across .github/workflows/ (zero = the\n     forever-pending context of PR #102; two = ambiguous),\n  b) present in the on-disk required-context snapshot, which is what\n     makes S3 measurable rather than assumed.\n\nAn unreadable workflow dir or snapshot yields NOT-CHECKED, which\nblocks the aggregate: a gate that cannot measure must not report GREEN.\n\nMeasured result on this tree: G6 GREEN, both names resolve to exactly\none job each and both are required contexts — green for the true\nreason now, over 40 workflow files.\n\nTests: TV-SM-16 rewritten to the measuring signature, plus TV-SM-29..34\n(no job home / ambiguous / not-required / unmeasurable surface / real\non-disk resolution / the specific premise that was false). No test\ndeleted, no expectation weakened.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <norepl