eview (Tomás Reinhart, 2026-09-21) gave the cut for one\nof the two jobs and refused it for the other. This implements exactly\nthat, no more.\n\nMoved: `digest-verify python:3.13-slim`. DockerHub rebuilds the tag on\nits own calendar; no pull request can cause that drift or cure it. It\nnow runs on `schedule` + `push` + `workflow_dispatch` via\n`if: github.event_name != ''pull_request''`. The structural half it used\nto cover — all pin sites carrying the same digest — is in the\nrequired, hermetic `verify-containerfile-base-image-digest-pins` since\n#546. No coverage is lost.\n\nNot moved: `cosign verify wakir-provisioner`. Our image, our registry,\nour workflow, our Quadlet pin — nothing in it follows a third party''s\ncalendar, so it fails the very test that moves job 2. It is red today\nfor a true reason (the pinned digest 404s), and being red is not an\nargument for belonging somewhere else; otherwise every finding would\njustify its own invisibility. The remedy is the rebuild + pin refresh.\n\nC2 — the model was cited wrongly and the citation is replaced, not\nwaved through. `Cosign-Keyless-OIDC-Drift-Probe (daily)` was quoted as\nprecedent for \"liveness moves to schedule\". Verified against the file\nand the live rule: it runs on schedule AND dispatch AND push AND\npull_request, it IS one of the 13 required contexts, and it is\nhermetic. Its calendar axis is an addition to its PR axis, never a\nreplacement — it is evidence for the opposite of what it was cited\nfor, and it is the model for the *shape* of this change instead.\n\nC3 — form: no new file, no new context name, `schedule:` added to the\nexisting `on:` block, one `if:` on job 2. A split would have left\ntests/infra/test_cosign_drift_coverage_a6.py green while its own\ncomment (\"three places\") turned false.\n\nWhat the cut does NOT do, named because the opposite was easy to\nclaim: it does not take liveness off the PR surface. Job 1 is itself a\nlive network check (cosign verify + crane cross-check, exit 2 on\ndrift) and the only one of the three that can block anything — an\nupstream re-push or a Sigstore outage reds a required context on every\nPR. The cut removes the two exposures that block nothing and leaves\nthe one that blocks everything.\n\nThe receiver is wired into this workflow too, watching all three jobs:\na `push: main` failure had no reader either. Expect exactly one issue\non the first push to main after this lands — job 3''s true red — which\nthe receiver closes by itself once the pin is refreshed.\n\nAlso corrects §1 of docs/operations/cosign-strict-mode-activation.md,\nwhich still carried the same two false premises that #546 fixed in §S1\nand this PR fixes in the readiness script.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(ci): flip the negative control to success to prove the recovery leg\n\nThird and last leg of the receiver proof: run 1 created issue #548,\nrun 2 failed again and created no second issue (comment only), run 3\nsucceeds and must close #548.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* chore(ci): remove the negative-control workflow and record its evidence\n\nThe receiver is proven, so the scaffolding goes. A workflow-dispatch-\nonly or branch-only workflow left lying around is the anti-pattern\nthis PR argues against (resolve-image-pins-ci.yml: 0 runs, ever).\n\nEvidence now lives in the runbook, with the three run URLs:\n  run 1 failure -> issue #548 created\n  run 2 failure -> no second issue, comment appended\n  run 3 success -> #548 closed with a recovery comment\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Fred <fabian@callandor.gmbh>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqsXbMCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA3bIQAENe2dDqMPv8gSto9GibHr9Z\nNQmOHeN8W3A7eAxJ0F+OP7OGaIucEdsAvM9vZPUQrOUBP6m2ZP1AZ8SUI/zlmLND\nGIkXJmcrqp7wN/r4gdInB7Fld6010DhQxK9HHCA0sOxzMUFRlwVdM553t+f4V/03\nSBbrLQHpmq5flYtMvdiHZr7NXKruW3YItjUx9LalKQLqZud6GKd1UT+sRLUfWCmF\noWfQH3EmIeepyN5rQbe3JHOJ9mehJ20XHnwS78JDr+Yz7mLJ5PrA/dkFduUKV8cv\n3U/hLypZCvV06nkGvFhggO9ErYt2TAAZnYGluX3AsnVdH4zPlB3+VDXEUmAuVoiL\nHm4tg7/DEvcinghDQ2qeoeVVgxVmSOZP2o+1OGpyW0J/wBd+lOIsxtmaLvKrrnt/\nmlAOZDB63SEcNjUx/jmDsc+G/ikiV8DGmUX+uWCjRfF2lyukbUSGTuPOCSVnWEJP\nWeS5xK1Bg5Hg6oHrhg4h/N/DDLjV3ATyK47CtLietaM12pp9VMFrow2fpxrH2uvi\nUg7ovxmBao+/7H7B4oGgxhLCJiFSmmIHqg0R2QUe8Ak83iSSO6/AiAnxUVPT4+ZO\nQmXoXHEzkXJAMEE1cRLGJxB+d0u+auHz8oGcoTV5Phi0oZA1PnIpAWUWa3cvccgX\nnOigydgWBiv5TOK1rD8C\n=cNh7\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-21T18:26:21Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/64540938d5f124a8a37c1446929ab3fe9f01f877","node_id":"C_kwDOSVv36NoAKDY0NTQwOTM4ZDVmMTI0YThhMzdjMTQ0NjkyOWFiM2ZlOWYwMWY4Nzc","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/67a62fc78bf5dfd5f3b46a7d70b2805a9444c067","sha":"67a62fc78bf5dfd5f3b46a7d70b2805a9444c067","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/67a62fc78bf5dfd5f3b46a7d70b2805a9444c067"}],"sha":"64540938d5f124a8a37c1446929ab3fe9f01f877","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/64540938d5f124a8a37c1446929ab3fe9f01f877"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/281068747?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs","id":281068747,"login":"wakir-labs","node_id":"U_kgDOEMDEyw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/67a62fc78bf5dfd5f3b46a7d70b2805a9444c067/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-20T22:32:31Z","email":"xypu4r8e@addy.io","name":"wakir-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-20T22:32:31Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Close the digest-gate coverage gap and resolve the 14 base-image placeholders (#546)\n\nThree things, one movement: a gate that was green about files it never\nread, and 14 pins that were not pins.\n\nM3 — coverage. `digest-verify python:3.13-slim` extracted the pin with\n`grep ... infra/spire/federation/provisioner/Containerfile | head -n1`.\nOne of three files, and of that file the first match. A change that\nrefreshed the provisioner Containerfile alone turned the check green\nwhile two files stayed on the old digest. New hermetic tool\n`tooling/ci/verify_image_pin_consistency.py`: every file that pins the\nsame `<image>:<tag>` must pin the same digest, tree-wide across\n`infra/`, `quadlet/` and `compose/`. Wired as a second step inside the\nexisting required job `verify-containerfile-base-image-digest-pins` —\nno new branch-protection context name. The live job now derives its\nsite list from the same module, so structural and live checks cannot\ndisagree about where the pins are. Negative control replays the defect:\nthree files, one updated, gate red.\n\nM1 — the 14 `DIGEST_PENDING_KAI_REVIEW` placeholders are resolved, not\ndated. `rust:1.85-slim-bookworm` and `gcr.io/distroless/cc-debian12:\nnonroot` both resolve, so the better outcome was available. The\nplaceholders were not a deferred decision: the seven\n`build-rust-cli-*.yml` workflows substituted them in-runner with a\nfreshly resolved digest, which meant the repository pinned nothing\nwhile the required gate reported PASS on the `@sha256:` shape. The\ndigests are now committed and the workflows verify them against the\nlive registry instead, fail-closed on drift. Same dead substitution\nremoved from `build-wakir-provisioner.yml` and\n`build-wakir-persona-engine.yml`, where the sed had been a no-op since\nPR #503 and only its misleading error message survived.\n`DIGEST_PENDING_KAI_REVIEW` is removed from the allow-list in\n`verify_containerfile_base_image_digest.py`.\n\nDoc drift. `docs/operations/cosign-strict-mode-activation.md` §S1\ndescribed a path-filtered trigger surface that PR #503 never created,\nand the workflow header still claimed a workflow_dispatch-only posture.\nBoth now describe what is measurably there.\n\nFinding, fixed in the gate, not in the pin:\n`ghcr.io/wakir-labs/wakir-provisioner:0.1.2` is pinned at\n`sha256:9af7ee68...`, which returns MANIFEST_UNKNOWN from ghcr.io. The\ntag was re-pushed; the pinned manifest is gone and only its orphaned\n`.sig` tag remains. `cosign verify` was green throughout, because it\nchecks the signature tag derived from the digest and never needs the\nimage. The one step that noticed the mismatch had it explained away by\na comment about a Sigstore wrapper layer that does not exist. Existence\nis now a separate, fatal check; the false comment is corrected. The\nremedy is a rebuild and re-sign, which is Operator-Hand.\n\nNot in this PR: the trigger cut for the two non-required liveness jobs.\nIt touches Cross-Review Zone C and has no sign-off.\n\nCo-authored-by: Kai Hoffmann <fabian@callandor.gmbh>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"d572aaab9f114d1b916db7596dd51cf492c35568","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/trees/d572aaab9f114d1b916db7596dd51cf492c35568"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/commits/67a62fc78bf5dfd5f3b46a7d70b2805a9444c067","verification":{"payload":"tree d572aaab9f114d1b916db7596dd51cf492c35568\nparent 649d0729468c7c7fc175866013d67671efd49d43\nauthor wakir-labs <xypu4r8e@addy.io> 1789943551 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789943551 +0200\n\nClose the digest-gate coverage gap and resolve the 14 base-image placeholders (#546)\n\nThree things, one movement: a gate that was green about files it never\nread, and 14 pins that were not pins.\n\nM3 — coverage. `digest-verify python:3.13-slim` extracted the pin with\n`grep ... infra/spire/federation/provisioner/Containerfile | head -n1`.\nOne of three files, and of that file the first match. A change that\nrefreshed the provisioner Containerfile alone turned the check green\nwhile two files stayed on the old digest. New hermetic tool\n`tooling/ci/verify_image_pin_consistency.py`: every file that pins the\nsame `<image>:<tag>` must pin the same digest, tree-wide across\n`infra/`, `quadlet/` and `compose/`. Wired as a second step inside the\nexisting required job `verify-containerfile-base-image-digest-pins` —\nno new branch-protection context name. The live job now derives its\nsite list from the same module, so structural and live checks cannot\ndisagree about where the pins are. Negative control replays the defect:\nthree files, one updated, gate red.\n\nM1 — the 14 `DIGEST_PENDING_KAI_REVIEW` placeholders are resolved, not\ndated. `rust:1.85-slim-bookworm` and `gcr.io/distroless/cc-debian12:\nnonroot` both resolve, so the better outcome was available. The\nplaceholders were not a deferred decision: the seven\n`build-rust-cli-*.yml` workflows substituted them in-runner with a\nfreshly resolved digest, which meant the repository pinned nothing\nwhile the required gate reported PASS on the `@sha256:` shape. The\ndigests are now committed and the workflows verify them against the\nlive registry instead, fail-closed on drift. Same dead substitution\nremoved from `build-wakir-provisioner.yml` and\n`build-wakir-persona-engine.yml`, where the sed had been a no-op since\nPR #503 and only its misleading error message survived.\n`DIGEST_PENDING_KAI_REVIEW` is removed from the allow-list in\n`verify_containerfile_base_image_digest.py`.\n\nDoc drift. `docs/operations/cosign-strict-mode-activation.md` §S1\ndescribed a path-filtered trigger surface that PR #503 never created,\nand the workflow header still claimed a workflow_dispatch-only posture.\nBoth now describe what is measurably there.\n\nFinding, fixed in the gate, not in the pin:\n`ghcr.io/wakir-labs/wakir-provisioner:0.1.2` is pinned at\n`sha256:9af7ee68...`, which returns MANIFEST_UNKNOWN from ghcr.io. The\ntag was re-pushed; the pinned manifest is gone and only its orphaned\n`.sig` tag remains. `cosign verify` was green throughout, because it\nchecks the signature tag derived from the digest and never needs the\nimage. The one step that noticed the mismatch had it explained away by\na comment about a Sigstore wrapper layer that does not exist. Existence\nis now a separate, fatal check; the false comment is corrected. The\nremedy is a rebuild and re-sign, which is Operator-Hand.\n\nNot in this PR: the trigger cut for the two non-required liveness jobs.\nIt touches Cross-Review Zone C and has no sign-off.\n\nCo-authored-by: Kai Hoffmann <fabian@callandor.gmbh>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqsF7/CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAsfAQAA54AcxP14pHcSrktTTXFFOY\nRqygtjSTSOcesL3JVsJNqzrK56M/aQu2Wwyszqoab7dFPG920aWxs7k6VaEJ/72q\nTOUcnYu09tWxnzbbfvYu9HBWWi/6AIT2v3wM/meqN8BPZgOJ2xLZRM5TgXsCluI6\n46pwKO96SUmYT0zSTz+e94MZCrPF8rIQ14YfDU+0zHMR5uJ+rXi1AaM5wjZGfKU0\n3+kno88KeJVxbNikVcUAKR6d3Kq+kQM+UmdhGL8y5zicCLJBEpK7Q6KShOWIYBGi\n38xDsZj3gkDq0I4Zph6tCNiLIHd0FkJ+HXjuPSgkV18oamHgzLWetS7+2H2fn5Ua\nK5w08p2m7itLOmPTnNioikOzdIOm6m0uxLUTj8MQ1lCP29Pptnyq7PkHWw39L++E\nx+T/DqP/m8MlhQK8O/BMq1RYT4lc3irc3dhRQi4tdov5yNExTnb/qn+jHdWlZoLd\nsOhRUIMQeSTK5KXkHpLamSpb8sHrVNkNaQ1QzDpEHCWisVHxpWF4tgMGnmmsto7D\nJ7A2d3tgXSjl8feHxm17tRcadE/tmRZedotDJ0SEMpxr2H36B5Lzymiwuig4Y6vE\nr63vjFh9j7CP/H2PvKjXpP4zGQbLbbduY9nv9hL8884CFieqBiZS/JnDTZdDoWMv\nRfIzaTZlp7wfy5F4oR+R\n=FWJZ\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-20T22:32:32Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/67a62fc78bf5dfd5f3b46a7d70b2805a9444c067","node_id":"C_kwDOSVv36NoAKDY3YTYyZmM3OGJmNWRmZDVmM2I0NmE3ZDcwYjI4MDVhOTQ0NGMwNjc","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/649d0729468c7c7fc175866013d67671efd49d43","sha":"649d0729468c7c7fc175866013d67671efd49d43","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/649d0729468c7c7fc175866013d67671efd49d43"}],"sha":"67a62fc78bf5dfd5f3b46a7d70b2805a9444c067","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/67a62fc78bf5dfd5f3b46a7d70b2805a9444c067"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/281068747?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs","id":281068747,"login":"wakir-labs","node_id":"U_kgDOEMDEyw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/649d0729468c7c7fc175866013d67671efd49d43/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-20T21:59:32Z","email":"xypu4r8e@addy.io","name":"wakir-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-20T21:59:32Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"chore(supply-chain): refresh python:3.13-slim digest pin after verified upstream rebuild (#545)\n\nUpstream re-pushed the `python:3.13-slim` tag on 2026-09-19, which turned\nthe non-required `digest-verify python:3.13-slim` check red on every PR\nopened since. This refreshes the pin at all three sites that carry it.\n\nLegitimacy of the new digest was established BEFORE the pin was moved\n(a refresh without that proof would be switching the gate off while\npretending to have used it):\n\n  * Live manifest-list digest resolves to\n    sha256:8d9d0b8b... via registry-1.docker.io; Docker Hub tag\n    metadata reports the same digest, pushed 2026-09-19T08:10:14Z by\n    `doijanky` — the standard Docker-Official-Images publisher account\n    (cross-checked against library/debian, library/alpine, library/node).\n  * Tag alias family intact: 3.13-slim == 3.13.15-slim ==\n    3.13-slim-trixie == 3.13.15-slim-trixie all resolve to the new\n    digest. No Python-version and no Debian-suite jump.\n  * CPython source unchanged: the image config declares\n    PYTHON_SHA256=1e66a794..., which is the verified sha256 of\n    Python-3.13.15.tar.xz as downloaded from python.org (HTTP 200,\n    23160540 bytes). GPG_KEY and PYTHON_VERSION unchanged.\n  * Build recipe unchanged: all 9 history entries of the old and new\n    amd64 configs are byte-identical except H0, the Debian rootfs\n    snapshot line, which moves from `trixie ''@1787529600''`\n    (2026-08-24) to `trixie ''@1789689600''` (2026-09-18). Env,\n    Cmd, Entrypoint, User, WorkingDir, Labels all unchanged; 4 layers\n    before and after.\n  * Upstream git agrees: docker-library/python master\n    3.13/slim-trixie/Dockerfile declares the same GPG_KEY,\n    PYTHON_VERSION and PYTHON_SHA256.\n\nVerdict: a routine Debian trixie base-rootfs refresh. All layer\ndigests differ because the rootfs moved and everything above it was\nrebuilt on top of it — which is expected, not suspicious.\n\nOld: sha256:9d2e5553305c7c7b0097999bb17187c69b921ccd6bc9d40e4bb5ebe652c00285\nNew: sha256:8d9d0b8bcf6506481eae4907c18f5e3e7902e629f5f6d684f9e7c32e85e3ddf0\n\nCo-authored-by: Kai Hoffmann (DevOps) <kai@wakir-labs.dev>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"9f050c5edf59f0a30ed2054bb22b4e4432ad9aaa","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/trees/9f050c5edf59f0a30ed2054bb22b4e4432ad9aaa"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/commits/649d0729468c7c7fc175866013d67671efd49d43","verification":{"payload":"tree 9f050c5edf59f0a30ed2054bb22b4e4432ad9aaa\nparent 5515058cd1e825d47d40b8491c73a0396927dc05\nauthor wakir-labs <xypu4r8e@addy.io> 1789941572 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789941572 +0200\n\nchore(supply-chain): refresh python:3.13-slim digest pin after verified upstream rebuild (#545)\n\nUpstream re-pushed the `python:3.13-slim` tag on 2026-09-19, which turned\nthe non-required `digest-verify python:3.13-slim` check red on every PR\nopened since. This refreshes the pin at all three sites that carry it.\n\nLegitimacy of the new digest was established BEFORE the pin was moved\n(a refresh without that proof would be switching the gate off while\npretending to have used it):\n\n  * Live manifest-list digest resolves to\n    sha256:8d9d0b8b... via registry-1.docker.io; Docker Hub tag\n    metadata reports the same digest, pushed 2026-09-19T08:10:14Z by\n    `doijanky` — the standard Docker-Official-Images publisher account\n    (cross-checked against library/debian, library/alpine, library/node).\n  * Tag alias family intact: 3.13-slim == 3.13.15-slim ==\n    3.13-slim-trixie == 3.13.15-slim-trixie all resolve to the new\n    digest. No Python-version and no Debian-suite jump.\n  * CPython source unchanged: the image config declares\n    PYTHON_SHA256=1e66a794..., which is the verified sha256 of\n    Python-3.13.15.tar.xz as downloaded from python.org (HTTP 200,\n    23160540 bytes). GPG_KEY and PYTHON_VERSION unchanged.\n  * Build recipe unchanged: all 9 history entries of the old and new\n    amd64 configs are byte-identical except H0, the Debian rootfs\n    snapshot line, which moves from `trixie ''@1787529600''`\n    (2026-08-24) to `trixie ''@1789689600''` (2026-09-18). Env,\n    Cmd, Entrypoint, User, WorkingDir, Labels all unchanged; 4 layers\n    before and after.\n  * Upstream git agrees: docker-library/python master\n    3.13/slim-trixie/Dockerfile declares the same GPG_KEY,\n    PYTHON_VERSION and PYTHON_SHA256.\n\nVerdict: a routine Debian trixie base-rootfs refresh. All layer\ndigests differ because the rootfs moved and everything above it was\nrebuilt on top of it — which is expected, not suspicious.\n\nOld: sha256:9d2e5553305c7c7b0097999bb17187c69b921ccd6bc9d40e4bb5ebe652c00285\nNew: sha256:8d9d0b8bcf6506481eae4907c18f5e3e7902e629f5f6d684f9e7c32e85e3ddf0\n\nCo-authored-by: Kai Hoffmann (DevOps) <kai@wakir-labs.dev>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqsFdECRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA+RkQAA6/CxyoEW9bvF4vUCMfwzg/\nzroO6H92zprfs7maGKH5neRofuCbCjSijwg1h0kIvRxTE/8HMd5kNyDNY7K/O6wZ\nSrtL1BZdx6MdnAf75f0ewnIhGDDaVfb0HfZBVFGOBCyELFCQ0FnRf8i/dTA3pBXJ\nCBHEWyoaOnAUEfqlU2PgHjnhSXZssrYrTn+rmuL5G2JE8EoxjrrSGO/D8ecujX3/\nqgSFhrYY2Zqxb4LcCcXhDJEo5HnM9c6ynFHmB8aadFyFqLXOuTA4dGvlp+kQfC93\n+lbOVLKR5yhGJH6wnJvLl7QuVkEInncy0+8jDv1Wtumao2yJproXWJqmcO13UYgv\nipAAfSRfPzj08mfPiBkkbYFX+it2mjKZk+mNC0szzFyyxT8bbqGzQQJ516xTdB/t\nix8qgCpSNMj1gLbqjU5v17BQm54xLdgyeb38TSEdEFP0kHYoXxrg5SnvO/W61DxS\n2tjT0AD+1VJjqpzqayTWiLCEezc9QyONInfwK5ON63pm2Y79XOS4LpxRxN4DOkaT\nWRPF3TK5pfd+two8M967pxeJfoQumyHum6PaywmNgTfnzpk0STMhxqIgUZzh8oOQ\nfPIK5G9NLFsoOBdecAiNH/VRHPXcmhOIGLYecMkb5UP137wH7KCXNNludVZDSksU\nPv7T7jbB/GmvDiOsWzm8\n=93ta\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-20T21:59:32Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/649d0729468c7c7fc175866013d67671efd49d43","node_id":"C_kwDOSVv36NoAKDY0OWQwNzI5NDY4YzdjN2ZjMTc1ODY2MDEzZDY3NjcxZWZkNDlkNDM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/5515058cd1e825d47d40b8491c73a0396927dc05","sha":"5515058cd1e825d47d40b8491c73a0396927dc05","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/5515058cd1e825d47d40b8491c73a0396927dc05"}],"sha":"649d0729468c7c7fc175866013d67671efd49d43","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/649d0729468c7c7fc175866013d67671efd49d43"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/281068747?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs","id":281068747,"login":"wakir-labs","node_id":"U_kgDOEMDEyw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/5515058cd1e825d47d40b8491c73a0396927dc05/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-20T21:43:56Z","email":"xypu4r8e@addy.io","name":"wakir-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-20T21:43:56Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Run the phase-3c-opt-in group for the first time, fix what it found, and retire the exemption (#544)\n\n* fix(phase-3c): restore the {welle} placeholders the archaeology rename broke\n\nPR #533 renamed \"Welle\" to \"wave\" across the test tree. The rename ran\nover f-string *placeholders* as well as prose: 21 expressions in\ntests/acceptance/phase_3c/_ac_assertions.py became `{wave}` while the\nfunction parameter stayed `welle`. Every one of them sits inside an\nassertion''s failure message, so the defect is invisible on the happy\npath and raises NameError the moment an acceptance criterion is\nviolated.\n\nMeasured on this branch, opt-in enabled for all three markers:\n\n  before  175 passed, 15 failed, 17 skipped\n  after   190 passed,  0 failed, 17 skipped\n\nAll 15 failures were the same root cause. They are the negative\ncontrols -- `*_drift_blocks`, `*_rejects`, `*_hc_ac_*` -- which feed a\nviolating record and expect `pytest.raises(AssertionError, match=\"AC-N\")`.\nNameError is not AssertionError, so the suite''s entire failure half was\ninoperative: an AC-1 violation at cutover time would have crashed the\ngate instead of naming the drifting records.\n\nThe suite is green at be93a51 (2026-05-18) and at c26fd25^ (2026-09-14),\nboth verified in a worktree. Nothing else in four months touched it.\n\nNo expectation was rewritten and no test was removed; the diff restores\nthe parameter name the messages always referred to.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(ci): lint undefined names in f-string placeholders\n\nNine of the 21 placeholders the rename broke were caught by a negative\ncontrol in the Phase-3c suite. The other twelve were not, and would not\nbe even with that suite in a lane: their negative controls are the 17\ntests skipped a second time inside the modules, pending perf gauges and\noperator-hand drills that do not exist yet. A test can only catch a\nbroken error message by provoking the message.\n\nSo a static check for the class. It resolves names the way CPython\nscopes them -- module globals, parameters, function-wide bindings,\ncomprehension targets, `except as`, `with as`, walrus, imports, nested\nfunction and lambda parameters, builtins -- and reports a Name load\ninside a FormattedValue that none of them bind.\n\nDeliberately narrow: placeholders only, not a general undefined-name\nlinter. A plain NameError fires the first time its code path runs; a\nplaceholder in an assertion message fires only on the path nobody\nexercises. That asymmetry is the whole reason this is worth a gate.\n\nRetro-verified: 21 findings against `979f3f9`''s copy of\n`_ac_assertions.py`, 0 against the same file at `c26fd25^`. Zero\nfindings across the tree at this commit; one module is skipped and\nnamed because `from x import *` defeats static resolution.\n\n`tests/ci/` is the whole-directory target of `lane-ci-meta`, so this\nruns without further wiring. The guard carries five negative controls,\nincluding the incident''s exact shape -- a lint whose only assertion is\n\"the tree is clean\" passes just as well when the checker is broken.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(ci): skip-gate — a suite that skips itself is not a green suite\n\n`pytest` exits 0 when every selected test is skipped. A lane built\naround a skip-by-default marker therefore reports the same green for\n\"the opt-in was taken and 190 tests passed\" and \"the opt-in quietly\nstopped working and nothing ran\". Renaming a flag in a conftest, or\ndropping one of three from an invocation, is enough.\n\nThat is not a hypothetical for the Phase-3c lane in the next commit: its\nwhole justification is that the opt-in had never been taken. A lane that\ncannot tell execution from selection would reproduce the problem it is\nmeant to end, one layer up.\n\n`skip_gate.py` reads the JUnit report and refuses a run in which any\ntest was skipped for a reason matching `--forbid-skip-reason` (used with\nthe opt-in wording, so it needs no maintenance as the suite grows), or\nin which fewer than `--min-executed` tests reached a verdict. Counts\ncome from the `<testcase>` elements, not the `<testsuite>` summary\nattributes -- this module exists because summaries can be true and empty\nat the same time, and one of its tests pins that.\n\nIt is the skip-side counterpart of `tooling/ci/collect_gate.py`, which\nclosed the import-guard half of the same class.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(lanes): retire the phase-3c-opt-in exemption and put its 207 tests in a lane\n\nThe exemption''s stated reason was that the opt-in had never been taken.\nThat reason is spent: on 2026-09-20 it was taken, and the run is the\nargument for this change rather than a formality.\n\n  before fix   175 passed, 15 failed, 17 skipped\n  after fix    190 passed,  0 failed, 17 skipped\n  cost         0.6 s, no podman, no NATS, no SSH, no network\n\nThe earlier objection was drill character, meaning cost and\ninfrastructure. The fixtures import hashlib, os, pytest, dataclasses and\ntyping; there is nothing to weigh. Against that: the first run found a\ndefect that had merged green six days earlier precisely because this\ngroup ran nowhere. Renewing on those facts would have required a reason\nthe run did not supply.\n\nWhat changes:\n\n* `lane-phase-3c-acceptance` in runtime-acceptance-gates.yml — all three\n  opt-ins as CLI flags rather than env-vars, because an unregistered flag\n  is an immediate pytest error while a misspelt env-var is silently\n  false. A dependency of the aggregator, in both `needs:` and\n  `GATE_RESULTS`; no new branch-protection context.\n* skip-gate after the run. `pytest` exits 0 when everything skips, so\n  without it the lane would report green in exactly the state it exists\n  to prevent. Verified both ways on this branch: clean with the flags,\n  two errors without them.\n* The 19 modules move `opt-in-marker` -> `required` via\n  `lane_inventory.py --sync`; the group is removed, and\n  `test_no_unused_exemption_groups` is what forced the removal rather\n  than leaving an orphan behind.\n* `phase_3c_lane_promotion` in lane_assignment.json records the\n  measurement, the defect, and what green here does not mean.\n\nWhat this does **not** claim: that the cutover of 2026-05-20 met\nAC-1..AC-5. The fixtures are placeholder oracles with hard-coded numbers\n(\"python_baseline_p95_ms = 3.5  # placeholder\"), and 17 of the 207 carry\na second, inner skip pending real perf gauges, a real SPIRE agent and\noperator-hand runbooks. Green means the acceptance assertions hold their\nshape, including their failure paths. The 17 have no expiry date and are\nreported as a follow-up for selin rather than dated here — QA owns the\nlane, not the substance behind those skips.\n\n`live-vm-operator`, the other exemption in the warning window, is\nuntouched.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ci): name rollback_drill/ explicitly so --rollback-drill is registered\n\nThe lane''s first run failed with \"unrecognized arguments:\n--rollback-drill\". `pytest` loads conftests for the paths on the command\nline before it parses arguments, and that flag is registered by\n`tests/acceptance/phase_3c/rollback_drill/conftest.py`, not by the\nparent. Naming the parent directory alone cannot accept it.\n\nBoth paths are now named; node IDs are de-duplicated, so the selection\nis still 207 (190 executed, 17 inner-skipped), verified locally.\n\nMy error, and one the local runs could not have caught: I verified the\nsuite with the `WAKIR_PHASE_3C_*` env-vars and wired the job with the\nCLI flags. Worth recording rather than squashing away, because it is the\nargument for the flags in the first place -- the wrong invocation came\nback in 0.2 s instead of skipping 36 rollback drills in silence, and the\nskip-gate caught the missing report behind it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"84c29b4e3d2a6fcd3bac9a0f8e5f38e89439d1ce","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/trees/84c29b4e3d2a6fcd3bac9a0f8e5f38e89439d1ce"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/commits/5515058cd1e825d47d40b8491c73a0396927dc05","verification":{"payload":"tree 84c29b4e3d2a6fcd3bac9a0f8e5f38e89439d1ce\nparent 979f3f98b121376b59d39b76d1c8fac137bed39e\nauthor wakir-labs <xypu4r8e@addy.io> 1789940636 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789940636 +0200\n\nRun the phase-3c-opt-in group for the first time, fix what it found, and retire the exemption (#544)\n\n* fix(phase-3c): restore the {welle} placeholders the archaeology rename broke\n\nPR #533 renamed \"Welle\" to \"wave\" across the test tree. The rename ran\nover f-string *placeholders* as well as prose: 21 expressions in\ntests/acceptance/phase_3c/_ac_assertions.py became `{wave}` while the\nfunction parameter stayed `welle`. Every one of them sits inside an\nassertion''s failure message, so the defect is invisible on the happy\npath and raises NameError the moment an acceptance criterion is\nviolated.\n\nMeasured on this branch, opt-in enabled for all three markers:\n\n  before  175 passed, 15 failed, 17 skipped\n  after   190 passed,  0 failed, 17 skipped\n\nAll 15 failures were the same root cause. They are the negative\ncontrols -- `*_drift_blocks`, `*_rejects`, `*_hc_ac_*` -- which feed a\nviolating record and expect `pytest.raises(AssertionError, match=\"AC-N\")`.\nNameError is not AssertionError, so the suite''s entire failure half was\ninoperative: an AC-1 violation at cutover time would have crashed the\ngate instead of naming the drifting records.\n\nThe suite is green at be93a51 (2026-05-18) and at c26fd25^ (2026-09-14),\nboth verified in a worktree. Nothing else in four months touched it.\n\nNo expectation was rewritten and no test was removed; the diff restores\nthe parameter name the messages always referred to.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(ci): lint undefined names in f-string placeholders\n\nNine of the 21 placeholders the rename broke were caught by a negative\ncontrol in the Phase-3c suite. The other twelve were not, and would not\nbe even with that suite in a lane: their negative controls are the 17\ntests skipped a second time inside the modules, pending perf gauges and\noperator-hand drills that do not exist yet. A test can only catch a\nbroken error message by provoking the message.\n\nSo a static check for the class. It resolves names the way CPython\nscopes them -- module globals, parameters, function-wide bindings,\ncomprehension targets, `except as`, `with as`, walrus, imports, nested\nfunction and lambda parameters, builtins -- and reports a Name load\ninside a FormattedValue that none of them bind.\n\nDeliberately narrow: placeholders only, not a general undefined-name\nlinter. A plain NameError fires the first time its code path runs; a\nplaceholder in an assertion message fires only on the path nobody\nexercises. That asymmetry is the whole reason this is worth a gate.\n\nRetro-verified: 21 findings against `979f3f9`''s copy of\n`_ac_assertions.py`, 0 against the same file at `c26fd25^`. Zero\nfindings across the tree at this commit; one module is skipped and\nnamed because `from x import *` defeats static resolution.\n\n`tests/ci/` is the whole-directory target of `lane-ci-meta`, so this\nruns without further wiring. The guard carries five negative controls,\nincluding the incident''s exact shape -- a lint whose only assertion is\n\"the tree is clean\" passes just as well when the checker is broken.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(ci): skip-gate — a suite that skips itself is not a green suite\n\n`pytest` exits 0 when every selected test is skipped. A lane built\naround a skip-by-default marker therefore reports the same green for\n\"the opt-in was taken and 190 tests passed\" and \"the opt-in quietly\nstopped working and nothing ran\". Renaming a flag in a conftest, or\ndropping one of three from an invocation, is enough.\n\nThat is not a hypothetical for the Phase-3c lane in the next commit: its\nwhole justification is that the opt-in had never been taken. A lane that\ncannot tell execution from selection would reproduce the problem it is\nmeant to end, one layer up.\n\n`skip_gate.py` reads the JUnit report and refuses a run in which any\ntest was skipped for a reason matching `--forbid-skip-reason` (used with\nthe opt-in wording, so it needs no maintenance as the suite grows), or\nin which fewer than `--min-executed` tests reached a verdict. Counts\ncome from the `<testcase>` elements, not the `<testsuite>` summary\nattributes -- this module exists because summaries can be true and empty\nat the same time, and one of its tests pins that.\n\nIt is the skip-side counterpart of `tooling/ci/collect_gate.py`, which\nclosed the import-guard half of the same class.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(lanes): retire the phase-3c-opt-in exemption and put its 207 tests in a lane\n\nThe exemption''s stated reason was that the opt-in had never been taken.\nThat reason is spent: on 2026-09-20 it was taken, and the run is the\nargument for this change rather than a formality.\n\n  before fix   175 passed, 15 failed, 17 skipped\n  after fix    190 passed,  0 failed, 17 skipped\n  cost         0.6 s, no podman, no NATS, no SSH, no network\n\nThe earlier objection was drill character, meaning cost and\ninfrastructure. The fixtures import hashlib, os, pytest, dataclasses and\ntyping; there is nothing to weigh. Against that: the first run found a\ndefect that had merged green six days earlier precisely because this\ngroup ran nowhere. Renewing on those facts would have required a reason\nthe run did not supply.\n\nWhat changes:\n\n* `lane-phase-3c-acceptance` in runtime-acceptance-gates.yml — all three\n  opt-ins as CLI flags rather than env-vars, because an unregistered flag\n  is an immediate pytest error while a misspelt env-var is silently\n  false. A dependency of the aggregator, in both `needs:` and\n  `GATE_RESULTS`; no new branch-protection context.\n* skip-gate after the run. `pytest` exits 0 when everything skips, so\n  without it the lane would report green in exactly the state it exists\n  to prevent. Verified both ways on this branch: clean with the flags,\n  two errors without them.\n* The 19 modules move `opt-in-marker` -> `required` via\n  `lane_inventory.py --sync`; the group is removed, and\n  `test_no_unused_exemption_groups` is what forced the removal rather\n  than leaving an orphan behind.\n* `phase_3c_lane_promotion` in lane_assignment.json records the\n  measurement, the defect, and what green here does not mean.\n\nWhat this does **not** claim: that the cutover of 2026-05-20 met\nAC-1..AC-5. The fixtures are placeholder oracles with hard-coded numbers\n(\"python_baseline_p95_ms = 3.5  # placeholder\"), and 17 of the 207 carry\na second, inner skip pending real perf gauges, a real SPIRE agent and\noperator-hand runbooks. Green means the acceptance assertions hold their\nshape, including their failure paths. The 17 have no expiry date and are\nreported as a follow-up for selin rather than dated here — QA owns the\nlane, not the substance behind those skips.\n\n`live-vm-operator`, the other exemption in the warning window, is\nuntouched.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ci): name rollback_drill/ explicitly so --rollback-drill is registered\n\nThe lane''s first run failed with \"unrecognized arguments:\n--rollback-drill\". `pytest` loads conftests for the paths on the command\nline before it parses arguments, and that flag is registered by\n`tests/acceptance/phase_3c/rollback_drill/conftest.py`, not by the\nparent. Naming the parent directory alone cannot accept it.\n\nBoth paths are now named; node IDs are de-duplicated, so the selection\nis still 207 (190 executed, 17 inner-skipped), verified locally.\n\nMy error, and one the local runs could not have caught: I verified the\nsuite with the `WAKIR_PHASE_3C_*` env-vars and wired the job with the\nCLI flags. Worth recording rather than squashing away, because it is the\nargument for the flags in the first place -- the wrong invocation came\nback in 0.2 s instead of skipping 36 rollback drills in silence, and the\nskip-gate caught the missing report behind it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqsFOcCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA8NkQAC6kCAcyKyzg2BTVshywdnIt\nmuuuVk+dl4MEQJRYKCUm7lWg0TF4H5+Fwsppin7q1Xlh08jpN3mb1hqrPfrJI/97\n5TQSqekdCI25gGEFPoK9N+MIOqWvtOk0QopmfPjv9OFmsPiq0/iZKJiyJU50skel\nR08qGZJJLugjTkBzB0/uzf3Oh+dY4ffvXZPFWCC2TwA5AP1Rt6sCC83DV4gMkUqM\nSgDSwM4od2xVioItp82n0oCKZcqmQh0pyB76rTzwR7aIzxrtwFb2kKsPmRmB7D8u\nNRuDqaB7NbzwYZeGHKJHvxFlrL1lXQha3Z9prLoR4qxEI2LoLZjRItBxMdVteRAn\ns1TOHIq/6HwVtSCnzVHKAC+dHvvYh9Zq5BBjTe1CvAKXuiNrZSFYe469dhKyagcc\nca1PVGHggOJlbuTt87aAueQS+T+nAqotoL/gbuzv50fdctHWREtgSM5P7B9QIdGt\nQW5rQmbfPC3CH1qBQrke+QV9UR0dXbAY0LKSmpXt5mzpoKkVtYabs0DrEpkaQ4vu\n8GRattQH/7BpyyAM0FZZlXcr3ksIf6976b13r5NFooCO2enu6fZHI35SuY4SmfVF\nNwcrWcVmMvcARRlULF+r5uMmkPQiv4CyTHSAEEJ2Xgvqhom7nIy3QFRafkqWFG//\nyWK3gAxRD2AoIxeIgZCX\n=rlMX\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-20T21:43:57Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/5515058cd1e825d47d40b8491c73a0396927dc05","node_id":"C_kwDOSVv36NoAKDU1MTUwNThjZDFlODI1ZDQ3ZDQwYjg0OTFjNzNhMDM5NjkyN2RjMDU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/979f3f98b121376b59d39b76d1c8fac137bed39e","sha":"979f3f98b121376b59d39b76d1c8fac137bed39e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/979f3f98b121376b59d39b76d1c8fac137bed39e"}],"sha":"5515058cd1e825d47d40b8491c73a0396927dc05","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/5515058cd1e825d47d40b8491c73a0396927dc05"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/281068747?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs","id":281068747,"login":"wakir-labs","node_id":"U_kgDOEMDEyw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/979f3f98b121376b59d39b76d1c8fac137bed39e/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-15T00:47:59Z","email":"xypu4r8e@addy.io","name":"wakir-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-15T00:47:59Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Retire the cross-workflow aggregator (ADR-0075 §1), add the evidence-citation lint, and close Zone 3 on the spec side (#543)\n\n* ci: retire the cross-workflow aggregator instead of completing ADR-0068\n\nADR-0075 §1 (approved 2026-09-15) supersedes ADR-0068 (approved\n2026-05-18). ADR-0068 required exactly one status context named\n`ci-aggregator`. This is not a silent deletion, and the reason belongs\nin the release note rather than only in the ADR:\n\n  The workflow ran 865 times, was always green, and never blocked\n  anything. It was not one of the thirteen required contexts on `main`,\n  and it polled 6 of those 13 over the Actions API. Its own\n  documentation said \"the aggregator is *the* Required-Status\".\n\nExecuting ADR-0068 today would have traded thirteen enforcement points\nfor one that had never been on the critical path. Finding R6 of the\nexternal re-review is the empirical argument against the pattern\nitself: a single collecting context over everything is the largest\npossible fail-open surface, and polling results instead of inheriting\nthem via `needs:` makes it larger. The binding pattern is aggregation\ninside one workflow — `if: always()` plus an explicit `needs.*.result`\ncheck, as `runtime-acceptance-gates.yml` has carried since 2026-09-14.\n\nRemoved: `.github/workflows/ci-aggregator.yml`,\n`scripts/ci/ci_aggregator.py`, `docs/ci/aggregator-workflow.md`, and\n`tests/ci/test_ci_aggregator_workflow.py`. The test goes in the same\ncommit as its subject: Box A had just promoted it into a mandatory\nlane, where it pins `ci-aggregator.yml`, so leaving it behind would\nhave turned the CI-meta lane red — correctly.\n\n`EXPECTED_CHECK_COUNT` stays at 13 and gains the comment ADR-0075 asks\nfor. The constant measures the status quo, not a decision; it was\ncopied from a live branch-protection read. That is why this verifier\nwas green for the four months in which the live rule contradicted an\napproved ADR — a tool whose constant comes from the status quo can only\ndetect drift away from the status quo, never a violation of a decision.\n\nTwo things deliberately not done, and reported instead:\n\n  * `tests/observability/test_aggregator_failure_rate_tracker.py` had a\n    `pytest.skip(\"ci_aggregator.py not present in checkout\")` guard,\n    which this commit would have fired — a test going quiet at the\n    moment its subject disappears, reporting green for the wrong\n    reason, in the same PR that retires a decision for exactly that\n    defect class. The check is re-pointed, not deleted: the six legacy\n    names must still be live required contexts, read from\n    `lane_assignment.json`, with no skip path.\n  * `scripts/ci/adr-0068-migration-step-3-cutover.py` and\n    `scripts/observability/aggregator-failure-rate-tracker.py` stay,\n    with banners. Removing them means deleting their test modules, and\n    this PR removes exactly one test module — the one whose subject it\n    deletes. The cutover script would now PATCH branch protection down\n    to a context that can never report, so the banner is a safety\n    catch, not decoration. Follow-up, separate owner.\n\n`docs/ci/retired-workflows.md` is the new register: a mentioned\nworkflow either exists or is registered there with the change that\nremoved it. Three historical entries plus this one.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(ci): lint the evidence our two trust documents cite\n\nCTO decision paper 2026-09-14 §2. Not the lint that was proposed\nfirst: \"every workflow filename a document mentions must exist\" was\nmeasured against `main` and produced three false positives, all of them\ncareful historical prose — one of them a sentence whose entire point\nwas that the workflow is gone. A lint that is wrong about correct\nwriting gets switched off inside a fortnight, and that one would have\ncaught two of the four PR-#540 defects.\n\nThis one checks evidence instead:\n\n  1. A cited test module exists, has an entry in lane_assignment.json,\n     and that entry''s profile is one that is actually executed.\n  2. A cited workflow exists under .github/workflows/, or is registered\n     in docs/ci/retired-workflows.md with the change that removed it.\n     Registration is not amnesty — a registered workflow that exists\n     again fails too, or the register slowly becomes a suppression list.\n  3. Any other cited repository path exists.\n\nMy own measurement, not an inherited number. Read 2026-09-15 on the\nBox-A merge commit: seven test-module citations, seven lane entries,\nall `required`. The paper''s \"five of seven red\" and the number in my\nown assignment are both true of `441c9d6` and both stale — Box A healed\nevery one of them. The lint therefore lands green on rule 1 rather than\nred, which is the outcome the sequencing was designed for.\n\nIt does not land green on rule 3. Two citations of\n`docs/wat-leaf-projection.md` (STABILITY.md:66, layers.md:113) point at\na path that has never existed in the tree; the spec is at\n`wirelang/specs/wat-leaf-projection.md`. Both corrected here. That is\nthe lint''s first real finding, and it is the same defect class in a\nthird sub-species: not a deleted workflow, not a dead test — a\nspecification cited by a path nobody ever checked.\n\nNegative controls, because a lint with an untested failure path is a\ncomment: every rule has a red case, an unknown lane profile is an error\nrather than a default-pass, prose that merely looks like a path is not\nlinted, and the extractor is pinned against going vacuously quiet.\n\nThe proof that it would have caught the errors it was commissioned for\nis the last block: the three generations of STABILITY.md line 82,\nreplayed verbatim from git history. Pre-#540 credits a deleted workflow\n(rule 2, red). #540 replaces it with a test module no workflow ran\n(rule 1, red) — the correction that stayed inside the error class and\nonly changed sub-species. After Box A, green.\n\nStated in the module rather than left to be assumed: this lint is\nhermetic and cannot see run counts. The other two PR-#540 defects were\nof that kind — a workflow that exists and has had zero runs since May,\nand a dispatch leg never dispatched. Only the Actions API knows that,\nand the counterpart in the tree is the `execution_evidence` block on\nevery standing exemption.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* spec(wat): close Zone 3 on the specification side\n\nCTO decision paper §3/L4. Producer and consumer were closed on\n2026-09-14 — the bridge audit writer defaults to `NO_CAPABILITY_DIGEST`\nand `aggregator_cli._validate_events` enforces `^[0-9a-f]{64}$`. The\nnormative specification was not, and nothing compared the two. Anyone\nimplementing `wirelang/specs/wat-leaf-projection.md` §3.4 faithfully\nbuilt a spool record our own pipeline rejects.\n\nChanged, all of it catching the documents up to a decision already\ntaken (ADR-0072 W4), none of it a new rule:\n\n* §2 tuple table, §3.4 rule list, §4.2 example, §5 edge-case table —\n  the all-zero digest, with a new §3.4.0 that records what the rule was,\n  why it changed, and which code enforces it.\n* Two further instances I found while checking the first, same defect\n  class, one layer down each: `docs/wat-spool-spec.md` (\"<64-char hex\n  or empty>\" — the spool is the sole input of the step that rejects it)\n  and `docs/wat-manifest-spec.md` (\"empty string allowed for\n  non-capability events\" — contradicting the canonical schema this very\n  document describes). Both corrected; both were reachable from the\n  same reading and would otherwise have been the next finding.\n* `layers.md` line 227 said the empty string \"passes through verbatim\".\n  True at the spool layer and misleading one step later, where the same\n  value is refused. Now says both halves.\n* `compute_leaf_hash` docstring: permissive here, strict one layer up,\n  and why that is deliberate rather than leftover.\n\nWhat did not change, on purpose: the hash primitive and\n`tests/fixtures/jcs-leaf-vectors/vector-3-no-capability-token.json`,\nwhich keeps the empty string and its recorded hash. Leaves anchored\nunder the earlier rule have to stay verifiable forever. Tightening the\nprimitive to match the manifest domain would be the opposite mistake\nand cost more than the original one, so there is a test against that\nover-correction too.\n\nThe guard Priya asked for, built to be as cheap as she described: the\nZone-3 module now parses the values out of the spec''s own §4.1/§4.2/§5\ntables — the rendered tables, the same thing a human implementer reads\n— and sends them through the real `_validate_events`. It does not\nrestate the rule, it executes whatever the file currently prints.\nNegative control run against the real file: reverting §4.2 and §5 to\n`\"\"` turns three of the new tests red.\n\nNot touched, per Priya''s correction to my own follow-up: the Rust\nsibling. `persona-engine-bridge-audit-writer` mirrors the envelope\n(11 keys, no `capability_token_hash`), not the leaf projection. That\nfollow-up is a missing surface, not a divergence, and it is not this PR.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ci: drop the stale pointer to the deleted aggregator script\n\n`runtime-acceptance-gates.yml` told a future maintainer to update\n`scripts/ci/ci_aggregator.py` when renaming the required job. That file\nno longer exists as of the first commit in this PR, and an instruction\npointing at a deleted file is how the next reader learns to ignore the\ncomment block that also contains the rename prohibition.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Tomás Reinhart <fabian@callandor.gmbh>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"52c0b177e3fb90105606a6bb6656f4906e407c91","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/trees/52c0b177e3fb90105606a6bb6656f4906e407c91"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/commits/979f3f98b121376b59d39b76d1c8fac137bed39e","verification":{"payload":"tree 52c0b177e3fb90105606a6bb6656f4906e407c91\nparent 0ac72ddba6cad388763395363550e9093e941b17\nauthor wakir-labs <xypu4r8e@addy.io> 1789433279 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789433279 +0200\n\nRetire the cross-workflow aggregator (ADR-0075 §1), add the ev