r a violation of a decision.\n\nTwo things deliberately not done, and reported instead:\n\n  * `tests/observability/test_aggregator_failure_rate_tracker.py` had a\n    `pytest.skip(\"ci_aggregator.py not present in checkout\")` guard,\n    which this commit would have fired — a test going quiet at the\n    moment its subject disappears, reporting green for the wrong\n    reason, in the same PR that retires a decision for exactly that\n    defect class. The check is re-pointed, not deleted: the six legacy\n    names must still be live required contexts, read from\n    `lane_assignment.json`, with no skip path.\n  * `scripts/ci/adr-0068-migration-step-3-cutover.py` and\n    `scripts/observability/aggregator-failure-rate-tracker.py` stay,\n    with banners. Removing them means deleting their test modules, and\n    this PR removes exactly one test module — the one whose subject it\n    deletes. The cutover script would now PATCH branch protection down\n    to a context that can never report, so the banner is a safety\n    catch, not decoration. Follow-up, separate owner.\n\n`docs/ci/retired-workflows.md` is the new register: a mentioned\nworkflow either exists or is registered there with the change that\nremoved it. Three historical entries plus this one.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(ci): lint the evidence our two trust documents cite\n\nCTO decision paper 2026-09-14 §2. Not the lint that was proposed\nfirst: \"every workflow filename a document mentions must exist\" was\nmeasured against `main` and produced three false positives, all of them\ncareful historical prose — one of them a sentence whose entire point\nwas that the workflow is gone. A lint that is wrong about correct\nwriting gets switched off inside a fortnight, and that one would have\ncaught two of the four PR-#540 defects.\n\nThis one checks evidence instead:\n\n  1. A cited test module exists, has an entry in lane_assignment.json,\n     and that entry''s profile is one that is actually executed.\n  2. A cited workflow exists under .github/workflows/, or is registered\n     in docs/ci/retired-workflows.md with the change that removed it.\n     Registration is not amnesty — a registered workflow that exists\n     again fails too, or the register slowly becomes a suppression list.\n  3. Any other cited repository path exists.\n\nMy own measurement, not an inherited number. Read 2026-09-15 on the\nBox-A merge commit: seven test-module citations, seven lane entries,\nall `required`. The paper''s \"five of seven red\" and the number in my\nown assignment are both true of `441c9d6` and both stale — Box A healed\nevery one of them. The lint therefore lands green on rule 1 rather than\nred, which is the outcome the sequencing was designed for.\n\nIt does not land green on rule 3. Two citations of\n`docs/wat-leaf-projection.md` (STABILITY.md:66, layers.md:113) point at\na path that has never existed in the tree; the spec is at\n`wirelang/specs/wat-leaf-projection.md`. Both corrected here. That is\nthe lint''s first real finding, and it is the same defect class in a\nthird sub-species: not a deleted workflow, not a dead test — a\nspecification cited by a path nobody ever checked.\n\nNegative controls, because a lint with an untested failure path is a\ncomment: every rule has a red case, an unknown lane profile is an error\nrather than a default-pass, prose that merely looks like a path is not\nlinted, and the extractor is pinned against going vacuously quiet.\n\nThe proof that it would have caught the errors it was commissioned for\nis the last block: the three generations of STABILITY.md line 82,\nreplayed verbatim from git history. Pre-#540 credits a deleted workflow\n(rule 2, red). #540 replaces it with a test module no workflow ran\n(rule 1, red) — the correction that stayed inside the error class and\nonly changed sub-species. After Box A, green.\n\nStated in the module rather than left to be assumed: this lint is\nhermetic and cannot see run counts. The other two PR-#540 defects were\nof that kind — a workflow that exists and has had zero runs since May,\nand a dispatch leg never dispatched. Only the Actions API knows that,\nand the counterpart in the tree is the `execution_evidence` block on\nevery standing exemption.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* spec(wat): close Zone 3 on the specification side\n\nCTO decision paper §3/L4. Producer and consumer were closed on\n2026-09-14 — the bridge audit writer defaults to `NO_CAPABILITY_DIGEST`\nand `aggregator_cli._validate_events` enforces `^[0-9a-f]{64}$`. The\nnormative specification was not, and nothing compared the two. Anyone\nimplementing `wirelang/specs/wat-leaf-projection.md` §3.4 faithfully\nbuilt a spool record our own pipeline rejects.\n\nChanged, all of it catching the documents up to a decision already\ntaken (ADR-0072 W4), none of it a new rule:\n\n* §2 tuple table, §3.4 rule list, §4.2 example, §5 edge-case table —\n  the all-zero digest, with a new §3.4.0 that records what the rule was,\n  why it changed, and which code enforces it.\n* Two further instances I found while checking the first, same defect\n  class, one layer down each: `docs/wat-spool-spec.md` (\"<64-char hex\n  or empty>\" — the spool is the sole input of the step that rejects it)\n  and `docs/wat-manifest-spec.md` (\"empty string allowed for\n  non-capability events\" — contradicting the canonical schema this very\n  document describes). Both corrected; both were reachable from the\n  same reading and would otherwise have been the next finding.\n* `layers.md` line 227 said the empty string \"passes through verbatim\".\n  True at the spool layer and misleading one step later, where the same\n  value is refused. Now says both halves.\n* `compute_leaf_hash` docstring: permissive here, strict one layer up,\n  and why that is deliberate rather than leftover.\n\nWhat did not change, on purpose: the hash primitive and\n`tests/fixtures/jcs-leaf-vectors/vector-3-no-capability-token.json`,\nwhich keeps the empty string and its recorded hash. Leaves anchored\nunder the earlier rule have to stay verifiable forever. Tightening the\nprimitive to match the manifest domain would be the opposite mistake\nand cost more than the original one, so there is a test against that\nover-correction too.\n\nThe guard Priya asked for, built to be as cheap as she described: the\nZone-3 module now parses the values out of the spec''s own §4.1/§4.2/§5\ntables — the rendered tables, the same thing a human implementer reads\n— and sends them through the real `_validate_events`. It does not\nrestate the rule, it executes whatever the file currently prints.\nNegative control run against the real file: reverting §4.2 and §5 to\n`\"\"` turns three of the new tests red.\n\nNot touched, per Priya''s correction to my own follow-up: the Rust\nsibling. `persona-engine-bridge-audit-writer` mirrors the envelope\n(11 keys, no `capability_token_hash`), not the leaf projection. That\nfollow-up is a missing surface, not a divergence, and it is not this PR.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ci: drop the stale pointer to the deleted aggregator script\n\n`runtime-acceptance-gates.yml` told a future maintainer to update\n`scripts/ci/ci_aggregator.py` when renaming the required job. That file\nno longer exists as of the first commit in this PR, and an instruction\npointing at a deleted file is how the next reader learns to ignore the\ncomment block that also contains the rename prohibition.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Tomás Reinhart <fabian@callandor.gmbh>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqJW/CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAvbYQAJVdEyjue9urq3gm08QflhFg\nk348tAVSAzTrpxmNGXC4WXGPXx2UsNu0kAHTBjZ8uh+y4mo0rJ5dT1Fy3D8JOm05\nr5r0kGhkE0gu41vxwpt7iR472YhpBQiyBhHIYq0MQh2GHMMUDKDmE+E7j04YH/JY\nASW7VCj/GvZbODWIo91q3Wm9fX1McxgHcSyvU3BDj3eWRnQ7M5iLnaxQ/SRcpmIE\nxR3G8Vzd5Zij/cjDrdBNxXMKznugspaFA6CBY8gnsncyp+8VtY2XLfr8lK+vIfaV\n6yeqPC3mrM9Vfzxbd35b1U6+F9hsF4PrXW7xiO60fQi0IZDgrBtd4jIzDEz7UpHX\nEfrj9aiKC/mBy0tg4i3S1kJq04CGJ4kOrThtMMrmv52P+nTC2Q8/WxolDSpPMCrk\nRgYmJ43Mc+AfEeu1nxhIMwlezTh7UCSWhiLR8/kCNgljndd4xERZveb4sjuFGezx\niO5E1LYl8A30+w3buD1/2NdSUQ/sQsYng7Mkrp5PC6A+ech0Y7H7O/HheYkKJ2lG\nlDeU3xl8zL4kcdHl2zocalIHqtowWJZAECsOHw4jtXUyOw6rPLdw0iKebEttpY4N\n7g/10HtAC9G4+VVt33EhYsl1Oo4jDmUtyKH/DITM1/oKAtgH2nPAr6DSbfPOnpSY\nCaEhRqaHKIe7U0ZO62IK\n=41eb\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-15T00:48:00Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/979f3f98b121376b59d39b76d1c8fac137bed39e","node_id":"C_kwDOSVv36NoAKDk3OWYzZjk4YjEyMTM3NmI1OWQzOWI3NmQxYzhmYWMxMzdiZWQzOWU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/0ac72ddba6cad388763395363550e9093e941b17","sha":"0ac72ddba6cad388763395363550e9093e941b17","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/0ac72ddba6cad388763395363550e9093e941b17"}],"sha":"979f3f98b121376b59d39b76d1c8fac137bed39e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/979f3f98b121376b59d39b76d1c8fac137bed39e"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/281068747?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs","id":281068747,"login":"wakir-labs","node_id":"U_kgDOEMDEyw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/0ac72ddba6cad388763395363550e9093e941b17/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-15T00:17:27Z","email":"xypu4r8e@addy.io","name":"wakir-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-15T00:17:27Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"test(lanes): promote 97 modules into enforced lanes, add an observation lane, and give exemption dates teeth (ADR-0075) (#542)\n\n* test(lanes): run the fail-open guard before anything depends on it\n\n`tests/workflows/` — five hermetic structure suites over\n`.github/workflows/*.yml`, 177 tests, 1.9 s — ran in no workflow at all.\nAmong them is `test_required_context_error_propagation.py`, the guard\nthat asserts a required status context built on `needs:` cannot fail\nopen: it must carry `if: always()` and must turn every\n`needs.<job>.result` into its own exit code in its first step. That\nguard was added in the same commit that fixed finding R6 and has never\nexecuted.\n\nIt is wired into `production-suite` in `tests.yml`, whose display name\n`wirelang suite with rfc8785 + jsonschema` is already a branch-\nprotection context. No new required context, no branch-protection\nchange.\n\nTwo reasons for that lane rather than a new gate job under\n`runtime-acceptance-gates.yml`:\n\n* Ordering. The next commit extends the acceptance-gate aggregator with\n  new `needs:` entries. A guard that only goes live *through* that\n  change cannot have checked it. Attaching it to an independent,\n  already-required lane first makes the aggregator extension the first\n  edit the guard covers.\n* Dependencies. The guard opens with `pytest.importorskip(\"yaml\")`.\n  `pip install -e . pytest` does not pull PyYAML (it sits behind the\n  `persona` extra), so in a fresh gate job the module would collect zero\n  tests and report green without checking anything — the exact defect\n  class it exists to catch. This lane installs PyYAML explicitly.\n\nThe five modules move from `unassigned` to `required` in\n`tests/lanes/lane_assignment.json`; the `workflow-shape` exemption group\nis gone because it no longer describes anything.\n\nRefs: ADR-0075, CTO decision paper 2026-09-14 §1.3, §1.7, §4.2 step 1.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(lanes): attach the CI-meta suite to the acceptance-gate aggregator\n\nStep 2 of the Welle-3 lane promotion. `tests/ci/` — the hermetic tests\nabout the CI substrate itself — becomes a gate job under\n`runtime-acceptance-gates.yml` and is pulled into the aggregator''s\n`needs:` and its `GATE_RESULTS` result check in the same edit.\n\nAmong the modules that now run: the branch-protection check-name audit\n(the PR #102 forever-pending class) and `test_validate_demo_proof_report.py`,\nthe test module of the R1 report validator that `STABILITY.md` cites as\nevidence. Two path-filtered probes that were `optional-ci` are promoted\nwith them — both hermetic, both already on the PR surface, and a path\nfilter is not enforcement.\n\nNo new required status context and no branch-protection change: the\naggregator''s display name stays the only context of this lane, and the\ngate job inherits its enforcement.\n\n`tooling/ci/lane_inventory.py` learns that inheritance, because\notherwise the assignment file would have to call these modules\n`optional-ci` while they block merges — a measuring tool taking its\nconstant from the status quo instead of the decision, which is the class\nADR-0075 is about. The inheritance is granted only while all three\nhalves of the propagation hold: the job is in `needs:`, the aggregator\ncarries job-level `if: always()`, and its first unconditional step turns\n`needs.<job>.result` into an exit code.\n\nThat makes the omission of a `GATE_RESULTS` entry visible twice, from\ntwo directions. Negative control, run before this commit (entry removed\nfrom `GATE_RESULTS` only, job left in `needs:`):\n\n  * tests/workflows/test_required_context_error_propagation.py\n    ::test_required_context_checks_every_dependency_result_first — FAILED\n  * tests/lanes/test_required_inheritance.py\n    ::test_the_live_acceptance_gate_aggregator_propagates_to_every_gate_job — FAILED\n  * tests/lanes/test_lane_assignment.py::test_declared_profile_matches_workflows\n    — FAILED (the 11 modules fall back to optional-ci)\n\nThe first of those three is the guard that step 1 made live. It was\nalready running when this commit was written, which is the whole reason\nstep 1 came first.\n\nThe lane also runs `collect_gate.py --profile production --root tests/ci`\nbefore pytest, so a module-level import guard cannot make a module\ndisappear into a green run.\n\nRefs: ADR-0075 §1, CTO decision paper 2026-09-14 §1.3, §1.7, §4.2 step 2.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(lanes): promote WAT core, spec freeze, parity, routing — and make the four-validator claim true\n\nStep 3 of the Welle-3 lane promotion (ADR-0075; CTO decision paper\n§1.3 table A, §3/L2). Three additions, all attached to existing\nenforcement rather than registered as new required contexts.\n\n`lane-wat-core` (46 modules, ~20 s)\n  Merkle-root construction and proof verification, manifest v1/v2\n  signature and schema admission, OTS full-verify, cross-module anchor\n  pin consistency — the proof path Phase 4 exists to seal, running in no\n  workflow until now.\n\n`lane-hermetic-invariants` (36 modules, ~4 s)\n  Spec-freeze strict-superset property (the required freeze-seal probe\n  checks the seal envelope, not the property), six Python/Rust parity\n  suites that sit under `tests/` instead of `wirelang/` and were\n  therefore outside the required suite, twenty alert-routing modules\n  (the receiver-catalog completeness class — the outage we actually\n  had), the bug-42 regression suite, and the five hermetic counterparts\n  of the shell-only digest-pin context.\n\n`proof-path`\n  `tests/scripts/test_demo_proof.py` joins the context that already\n  executes the driver it tests. Placed there and not in a generic\n  hermetic lane for a measured reason: two of its tests skip without\n  `wakir_verify` importable, and this is the only job in the repository\n  that installs it.\n\nThe Node question (finding L2), decided\n---------------------------------------\n\n`STABILITY.md` claims two independent JSON-Schema implementations\nvalidate our published schemas. Measured: no workflow ran the guard\nmodule, and no workflow installed Node at all — the claim had been\nexecuted zero times. Putting the module in a lane without Node would\nhave left it that way, with a green tick on top.\n\nDecision: install Node in the lane. Measured before deciding — with\nnode + ajv + hyperjump + fastjsonschema present, the module runs 15\ntests, 0 skipped, all passing. The claim is true; it had simply never\nbeen checked.\n\nTwo consequences carried deliberately:\n\n* `package-lock.json` for both harnesses is now committed and the\n  `.gitignore` lines that excluded them are removed. `npm ci` installs\n  the locked tree; the previous floating `^8.17.1` would have put an\n  unreviewed upstream release on the merge path. This reverses a\n  choice that was right for \"developer substrate\" and wrong for \"gate\".\n* `fastjsonschema`, the third validator pole, is installed by the lane\n  explicitly because `pyproject.toml` declares it nowhere — the same\n  shape as the PyYAML line in `tests.yml`, and the same follow-up: the\n  declaration belongs in `pyproject.toml`.\n\nA \"verify every validator pole is present\" step hard-fails if node,\neither `node_modules` tree, or either Python validator is missing, so\nthe poles cannot quietly go back to skipping.\n\nAfter this commit 292 of 414 modules are in an enforced lane and 80 are\nunassigned — exactly table B, which is the next step. The number is a\nby-product; the decision is which invariants are allowed to go\nunchecked.\n\nRefs: ADR-0075, CTO decision paper 2026-09-14 §1.3, §3/L2, §4.2 step 3.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(lanes): add the observation lane — 80 modules run, none of them block\n\nStep 4 of the Welle-3 lane promotion (ADR-0075; CTO decision paper §1.4\ntable B). `test-observation-lane.yml` runs the eighty modules that were\nin no lane and are not candidates for a required one yet: infra\nsubstrate, orchestrator substrate, the SPIRE federation test root,\nspec/ADR audit evidence, operator-script drivers.\n\nNot a required status context, not a dependency of the acceptance-gate\naggregator: a red run is visible on the pull request and does not block\nit. All of these are green on a developer machine; whether they behave\nthe same on a GitHub runner was unmeasured when this lane was written,\nand \"unmeasured\" is precisely why they are not blocking. The lane emits\na junit report and a skip-reason table into the job summary, because the\nskip balance is the evidence the promotion decision needs.\n\nPromotion date 2026-10-31, recorded per group and — from the next\ncommit — compared against the calendar.\n\nTwo derivation changes came out of building this, both because the file\nwould otherwise have claimed something untrue:\n\n* **Opt-in markers now outrank a lane that does not switch them on.**\n  The observation lane targets `tests/infra/` as a directory, which\n  includes `test_phase_3_acceptance_gates.py` — guarded by the\n  `phase_3_skeleton` marker that no workflow sets. Before this change\n  the module would have been reported `optional-ci`: in a lane, running,\n  covered. It collects and skips every test. `lane_inventory` now knows\n  which workflow switches which marker on, and a module whose marker\n  nobody switches stays `opt-in-marker` no matter how many runners are\n  pointed at it. The live-VM lane, which does pass `--run-live-vm`, is\n  unaffected and stays `operator-hand`.\n\n* **Observation-lane membership is an exception and needs a group.**\n  `optional-ci` on its own asks no questions, so a new module dropped\n  into `tests/infra/` would have joined the pen silently. A module whose\n  only lane is the observation lane now has to name a group, and the\n  group carries the owner and the date.\n\nRefs: ADR-0075 §3, CTO decision paper 2026-09-14 §1.4, §4.2 step 4.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(lanes): compare exemption dates against the calendar (ADR-0075 §3)\n\nStep 5 of the Welle-3 lane promotion, and the one without which the four\nbefore it are decoration.\n\n`tests/lanes/lane_assignment.json` has carried a `review_by` on every\nexemption group since the inventory landed. Nothing compared those dates\nto today: the only assertion was that the string parsed as an ISO date,\nand `grep -n \"today\\|now()\"` over the lane tooling returned zero hits. A\ndate that is never compared to today is a longer way of writing an\nundated exception.\n\nADR-0075 §3, approved 2026-09-15 in the hard variant (alternative (d),\n\"expiry dates as a warning only\", rejected):\n\n  * `tooling/ci/exemption_expiry.py` — every exemption carries a\n    mandatory `review_by`; expired is **red**, not a summary note. The\n    check runs inside `tests/lanes/test_exemption_expiry.py`, i.e.\n    inside the required `wirelang suite with rfc8785 + jsonschema`\n    context. Damper: warning from `review_by − 14 days`, red from\n    `review_by`, rendered into the job summary on every run.\n  * `tooling/ci/check_exemption_renewal.py` — the half a hermetic test\n    cannot do. On a pull request it reads the base commit''s version of\n    the file and refuses a `review_by` that moved without `renewed_on`\n    and a `renewal_reason` of its own. Without it, the way out of a red\n    lane is to edit one character on the morning it goes red.\n  * The two collect-gate allow-lists are read by the same rule. Both are\n    empty today and the file says keeping them empty is the point; the\n    rule has to exist before the first entry, because the first entry\n    will be added on a day when somebody is in a hurry.\n\nNo `--today` flag anywhere in the CLI, and a test asserts no workflow\npasses one. A gate that takes the calendar from its caller is a gate\nthat will be handed the wrong date on exactly the day it matters.\n\nNegative controls, run against the real file and the real base commit:\n\n  * `live-vm-operator` review_by set to 2026-09-01 → the required\n    lane-assignment suite FAILS with the group, the owner and the three\n    legitimate ways forward; `--check` exits 1.\n  * `phase-3c-opt-in` review_by moved 2026-09-30 → 2027-03-31 with no\n    renewal fields, diffed against `e1f1555` → renewal check exits 1.\n  * Same move with `renewed_on` + a reason → exits 0.\n  * Off-by-one pinned in both directions: `review_by` itself is still\n    warning, the day after is expired.\n\nLive state on 2026-09-15: nine exemptions, none expired, none in the\nwarning window. The two 2026-09-30 groups — the Phase-3c opt-in drills\nand the live-VM lane — enter the warning window tomorrow.\n\nRefs: ADR-0075 §3, CTO decision paper 2026-09-14 §1.6, §4.2 step 5.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(lanes): make the evidence citations true, and record what changed\n\nFour citation markers set on 2026-09-14 said \"not executed by any\nworkflow today\". Three of them are now false in the good direction and\nare corrected to name the job that runs the module; the fourth — the\nexternal-verifier harness — is rewritten, because its claim changed\nsubstance rather than status.\n\n* `STABILITY.md` merkle aggregator row: `test_hash_consistency.py` runs\n  in `Lane — WAT core`.\n* `STABILITY.md` report-validator row: the negative matrix in\n  `tests/ci/test_validate_demo_proof_report.py` runs in `Lane — CI\n  meta`. Until now the validator ran as the hard gate while its own\n  regression tests ran nowhere.\n* `STABILITY.md` external-verifier row: was \"two independent\n  implementations validate our schemas — and nothing in CI has ever run\n  them\". Now: four poles, executed, with the lane named and the reason\n  it stays `beta` stated plainly — the first real execution was today,\n  so the claim is checked but not checked *for long*. The row also\n  records that it has now been corrected three times, because the\n  previous correction stayed inside the error class it was fixing.\n* `docs/architecture/layers.md` rows 3 and 4 likewise; the module count\n  for `tests/wat/` is corrected from 55 to the actual 47.\n\nAlso:\n\n* The acceptance-gate workflow header states the name mismatch instead\n  of leaving a reader to discover it: the file carries three jobs that\n  are not Phase-2 acceptance gates, renaming the aggregator job is\n  forbidden because its display name is the branch-protection context,\n  and every `needs:` entry must also appear in `GATE_RESULTS`.\n* `tests.yml`''s whole-tree collect-gate comment no longer claims 177\n  modules run nowhere. They do now; the step''s own justification is\n  unchanged and restated.\n* `lane_assignment.json` gains a `lane_promotion` record — base commit,\n  before/after counts, one line per lane, and the inheritance caveat —\n  so the next report quotes the file instead of a brief.\n* `lane_inventory.py`''s docstring documents the two derivation rules it\n  learned in this PR, in the \"scope and honesty about it\" section where\n  a reader looks for exactly that.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(lanes): stop the observation lane from re-running modules that have their own lane\n\nThe first CI run of `test-observation-lane.yml` was red: 19 failures, and\nnone of them about the modules the lane was built to observe. Both causes\nare the same mistake of mine — a directory target that swallowed modules\nwhose lane deliberately runs somewhere else.\n\n* `tests/infra/test_quadlet_lint.py` (3 failures). Its lane in\n  `e2e-bringup-ci.yml` runs inside a Fedora 40 container **on purpose**;\n  the workflow says why in a comment: Ubuntu''s podman 4.9 rejects a\n  Quadlet key (`Entrypoint=`) the units use. On `ubuntu-latest` it fails\n  for a reason about the runner. My local podman is 5.8.4, which is why\n  this could not show up before a CI run.\n* `tests/infra/test_pilot_bringup_e2e_container.py` (16 failures, 1 its\n  own). It bind-mounts the checkout into a container and overwrites\n  `infra/spire/federation/config/spire-server-wakir.conf` and\n  `infra/spire/agent/config/spire-agent-wakir.conf` with `# stub …\n  conf`. In its own job nothing reads them afterwards. In a shared\n  session 14 later tests read them and failed — one cause, sixteen\n  symptoms, and the symptoms are what a reader sees first. It skips\n  locally because the sandbox has no usable podman.\n\nFixed at the root rather than at the symptom:\n\n* The lane ignores all 27 modules in its roots that already have a lane.\n  A module with its own lane is not being observed by a second one; it\n  is being run in the environment its own lane avoids.\n* `tooling/ci/lane_inventory.py` subtracts `--ignore` / `--ignore-glob`\n  / `--deselect` when resolving lane targets, in both the `--ignore=X`\n  and the `--ignore X` spelling. Without that the assignment file would\n  credit this lane with modules it does not run — the one claim the file\n  exists to make impossible.\n* `tests/lanes/test_lane_target_resolution.py` pins both halves: the\n  subtraction on a throw-away tree, and the live rule that no module the\n  observation lane targets has another lane. With a negative control\n  against the obvious way to satisfy that rule — an ignore list that\n  grows until the lane is empty.\n* The lane checks `git status --porcelain` after the run and says so in\n  the summary when a test wrote into the working tree. It is a\n  diagnostic, not a gate: the next cascade of this shape names its own\n  cause in the first line instead of the sixteenth.\n\nNot fixed here, reported instead: the mutation itself is a defect in\n`test_pilot_bringup_e2e_container.py` (container substrate, not my\ndomain), and the `Entrypoint=` key is unsupported by podman < 5, which\nis a portability statement about the Quadlet units rather than about\ntheir test. Both go to the infra owner via the done brief.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ci(lanes): log skip reasons in every promoted lane (-rs)\n\nThe repository''s pytest config carries `addopts = \"-q\"`, which collapses\nper-test output to per-file progress characters. A lane''s log therefore\nshowed `tests/wat/test_tv3_backfill_leg.py sssssssss` and no reason for\nany of the nine.\n\nThat is the wrong half to hide. A lane''s skip balance is the difference\nbetween \"these invariants are checked\" and \"these invariants are\ncollected\", and it is the specific thing the Welle-3 promotion was asked\nto report from a real CI run rather than a local one. `-rs` puts the\nreasons in the log where they can be read without re-running anything.\n\nApplied to the four promoted lanes and to the proof-path driver suite.\nThe observation lane already had it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ci(proof-path): install jsonschema so the proof-format check stops skipping\n\nMeasured in the first CI run of the promoted driver suite: two tests\nskipped in the required `proof-path` context with `could not import\n''jsonschema''`. They are the F7 regressions — they validate the emitted\n`proof.json` against `wirelang/schemas/wakir-inclusion-proof-v1.json`\nand check the schema itself is a well-formed Draft 2020-12 document.\n\n`jsonschema` sits behind the `test` extra, not in `dependencies`, so\n`pip install -e .` does not bring it. A required context whose schema\ncheck silently skips is the shape of the entire Welle-3 finding list, so\nthe lane installs it.\n\nAlso measured, and the reason this suite belongs in this context rather\nthan a generic hermetic lane: the two `wakir_verify`-gated tests did\n**not** skip here. This is the only job that installs it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(ci): say in the branch-protection inventory that lanes are attached, not registered\n\nThe required-check inventory listed row 11 as the Phase-2 acceptance\naggregator. It now also carries three lane jobs, and row 1''s job carries\n`tests/workflows/` and the exemption-expiry gate. None of them is a\nstatus context of its own, deliberately — a new context costs a\nbranch-protection change plus an exact display name, which is the\nPR #102 forever-pending class.\n\nThe consequence belongs next to the invariant it depends on: the\n`if: always()` plus per-dependency result check is now load-bearing for\nthree further jobs carrying dozens of modules each, and a broken\npropagation shows up a second time as a profile mismatch in\n`tests/lanes/lane_assignment.json`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"4fc656e6db97cbf6b1bc084b236f6708aef77656","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/trees/4fc656e6db97cbf6b1bc084b236f6708aef77656"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/commits/0ac72ddba6cad388763395363550e9093e941b17","verification":{"payload":"tree 4fc656e6db97cbf6b1bc084b236f6708aef77656\nparent e1f155565627dde1657a808f20438dacd2d2af3a\nauthor wakir-labs <xypu4r8e@addy.io> 1789431447 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789431447 +0200\n\ntest(lanes): promote 97 modules into enforced lanes, add an observation lane, and give exemption dates teeth (ADR-0075) (#542)\n\n* test(lanes): run the fail-open guard before anything depends on it\n\n`tests/workflows/` — five hermetic structure suites over\n`.github/workflows/*.yml`, 177 tests, 1.9 s — ran in no workflow at all.\nAmong them is `test_required_context_error_propagation.py`, the guard\nthat asserts a required status context built on `needs:` cannot fail\nopen: it must carry `if: always()` and must turn every\n`needs.<job>.result` into its own exit code in its first step. That\nguard was added in the same commit that fixed finding R6 and has never\nexecuted.\n\nIt is wired into `production-suite` in `tests.yml`, whose display name\n`wirelang suite with rfc8785 + jsonschema` is already a branch-\nprotection context. No new required context, no branch-protection\nchange.\n\nTwo reasons for that lane rather than a new gate job under\n`runtime-acceptance-gates.yml`:\n\n* Ordering. The next commit extends the acceptance-gate aggregator with\n  new `needs:` entries. A guard that only goes live *through* that\n  change cannot have checked it. Attaching it to an independent,\n  already-required lane first makes the aggregator extension the first\n  edit the guard covers.\n* Dependencies. The guard opens with `pytest.importorskip(\"yaml\")`.\n  `pip install -e . pytest` does not pull PyYAML (it sits behind the\n  `persona` extra), so in a fresh gate job the module would collect zero\n  tests and report green without checking anything — the exact defect\n  class it exists to catch. This lane installs PyYAML explicitly.\n\nThe five modules move from `unassigned` to `required` in\n`tests/lanes/lane_assignment.json`; the `workflow-shape` exemption group\nis gone because it no longer describes anything.\n\nRefs: ADR-0075, CTO decision paper 2026-09-14 §1.3, §1.7, §4.2 step 1.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(lanes): attach the CI-meta suite to the acceptance-gate aggregator\n\nStep 2 of the Welle-3 lane promotion. `tests/ci/` — the hermetic tests\nabout the CI substrate itself — becomes a gate job under\n`runtime-acceptance-gates.yml` and is pulled into the aggregator''s\n`needs:` and its `GATE_RESULTS` result check in the same edit.\n\nAmong the modules that now run: the branch-protection check-name audit\n(the PR #102 forever-pending class) and `test_validate_demo_proof_report.py`,\nthe test module of the R1 report validator that `STABILITY.md` cites as\nevidence. Two path-filtered probes that were `optional-ci` are promoted\nwith them — both hermetic, both already on the PR surface, and a path\nfilter is not enforcement.\n\nNo new required status context and no branch-protection change: the\naggregator''s display name stays the only context of this lane, and the\ngate job inherits its enforcement.\n\n`tooling/ci/lane_inventory.py` learns that inheritance, because\notherwise the assignment file would have to call these modules\n`optional-ci` while they block merges — a measuring tool taking its\nconstant from the status quo instead of the decision, which is the class\nADR-0075 is about. The inheritance is granted only while all three\nhalves of the propagation hold: the job is in `needs:`, the aggregator\ncarries job-level `if: always()`, and its first unconditional step turns\n`needs.<job>.result` into an exit code.\n\nThat makes the omission of a `GATE_RESULTS` entry visible twice, from\ntwo directions. Negative control, run before this commit (entry removed\nfrom `GATE_RESULTS` only, job left in `needs:`):\n\n  * tests/workflows/test_required_context_error_propagation.py\n    ::test_required_context_checks_every_dependency_result_first — FAILED\n  * tests/lanes/test_required_inheritance.py\n    ::test_the_live_acceptance_gate_aggregator_propagates_to_every_gate_job — FAILED\n  * tests/lanes/test_lane_assignment.py::test_declared_profile_matches_workflows\n    — FAILED (the 11 modules fall back to optional-ci)\n\nThe first of those three is the guard that step 1 made live. It was\nalready running when this commit was written, which is the whole reason\nstep 1 came first.\n\nThe lane also runs `collect_gate.py --profile production --root tests/ci`\nbefore pytest, so a module-level import guard cannot make a module\ndisappear into a green run.\n\nRefs: ADR-0075 §1, CTO decision paper 2026-09-14 §1.3, §1.7, §4.2 step 2.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(lanes): promote WAT core, spec freeze, parity, routing — and make the four-validator claim true\n\nStep 3 of the Welle-3 lane promotion (ADR-0075; CTO decision paper\n§1.3 table A, §3/L2). Three additions, all attached to existing\nenforcement rather than registered as new required contexts.\n\n`lane-wat-core` (46 modules, ~20 s)\n  Merkle-root construction and proof verification, manifest v1/v2\n  signature and schema admission, OTS full-verify, cross-module anchor\n  pin consistency — the proof path Phase 4 exists to seal, running in no\n  workflow until now.\n\n`lane-hermetic-invariants` (36 modules, ~4 s)\n  Spec-freeze strict-superset property (the required freeze-seal probe\n  checks the seal envelope, not the property), six Python/Rust parity\n  suites that sit under `tests/` instead of `wirelang/` and were\n  therefore outside the required suite, twenty alert-routing modules\n  (the receiver-catalog completeness class — the outage we actually\n  had), the bug-42 regression suite, and the five hermetic counterparts\n  of the shell-only digest-pin context.\n\n`proof-path`\n  `tests/scripts/test_demo_proof.py` joins the context that already\n  executes the driver it tests. Placed there and not in a generic\n  hermetic lane for a measured reason: two of its tests skip without\n  `wakir_verify` importable, and this is the only job in the repository\n  that installs it.\n\nThe Node question (finding L2), decided\n---------------------------------------\n\n`STABILITY.md` claims two independent JSON-Schema implementations\nvalidate our published schemas. Measured: no workflow ran the guard\nmodule, and no workflow installed Node at all — the claim had been\nexecuted zero times. Putting the module in a lane without Node would\nhave left it that way, with a green tick on top.\n\nDecision: install Node in the lane. Measured before deciding — with\nnode + ajv + hyperjump + fastjsonschema present, the module runs 15\ntests, 0 skipped, all passing. The claim is true; it had simply never\nbeen checked.\n\nTwo consequences carried deliberately:\n\n* `package-lock.json` for both harnesses is now committed and the\n  `.gitignore` lines that excluded them are removed. `npm ci` installs\n  the locked tree; the previous floating `^8.17.1` would have put an\n  unreviewed upstream release on the merge path. This reverses a\n  choice that was right for \"developer substrate\" and wrong for \"gate\".\n* `fastjsonschema`, the third validator pole, is installed by the lane\n  explicitly because `pyproject.toml` declares it nowhere — the same\n  shape as the PyYAML line in `tests.yml`, and the same follow-up: the\n  declaration belongs in `pyproject.toml`.\n\nA \"verify every validator pole is present\" step hard-fails if node,\neither `node_modules` tree, or either Python validator is missing, so\nthe poles cannot quietly go back to skipping.\n\nAfter this commit 292 of 414 modules are in an enforced lane and 80 are\nunassigned — exactly table B, which is the next step. The number is a\nby-product; the decision is which invariants are allowed to go\nunchecked.\n\nRefs: ADR-0075, CTO decision paper 2026-09-14 §1.3, §3/L2, §4.2 step 3.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(lanes): add the observation lane — 80 modules run, none of them block\n\nStep 4 of the Welle-3 lane promotion (ADR-0075; CTO decision paper §1.4\ntable B). `test-observation-lane.yml` runs the eighty modules that were\nin no lane and are not candidates for a required one yet: infra\nsubstrate, orchestrator substrate, the SPIRE federation test root,\nspec/ADR audit evidence, operator-script drivers.\n\nNot a required status context, not a dependency of the acceptance-gate\naggregator: a red run is visible on the pull request and does not block\nit. All of these are green on a developer machine; whether they behave\nthe same on a GitHub runner was unmeasured when this lane was written,\nand \"unmeasured\" is precisely why they are not blocking. The lane emits\na junit report and a skip-reason table into the job summary, because the\nskip balance is the evidence the promotion decision needs.\n\nPromotion date 2026-10-31, recorded per group and — from the next\ncommit — compared against the calendar.\n\nTwo derivation changes came out of building this, both because the file\nwould otherwise have claimed something untrue:\n\n* **Opt-in markers now outrank a lane that does not switch them on.**\n  The observation lane targets `tests/infra/` as a directory, which\n  includes `test_phase_3_acceptance_gates.py` — guarded by the\n  `phase_3_skeleton` marker that no workflow sets. Before this change\n  the module would have been reported `optional-ci`: in a lane, running,\n  covered. It collects and skips every test. `lane_inventory` now knows\n  which workflow switches which marker on, and a module whose marker\n  nobody switches stays `opt-in-marker` no matter how many runners are\n  pointed at it. The live-VM lane, which does pass `--run-live-vm`, is\n  unaffected and stays `operator-hand`.\n\n* **Observation-lane membership is an exception and needs a group.**\n  `optional-ci` on its own asks no questions, so a new module dropped\n  into `tests/infra/` would have joined the pen silently. A module whose\n  only lane is the observation lane now has to name a group, and the\n  group carries the owner and the date.\n\nRefs: ADR-0075 §3, CTO decision paper 2026-09-14 §1.4, §4.2 step 4.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(lanes): compare exemption dates against the calendar (ADR-0075 §3)\n\nStep 5 of the Welle-3 lane promotion, and the one without which the four\nbefore it are decoration.\n\n`tests/lanes/lane_assignment.json` has carried a `review_by` on every\nexemption group since the inventory landed. Nothing compared those dates\nto today: the only assertion was that the string parsed as an ISO date,\nand `grep -n \"today\\|now()\"` over the lane tooling returned zero hits. A\ndate that is never compared to today is a longer way of writing an\nundated exception.\n\nADR-0075 §3, approved 2026-09-15 in the hard variant (alternative (d),\n\"expiry dates as a warning only\", rejected):\n\n  * `tooling/ci/exemption_expiry.py` — every exemption carries a\n    mandatory `review_by`; expired is **red**, not a summary note. The\n    check runs inside `tests/lanes/test_exemption_expiry.py`, i.e.\n    inside the required `wirelang suite with rfc8785 + jsonschema`\n    context. Damper: warning from `review_by − 14 days`, red from\n    `review_by`, rendered into the job summary on every run.\n  * `tooling/ci/check_exemption_renewal.py` — the half a hermetic test\n    cannot do. On a pull request it reads the base commit''s version of\n    the file and refuses a `review_by` that moved without `renewed_on`\n    and a `renewal_reason` of its own. Without it, the way out of a red\n    lane is to edit one character on the morning it goes red.\n  * The two collect-gate allow-lists are read by the same rule. Both are\n    empty today and the file says keeping them empty is the point; the\n    rule has to exist before the first entry, because the first entry\n    will be added on a day when somebody is in a hurry.\n\nNo `--today` flag anywhere in the CLI, and a test asserts no workflow\npasses one. A gate that takes the calendar from its caller is a gate\nthat will be handed the wrong date on exactly the day it matters.\n\nNegative controls, run against the real file and the real base commit:\n\n  * `live-vm-operator` review_by set to 2026-09-01 → the required\n    lane-assignment suite FAILS with the group, the owner and the three\n    legitimate ways forward; `--check` exits 1.\n  * `phase-3c-opt-in` review_by moved 2026-09-30 → 2027-03-31 with no\n    renewal fields, diffed against `e1f1555` → renewal check exits 1.\n  * Same move with `renewed_on` + a reason → exits 0.\n  * Off-by-one pinned in both directions: `review_by` itself is still\n    warning, the day after is expired.\n\nLive state on 2026-09-15: nine exemptions, none expired, none in the\nwarning window. The two 2026-09-30 groups — the Phase-3c opt-in drills\nand the live-VM lane — enter the warning window tomorrow.\n\nRefs: ADR-0075 §3, CTO decision paper 2026-09-14 §1.6, §4.2 step 5.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(lanes): make the evidence citations true, and record what changed\n\nFour citation markers set on 2026-09-14 said \"not executed by any\nworkflow today\". Three of them are now false in the good direction and\nare corrected to name the job that runs the module; the fourth — the\nexternal-verifier harness — is rewritten, because its claim changed\nsubstance rather than status.\n\n* `STABILITY.md` merkle aggregator row: `test_hash_consistency.py` runs\n  in `Lane — WAT core`.\n* `STABILITY.md` report-validator row: the negative matrix in\n  `tests/ci/test_validate_demo_proof_report.py` runs in `Lane — CI\n  meta`. Until now the validator ran as the hard gate while its own\n  regression tests ran nowhere.\n* `STABILITY.md` external-verifier row: was \"two independent\n  implementations validate our schemas — and nothing in CI has ever run\n  them\". Now: four poles, executed, with the lane named and the reason\n  it stays `beta` stated plainly — the first real execution was today,\n  so the claim is checked but not checked *for long*. The row also\n  records that it has now been corrected three times, because the\n  previous correction stayed inside the error class it was fixing.\n* `docs/architecture/layers.md` rows 3 and 4 likewise; the module count\n  for `tests/wat/` is corrected from 55 to the actual 47.\n\nAlso:\n\n* The acceptance-gate workflow header states the name mismatch instead\n  of leaving a reader to discover it: the file carries three jobs that\n  are not Phase-2 acceptance gates, renaming the aggregator job is\n  forbidden because its display name is the branch-protection context,\n  and every `needs:` entry must also appear in `GATE_RESULTS`.\n* `tests.yml`''s whole-tree collect-gate comment no longer claims 177\n  modules run nowhere. They do now; the step''s own justification is\n  unchanged and restated.\n* `lane_assignment.json` gains a `lane_promotion` record — base commit,\n  before/after counts, one line per lane, and the inheritance caveat —\n  so the next report quotes the file instead of a brief.\n* `lane_inventory.py`''s docstring documents the two derivation rules it\n  learned in this PR, in the \"scope and honesty about it\" section where\n  a reader looks for exactly that.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(lanes): stop the observation lane from re-running modules that have their own lane\n\nThe first CI run of `test-observation-lane.yml` was red: 19 failures, and\nnone of them about the modules the lane was built to observe. Both causes\nare the same mistake of mine — a directory target that swallowed modules\nwhose lane deliberately runs somewhere else.\n\n* `tests/infra/test_quadlet_lint.py` (3 failures). Its lane in\n  `e2e-bringup-ci.yml` runs inside a Fedora 40 container **on purpose**;\n  the workflow says why in a comment: Ubuntu''s podman 4.9 rejects a\n  Quadlet key (`Entrypoint=`) the units use. On `ubuntu-latest` it fails\n  for a reason about the runner. My local podman is 5.8.4, which is why\n  this could not show up before a CI run.\n* `tests/infra/test_pilot_bringup_e2e_container.py` (16 failures, 1 its\n  own). It bind-mounts the checkout into a container and overwrites\n  `infra/spire/federation/config/spire-server-wakir.conf` and\n  `infra/spire/agent/config/spire-agent-wakir.conf` with `# stub …\n  conf`. In its own job nothing reads them afterwards. In a shared\n  session 14 later tests read them and failed — one cause, sixteen\n  symptoms, and the symptoms are what a reader sees first. It skips\n  locally because the sandbox has no usable podman.\n\nFixed at the root rather than at the symptom:\n\n* The lane ignores all 27 modules in its roots that already have a lane.\n  A module with its own lane is not being observed by a second one; it\n  is being run in the environment its own lane avoids.\n* `tooling/ci/lane_inventory.py` subtracts `--ignore` / `--ignore-glob`\n  / `--deselect` when resolving lane targets, in both the `--ignore=X`\n  and the `--ignore X` spelling. Without that the assignment file would\n  credit this lane with modules it does not run — the one claim the file\n  exists to make impossible.\n* `tests/lanes/test_lane_target_resolution.py` pins both halves: the\n  subtraction on a throw-away tree, and the live rule that no module the\n  observation lane targets has another lane. With a negative control\n  against the obvious way to satisfy that rule — an ignore list that\n  grows until the lane is empty.\n* The lane checks `git status --porcelain` after the run and says so in\n  the summary when a test wrote into the working tree. It is a\n  diagnostic, not a gate: the next cascade of this shape names its own\n  cause in the first line instead of the sixteenth.\n\nNot fixed here, reported instead: the mutation itself is a defect in\n`test_pilot_bringup_e2e_container.py` (container substrate, not my\ndomain), and the `Entrypoint=` key is unsupported by podman < 5, which\nis a portability statement about the Quadlet units rather than about\ntheir test. Both go to the infra owner via the done brief.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ci(lanes): log skip reasons in every promoted lane (-rs)\n\nThe repository''s pytest config carries `addopts = \"-q\"`, which collapses\nper-test output to per-file progress characters. A lane''s log therefore\nshowed `tests/wat/test_tv3_backfill_leg.py sssssssss` and no reason for\nany of the nine.\n\nThat is the wrong half to hide. A lane''s skip balance is the difference\nbetween \"these invariants are checked\" and \"these invariants are\ncollected\", and it is the specific thing the Welle-3 promotion was asked\nto report from a real CI run rather than a local one. `-rs` puts the\nreasons in the log where they can be read without re-running anything.\n\nApplied to the four promoted lanes and to the proof-path driver suite.\nThe observation lane already had it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ci(proof-path): install jsonschema so the proof-format check stops skipping\n\nMeasured in the first CI run of the promoted driver suite: two tests\nskipped in the required `proof-path` context with `could not import\n''jsonschema''`. They are the F7 regressions — they validate the emitted\n`proof.json` against `wirelang/schemas/wakir-inclusion-proof-v1.json`\nand check the schema itself is a well-formed Draft 2020-12 document.\n\n`jsonschema` sits behind the `test` extra, not in `dependencies`, so\n`pip install -e .` does not bring it. A required context whose schema\ncheck silently skips is the shape of the entire Welle-3 finding list, so\nthe lane installs it.\n\nAlso measured, and the reason this suite belongs in this context rather\nthan a generic hermetic lane: the two `wakir_verify`-gated tests did\n**not** skip here. This is the only job that installs it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(ci): say in the branch-protection inventory that lanes are attached, not registered\n\nThe required-check inventory listed row 11 as the Phase-2 acceptance\naggregator. It now also carries three lane jobs, and row 1''s job carries\n`tests/workflows/` and the exemption-expiry gate. None of them is a\nstatus context of its own, deliberately — a new context costs a\nbranch-protection change plus an exact display name, which is the\nPR #102 forever-pending class.\n\nThe consequence belongs next to the invariant it depends on: the\n`if: always()` plus per-dependency result check is now load-bearing for\nthree further jobs carrying dozens of modules each, and a broken\npropagation shows up a second time as a profile mismatch in\n`tests/lanes/lane_assignment.json`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqI6XCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAuhsQAKTpw0S2jTK95eVhwX/08kLh\n7j0uDUnDYHQMLB+9k4i+tuWTPeSt8IZJBkYYkqPIY0OBA9hyXbPT6bWHkkf7KPDl\n9N6KaThCs1CyOeET1JErx8E0dQpHz26gPwXYNud5FjuBFslT0+skjZZ/vQyvOjrL\nIgU6eI0eC25qJyNjYpUuaTriBQaoEErx73L6fvolmD7Y6JNbBK4tdwaTYizRzOrI\nQd4BAf9zTeOZoQxEFLaYJicUgky+BrXU6NRRqTa7TRPMLkgvyjiKywbP3rYuDR8N\nq3NTU6BYIZZBSYcxZIGv3wjrU3W/DsNWmAVrUZ98tWhS5BO+aI3Kpp6OaiuBYvWi\nWGXV1yziUMjLmCnialqxRplULEjL4lIDQ0kcd/Vq/D+ClhL6Hsrk7h+510HbrlUT\n4rd1kfvDWjNF0A4kXktBC0INQk391+DRttxcOS5LSddWo6Fnys2e8BVlZ5clnsLZ\nYRUYigf1oE3mRzdDIrbONF83xD+oF41J7EALAVH/oZZeYNepLiei22xbMsUlPjDN\ntAvI9X/ZbLb5uqxh1ePoP6Zk9A/vXV3SkbqAlKg70yX8Vpr0m2v34yZ5aAvks2Ij\n8spscvfBrCHt69uSqeeWpt8fpuiJJ8nIW70Hx1HPRoP5iJzP8aBXbxYipGnUGXCp\nyXsNCUySgxtkaOtsaWwA\n=t/bi\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-15T00:17:28Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/0ac72ddba6cad388763395363550e9093e941b17","node_id":"C_kwDOSVv36NoAKDBhYzcyZGRiYTZjYWQzODg3NjMzOTUzNjM1NTBlOTA5M2U5NDFiMTc","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/e1f155565627dde1657a808f20438dacd2d2af3a","sha":"e1f155565627dde1657a808f20438dacd2d2af3a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/e1f155565627dde1657a808f20438dacd2d2af3a"}],"sha":"0ac72ddba6cad388763395363550e9093e941b17","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/0ac72ddba6cad388763395363550e9093e941b17"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/281068747?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs","id":281068747,"login":"wakir-labs","node_id":"U_kgDOEMDEyw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/e1f155565627dde1657a808f20438dacd2d2af3a/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-14T22:18:03Z","email":"xypu4r8e@addy.io","name":"wakir-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-14T22:18:03Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"docs: mark cited test modules that no workflow executes (#541)\n\nMeasured, not assumed: of the five test modules cited in STABILITY.md and\nlayers.md, four are executed by no workflow. Only\ntests/compat/test_zone3_capability_token_hash.py runs, via cross-repo-compat.yml.\n\nOne of the four was introduced by yesterday''s correction. PR #540 replaced a\nreference to a deleted workflow with a reference to\ntests/wat/test_external_verifier_parity.py \"in the ordinary test lane\". No\nworkflow runs that module, and no workflow installs Node, so the Node.js half\nof the conformance harness could not run even if it were wired in. The\ncorrection stayed inside the error class it was fixing.\n\nNo module is wired into a lane here - that is the open decision from PR #539.\n\nCo-authored-by: Fred <fabian@callandor.gmbh>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"8188b5ca075ae776f12c9a68008352b425a4942a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/trees/8188b5ca075ae776f12c9a68008352b425a4942a"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/commits/e1f155565627dde1657a808f20438dacd2d2af3a","verification":{"payload":"tree 8188b5ca075ae776f12c9a68008352b425a4942a\nparent 441c9d604ead493bc6fab067883ef3bb311304cf\nauthor wakir-labs <xypu4r8e@addy.io> 1789424283 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789424283 +0200\n\ndocs: mark cited test modules that no workflow executes (#541)\n\nMeasured, not assumed: of the five test modules cited in STABILITY.md and\nlayers.md, four are executed by no workflow. Only\ntests/compat/test_zone3_capability_token_hash.py runs, via cross-repo-compat.yml.\n\nOne of the four was introduced by yesterday''s correction. PR #540 replaced a\nreference to a deleted workflow with a reference to\ntests/wat/test_external_verifier_parity.py \"in the ordinary test lane\". No\nworkflow runs that module, and no workflow installs Node, so the Node.js half\nof the conformance harness could not run even if it were wired in. The\ncorrection stayed inside the error class it was fixing.\n\nNo module is wired into a lane here - that is the open decision from PR #539.\n\nCo-authored-by: Fred <fabian@callandor.gmbh>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqHKbCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAmm8QAHwPnod8FmWbd+OJ4sB1SBra\nOQzIMAqH+cnda9RmIFZroDSHNwo6gZ0Mkrd+R5Y4msGeiWYYhTCm/pCP+UulQKuL\nRcq3Amisyn1rOn9mYs5EutQIlwmMrmpBwdq+BYJIkaDxcgQHQYAtV5kNQahTmW03\nZ6sUcv4mDiXsgZKa9K0KBTDZoEpNEUeNQueHZPIVM7cjtt7IkJPGXc/zFy/6zFL1\nXw1RhvI+IIyoQJ1gb2ctD53c4nYIUOHfnY//cI4Fb84zd4TDIspBWH7j13QhUQen\nbikJS8bDpJcvp9VmdRAClJIdg9uHfv4brOPN/Iuqda1JVQztyTuO+4MyODtyako3\nDkl6aoS8o06VYX3+r4s02R0uVuSCf7y743xZGW/8COWXhRHfmp+WFgrviGRkezUl\nAjINGAL7oErTG2FY/aP2eN3MjsBibN4Yhn8TxbNkdcDSEmM2v1hEyZXinc4+a063\ne5b92QujSSSL/VKXzBs3nUw4bc+Q1ScqiBJtNx5ybsYC2eJDq/vG4XFpVopHnRt2\nu22s/JMqikQnj8FIgeyRIW0DHniwPa4kuqc0UZ4YQ9Xpxeaj2R6NaK5qp0acjRzi\nrx4eXvNcI7kTnj+Q7EnE+k8ZrOD6FQ9tl9eL5Rrov0+n1hOaTgzvllwWFvhinIvX\nCv124/2JocuwHYDgxA78\n=+BEz\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-14T22:18:04Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/e1f155565627dde1657a808f20438dacd2d2af3a","node_id":"C_kwDOSVv36NoAKGUxZjE1NTU2NTYyN2RkZTE2NTdhODA4ZjIwNDM4ZGFjZDJkMmFmM2E","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/441c9d604ead493bc6fab067883ef3bb311304cf","sha":"441c9d604ead493bc6fab067883ef3bb311304cf","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/441c9d604ead493bc6fab067883ef3bb311304cf"}],"sha":"e1f155565627dde1657a808f20438dacd2d2af3a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/e1f155565627dde1657a808f20438dacd2d2af3a"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/281068747?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs","id":281068747,"login":"wakir-labs","node_id":"U_kgDOEMDEyw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/441c9d604ead493bc6fab067883ef3bb311304cf/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-14T21:49:11Z","email":"xypu4r8e@addy.io","name":"wakir-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-14T21:49:11Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"docs: correct four maturity-matrix claims that cite evidence which does not exist (#540)\n\nMeasured against the Actions API on 2026-09-14, not inferred:\n\n- STABILITY.md L99 said SPIRE live validation runs through\n  `live-vm-acceptance.yml`. That workflow has never run: zero runs since it\n  was added on 2026-05-16. The row implied a manual, point-in-time artefact\n  that cannot exist.\n- STABILITY.md L105 said the real VM leg of `e2e-vm-acceptance-gate.yml`\n  \"only runs on manual dispatch\". Of that workflow''s 71 runs, none was a\n  workflow_dispatch (43 pull_request, 28 push) — the real VM leg has never\n  run in Actions.\n- STABILITY.md L111 called the live-VM path \"where hermetic tests have\n  historically been proven insufficient\". That history happened by operator\n  hand on a VM; this workflow has never been dispatched.\n- STABILITY.md L82 credited `external-verifier-drift.yml` with watching the\n  ajv/hyperjump tooling for divergence. It never touched `tooling/` — it\n  drove the four-pole verifier copy removed under ADR-0074 — and no longer\n  exists. The actual watcher is `tests/wat/test_external_verifier_parity.py`.\n- docs/architecture/layers.md: drops the deleted workflow from the layer-4\n  gate list and restates the live bring-up lanes as unexercised.\n\nNone of the maturity ratings change — all four rows are `experimental` or\n`internal` already. What was wrong was the justification: it pointed at a\npath never taken. Same class as the Rust row corrected in PR #537.\n\nFollow-up for the lane decision (not in this PR because it changes what\nmerges block on): a lint asserting that every workflow file named in\nSTABILITY.md and layers.md exists. Both errors above would have been caught\nby it on the day they appeared.\n\nCo-authored-by: Fred <fabian@callandor.gmbh>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"7eed86332a4e9cbb9cb3ec70104a80f1b57984f8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/trees/7eed86332a4e9cbb9cb3ec70104a80f1b57984f8"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/commits/441c9d604ead493bc6fab067883ef3bb311304cf","verification":{"payload":"tree 7eed86332a4e9cbb9cb3ec70104a80f1b57984f8\nparent 459d621bad259334106c3eed701b5b88575f7e7d\nauthor wakir-labs <xypu4r8e@addy.io> 1789422551 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789422551 +0200\n\ndocs: correct four maturity-matrix claims that cite evidence which does not exist (#540)\n\nMeasured against the Actions API on 2026-09-14, not inferred:\n\n- STABILITY.md L99 said SPIRE live validation runs through\n  `live-vm-acceptance.yml`. That workflow has never run: zero runs since it\n  was added on 2026-05-16. The row implied a manual, point-in-time artefact\n  that cannot exist.\n- STABILITY.md L105 said the real VM leg of `e2e-vm-acceptance-gate.yml`\n  \"only runs on manual dispatch\". Of that workflow''s 71 runs, none was a\n  workflow_dispatch (43 pull_request, 28 push) — the real VM leg has never\n  run in Actions.\n- STABILITY.md L111 called the live-VM path \"where hermetic tests have\n  historically been proven insufficient\". That history happened by operator\n  hand on a VM; this workflow has never been dispatched.\n- STABILITY.md L82 credited `external-verifier-drift.yml` with watching the\n  ajv/hyperjump tooling for divergence. It never touched `tooling/` — it\n  drove the four-pole verifier copy removed under ADR-0074 — and no longer\n  exists. The actual watcher is `tests/wat/test_external_verifier_parity.py`.\n- docs/architecture/layers.md: drops the deleted workflow from the layer-4\n  gate list and restates the live bring-up lanes as unexercised.\n\nNone of the maturity ratings change — all four rows are `experimental` or\n`internal` already. What was wrong was the justification: it pointed at a\npath never taken. Same class as the Rust row corrected in PR #537.\n\nFollow-up for the lane decision (not in this PR because it changes what\nmerges block on): a lint asserting that every workflow file named in\nSTABILITY.md and layers.md exists. Both errors above would have been caught\nby it on the day they appeared.\n\nCo-authored-by: Fred <fabian@callandor.gmbh>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqGvXCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAtGgQAAkAZbSSPPbF/Et09qLYMrJB\nmKUtIQS3BDUyiGw5fTqKTuLz9RegCVt2ECF47liOiDHdPQo61k6q7M5w2jjfC4r0\nGbp1ykDVJE+f4I6q9mY0tj0Ddpgv7dXM3NNE7Y8Rgpp4id9PAtNjllz+iRK1M6Nc\nA5WSrAA42SJBFUrM2wC99AH0UOfd0asWdpuWE4k+Em3iQWimAFaUfWKEK4k6fn7k\nvfoeVrf3adKz0YIKlXrfrTOEu++kh4PohO4mc1sEGEZunDVPcnAMkO6GS/h5kJP2\nlb32alBycoipr0oh+XDallbuoM+M/m6cACiw4nz6+nu35AWqEnDM/EHTxxrS+hju\nGgHraWOGUOMJsZ/p4zzPKMAg6LI+Zeqb20567U7uo2cyhDUcZbJXlePB2ZIKcWc6\nUhQnYaIihpZNQJSyXR0AI/njBOgGaIY8lxng74t7N01gcXxIXfy8PiY0+ry8HvSh\nAYbIMHyTeClJcbi8BZ9MYwCZd3UU5Z+aA7RekSr/DPkTMr3TPAEvNkyMAcLmZYY+\n76ckLCR963vELvF07awMe6Eo5mOLF46bI5Ztdp1s2/xY+QQqVQEBeRTAegvZxE0l\nmXMR/xc7Gb9liY7KYYGyO9+uJWRpSr8pSQ4bIAbYRlNLYRhWnBMv41YE6jvGLBSj\nzesA3mDVW4CmOHoHF/1Z\n=J0RB\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-14T21:49:12Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/441c9d604ead493bc6fab067883ef3bb311304cf","node_id":"C_kwDOSVv36NoAKDQ0MWM5ZDYwNGVhZDQ5M2JjNmZhYjA2Nzg4M2VmM2JiMzExMzA0Y2Y","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/459d621bad259334106c3eed701b5b88575f7e7d","sha":"459d621bad259334106c3eed701b5b88575f7e7d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/459d621bad259334106c3eed701b5b88575f7e7d"}],"sha":"441c9d604ead493bc6fab067883ef3bb311304cf","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/441c9d604ead493bc6fab067883ef3bb311304cf"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/281068747?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs","id":281068747,"login":"wakir-labs","node_id":"U_kgDOEMDEyw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/459d621bad259334106c3eed701b5b88575f7e7d/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-14T21:44:17Z","email":"xypu4r8e@addy.io","name":"wakir-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-14T21:44:17Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"test(lanes): record which gate runs which test module, and fail on silent collection loss (#539)\n\n* test(lanes): record which gate runs which test module, and fail on silent collection loss\n\nPhase-4b Welle 2, Box B. The question was not \"how do we raise coverage\"\nbut \"which of the 419 test modules does a gate actually run, and for the\nrest: is that a decision or an accident?\"\n\nOwn measurement at this baseline, against the required contexts read from\nbranch protection on 2026-09-14:\n\n  419 test modules\n  190 targeted by a required status context\n   30 by a non-required workflow\n    2 reachable only via workflow_dispatch\n   20 guarded by a skip-by-default marker\n  177 targeted by nothing at all\n\nDeviation from the static survey (418 / 189 / 218-in-any-lane at 9bb5ab1),\nall five sources named in `finding_at_baseline` in the assignment file:\nthree modules run through `python -m unittest` rather than pytest — one of\nthem in a REQUIRED context, so a pytest-only scan under-reports required\ncoverage; `tests/live_vm/` is targeted through a bash array a line scan\ndoes not expand; and the tree gained one module between 9bb5ab1 and here.\nDirection: marginally better than reported, conclusion unchanged.\n\nThe part that reframes the rest: the whole tree is hermetically green.\n`pytest tests wirelang infra` = 7187 passed, 328 skipped, 0 failed, 84s,\nno podman, no VM, no network, no cargo. The 177 unrun modules are not\nunrunnable — they were never wired up.\n\nWhat this change does\n\n* `tests/lanes/lane_assignment.json` — module -> profile for all 419,\n  plus exemption groups carrying reason, owner and, for the accidental\n  ones, a review date. `tooling/ci/lane_inventory.py` derives the same\n  facts from `.github/workflows/`; `tests/lanes/test_lane_assignment.py`\n  refuses to let the two drift. A new module without an entry is red; a\n  module that loses its required lane is red; a required context whose\n  name no longer matches a job display-name is red here instead of\n  leaving pull requests pending forever.\n\n* `tooling/ci/collect_gate.py` replaces the two `--collect-only` steps in\n  `tests.yml`. It emits the same integer the drift envelope has always\n  consumed (node-ID count == summary count, verified equal at 3307) and\n  additionally fails on a collection error and — production profile only\n  — on a module that collects zero node IDs or guards an import on a\n  name pyproject.toml never declared. The sandbox profile keeps skipping\n  as its contract; the existing hard rfc8785/jsonschema guards and the\n  drift envelope are untouched. Each failure class has a negative\n  control, including the premise that a module-level `importorskip`\n  really does exit 0 and vanish.\n\n* A third step collects `tests/ wirelang/ infra/` whole. Collection, not\n  execution: the 177 cannot rot into unimportable code unnoticed, and\n  nothing is asserted about their contents.\n\n* `testpaths` named `[\"tests\"]` — the one tree the main lane does not\n  run — while `infra/spire/federation/tests/` was invisible to both. It\n  now names every root that holds tests, with a comment saying it\n  describes where tests are, not what CI executes. A test keeps it equal\n  to the inventory''s roots.\n\nNo test deleted, disabled or rewritten. No module added to a required\nlane to make a number look better. Contracts currently checked by no gate\nare named in docs/quality-gates/test-lane-assignment.md — the fail-open\nguard for required contexts, the WAT proof path, the proof-path driver''s\nfault handling, the check-name audit, the v0.4.3 strict-superset property.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(lanes): rebase onto ADR-0074, and date the exceptions that never ran\n\nRebased onto 527508e (#538). Eight modules disappeared with the duplicated\nfour-pole verifier, all from tests/wat/external_verifier/ — one optional-ci\ndirectory, so the tree is 413 modules and the unassigned count is unchanged at\n177. `lane_inventory.py --sync` did the bookkeeping, which is the repair path\nthe tooling exists for.\n\nBoth collect-gate allow-lists are now EMPTY: the two entries they held were the\nmatrix-only guards in the deleted package. Every module-level import guard left\nin the tree names something pyproject.toml declares or a package that lives\nhere. Added `test_allow_lists_have_no_stale_entries` so dead exceptions cannot\naccumulate, and a direct assertion of the state itself rather than only via CI.\n\nThe correction that matters: \"runs by operator hand\" was accepted as a reason\nwithout evidence, and the evidence contradicts it.\n\n  live-vm-acceptance.yml        0 runs, ever (added 2026-05-16)\n  sbom-baseline-refresh.yml     0 runs, ever\n  WAKIR_PHASE_3C_* / --phase-3c-*   set by no workflow in this repository\n  WAKIR_PHASE_3_SKELETON            set by no workflow in this repository\n\nAll 22 modules classified as deliberate standing exceptions have never\nexecuted. 207 Phase-3c tests documented as \"skip-by-default, opt-in\" are in\npractice skip-always: the acceptance criteria for a cutover, written down and\nunexecuted.\n\nSo every standing exception now carries `execution_evidence` — mechanism, what\nenables it, when it last actually ran, and where that was read — and a standing\nexception whose evidence says `never` loses its exemption from a review date.\n`test_standing_exceptions_state_how_they_would_run` and\n`test_standing_exceptions_must_have_actually_run` enforce both halves. The\nevidence is a pinned, dated reading rather than a live API call, because a\nhermetic test cannot query the API and pretending otherwise would be the same\nunfalsifiable claim in a new place.\n\nVerification after the rebase: pytest tests wirelang infra = 7160 passed, 326\nskipped, 0 failed; collect-gate production over the whole tree = 7486 node IDs,\nexit 0; sandbox = 3307, unchanged; reuse lint compliant; wrap lint intact.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Fred <fabian@callandor.gmbh>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"13b9a317f8d2449bb95a2a35d9e13c26a3c4f688","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/trees/13b9a317f8d2449bb95a2a35d9e13c26a3c4f688"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/commits/459d621bad259334106c3eed701b5b88575f7e7d","verification":{"payload":"tree 13b9a317f8d2449bb95a2a35d9e13c26a3c4f688\nparent 527508ee7eb8fa9ca267d5a66033bc2f03f19af2\nauthor wakir-labs <xypu4r8e@addy.io> 1789422257 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789422257 +0200\n\ntest(lanes): record which gate runs which test module, and fail on silent collection loss (#539)\n\n* test(lanes): record which gate runs which test module, and fail on silent collection loss\n\nPhase-4b Welle 2, Box B. The question was not \"how do we raise coverage\"\nbut \"which of the 419 test modules does a gate actually run, and for the\nrest: is that a decision or an accident?\"\n\nOwn measurement at this baseline, against the required contexts read from\nbranch protection on 2026-09-14:\n\n  419 test modules\n  190 targeted by a required status context\n   30 by a non-required workflow\n    2 reachable only via workflow_dispatch\n   20 guarded by a skip-by-default marker\n  177 targeted by nothing at all\n\nDeviation from the static survey (418 / 189 / 218-in-any-lane at 9bb5ab1),\nall five sources named in `finding_at_baseline` in the assignment file:\nthree modules run through `python -m unittest` rather than pytest — one of\nthem in a REQUIRED context, so a pytest-only scan under-reports required\ncoverage; `tests/live_vm/` is targeted through a bash array a line scan\ndoes not expand; and the tree gained one module between 9bb5ab1 and here.\nDirection: marginally better than reported, conclusion unchanged.\n\nThe part that reframes the rest: the whole tree is hermetically green.\n`pytest tests wirelang infra` = 7187 passed, 328 skipped, 0 failed, 84s,\nno podman, no VM, no network, no cargo. The 177 unrun modules are not\nunrunnable — they were never wired up.\n\nWhat this change does\n\n* `tests/lanes/lane_assignment.json` — module -> profile for all 419,\n  plus exemption groups carrying reason, owner and, for the accidental\n  ones, a review date. `tooling/ci/lane_inventory.py` derives the same\n  facts from `.github/workflows/`; `tests/lanes/test_lane_assignment.py`\n  refuses to let the two drift. A new module without an entry is red; a\n  module that loses its required lane is red; a required context whose\n  name no longer matches a job display-name is red here instead of\n  leaving pull requests pending forever.\n\n* `tooling/ci/collect_gate.py` replaces the two `--collect-only` steps in\n  `tests.yml`. It emits the same integer the drift envelope has always\n  consumed (node-ID count == summary count, verified equal at 3307) and\n  additionally fails on a collection error and — production profile only\n  — on a module that collects zero node IDs or guards an import on a\n  name pyproject.toml never declared. The sandbox profile keeps skipping\n  as its contract; the existing hard rfc8785/jsonschema guards and the\n  drift envelope are untouched. Each failure class has a negative\n  control, including the premise that a module-level `importorskip`\n  really does exit 0 and vanish.\n\n* A third step collects `tests/ wirelang/ infra/` whole. Collection, not\n  execution: the 177 cannot rot into unimportable code unnoticed, and\n  nothing is asserted about their contents.\n\n* `testpaths` named `[\"tests\"]` — the one tree the main lane does not\n  run — while `infra/spire/federation/tests/` was invisible to both. It\n  now names every root that holds tests, with a comment saying it\n  describes where tests are, not what CI executes. A test keeps it equal\n  to the inventory''s roots.\n\nNo test deleted, disabled or rewritten. No module added to a required\nlane to make a number look better. Contracts currently checked by no gate\nare named in docs/quality-gates/test-lane-assignment.md — the fail-open\nguard for required contexts, the WAT proof path, the proof-path driver''s\nfault handling, the check-name audit, the v0.4.3 strict-superset property.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(lanes): rebase onto ADR-0074, and date the exceptions that never ran\n\nRebased onto 527508e (#538). Eight modules disappeared with the duplicated\nfour-pole verifier, all from tests/wat/external_verifier/ — one optional-ci\ndirectory, so the tree is 413 modules and the unassigned count is unchanged at\n177. `lane_inventory.py --sync` did the bookkeeping, which is the repair path\nthe tooling exists for.\n\nBoth collect-gate allow-lists are now EMPTY: the two entries they held were the\nmatrix-only guards in the deleted package. Every module-level import guard left\nin the tree names something pyproject.toml declares or a package that lives\nhere. Added `test_allow_lists_have_no_stale_entries` so dead exceptions cannot\naccumulate, and a direct assertion of the state itself rather than only via CI.\n\nThe correction that matters: \"runs by operator hand\" was accepted as a reason\nwithout evidence, and the evidence contradicts it.\n\n  live-vm-acceptance.yml        0 runs, ever (added 2026-05-16)\n  sbom-baseline-refresh.yml     0 runs, ever\n  WAKIR_PHASE_3C_* / --phase-3c-*   set by no workflow in this repository\n  WAKIR_PHASE_3_SKELETON            set by no workflow in this repository\n\nAll 22 modules classified as deliberate standing exceptions have never\nexecuted. 207 Phase-3c tests documented as \"skip-by-default, opt-in\" are in\npractice skip-always: the acceptance criteria for a cutover, written down and\nunexecuted.\n\nSo every standing exception now carries `execution_evidence` — mechanism, what\nenables it, when it last actually ran, and where that was read — and a standing\nexception whose evidence says `never` loses its exemption from a review date.\n`test_standing_exceptions_state_how_they_would_run` and\n`test_standing_exceptions_must_have_actually_run` enforce both halves. The\nevidence is a pinned, dated reading rather than a live API call, because a\nhermetic test cannot query the API and pretending otherwise would be the same\nunfalsifiable claim in a new place.\n\nVerification after the rebase: pytest tests wirelang infra = 7160 passed, 326\nskipped, 0 failed; collect-gate production over the whole tree = 7486 node IDs,\nexit 0; sandbox = 3307, unchanged; reuse lint compliant; wrap lint intact.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Fred <fabian@callandor.gmbh>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqGqxCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAbE4QABOK4dg6xoNC47GI4zambgDO\nk+B/ezXtKjnd1CpDLCImB4OtVtncjON125axGETFoYUpU4LAQTJ7da4YNqwJ0ZAy\nhuDDmUHxdRd639rYfGdv5rC1lLNkk7VNkfoiZ4KSEPNfsS5/em5pl1Kf9+nY2I++\n/FkcDudo/Ucw1HfwllIB1QZkaF3scJM88rztIAljuOKHU0CSCGINHWnF0Td5hwTI\n0th9Va39rrRFTOL9TQjZvrnLnIO5xRbuuMl79XlOcfmvfJWLT8GaWqQMzY67b78E\nbujAGIsY1IZVrT9eBCI1a3Ydx9JAvqos3JnPe3mozwiQV3n5aIPC15Qhvubq7Rot\nnBXkVtGMfnpAdXelrNRnslxkrH01F/7tW6/Q62IzUQ7VaPiM07hGW1nFNsrmwbmV\nAytwB1xbz9j/6RvW8PYsXjwkW/N03EglBKCtctN/prItydt6be0+T20oRka7a3Pp\nxp1RqU9gUgB086OGsqJI8OymovTSc/OiP/epPJ8icq/kJE6d4UGMTSmmfP8TbF8G\nvDSBy63QajiIKWOWVgPGkRvWI6y0dTth83gae28O+emlFELWSHYY3bk45fi/q0Y0\nWE3aJ8VZjVe1/f7n2wozZ38Zy7Yaliu2usJMzFKOKQooGsszyXYp9ncDvrSe9m2C\nxAOCltehcZOPbXhamxpr\n=h9IW\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-14T21:44:17Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/459d621bad259334106c3eed701b5b88575f7e7d","node_id":"C_kwDOSVv36NoAKDQ1OWQ2MjFiYWQyNTkzMzQxMDZjM2VlZDcwMWI1Yjg4NTc1ZjdlN2Q","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/527508ee7eb8fa9ca267d5a66033bc2f03f19af2","sha":"527508ee7eb8fa9ca267d5a66033bc2f03f19af2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/527508ee7eb8fa9ca267d5a66033bc2f03f19af2"}],"sha":"459d621bad259334106c3eed701b5b88575f7e7d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/459d621bad259334106c3eed701b5b88575f7e7d"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/281068747?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs","id":281068747,"login":"wakir-labs","node_id":"U_kgDOEMDEyw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/527508ee7eb8fa9ca267d5a66033bc2f03f19af2/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-14T21:30:22Z","email":"xypu4r8e@addy.io","name":"wakir-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-14T21:30:22Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"refactor(wat): remove the duplicated four-pole verifier and `wat-verify` (ADR-0074) (#538)\n\n`wat/anchor/external_verifier/{poles,aggregator,types}.py` were\nbyte-identical, after namespace normalisation, to the `wakir-verify`\nstate before PR #6 — the state that returns a positive anchor quorum\nfor a file that is not a timestamp proof and for a swapped root\n(finding R3). They shipped as the console script `wat-verify`, so every\n`pip install wakir-runtime` handed a third party a verifier that judges\nfalse-positive. Nothing kept the two copies in sync; the tests that\ncovered this one ran only in `external-verifier-drift.yml`, which is\nnot a required context.\n\nADR-0074 removes the copy rather than porting the fix: two Apache-2.0\nimplementations of one contract plus a parity gate to hold them\ntogether is the drift surface the copy''s own README claimed to\neliminate. The role is already assigned — ADR-0062 Cut-1 gave \"offline,\nthird-party, no Wakir-runtime state required\" to `wakir-verify`, which\nhas fulfilled it correctly since PR #6.\n\nRemoved:\n  * `wat/anchor/external_verifier/**` and `tests/wat/external_verifier/**`\n  * `[project.scripts]` entry `wat-verify`\n  * the Apache-2.0 carve-out in `REUSE.toml` and its `LICENSING.md` row\n  * `.github/workflows/external-verifier-drift.yml` — the whole file: it\n    is the python-bitcoinlib matrix for the deleted package and touches\n    nothing under `tooling/external-verifier-*`. That matrix was ported\n    to `wakir-verify` first (PR #7); the ajv/hyperjump conformance\n    tooling and its driver `scripts/external_verifier_validation.py`\n    are untouched.\n\nKept deliberately:\n  * `wakir-wat-verify` — different contract (BUSL-1.1, operator-facing,\n    needs the manifest archive), not affected by R3.\n  * `tests/wat/test_external_verifier_real_tv2_sig.py` — despite the\n    name it drives `scripts/external_verifier_validation.py`, not the\n    deleted package. Its subject stays, so it stays.\n  * `tests/wat/test_external_verifier_parity.py` — ajv ↔ jsonschema\n    parity, unrelated to the deleted code.\n\nCoverage: everything substantial from the deleted tests that\n`wakir-verify` lacked went there first in PR #7 (the python-bitcoinlib\ndrift matrix, which was dead there because no lane installed the\nlibrary). Three test functions and one module symbol were deliberately\nnot carried over — each encodes the pre-R3 semantics and each has a\nreplacement; named individually in the PR body.\n\nFollow-on edits: `tests/wat/test_aggregator_side_signing_e2e.py`\nimported two constants from the deleted fixtures module behind a\n`try/except ImportError` fallback holding byte-identical literals; the\nliterals are now inline, since an import guard that can only ever fire\nis worse than the constant it hides. `test_brand_proof_verifier_remains_apache`\nlost its subject and is replaced by `test_merkle_read_half_remains_apache`\n— the Merkle Read-Half is now the only Apache-2.0 carve-out inside a BSL\nsub-tree and was previously unguarded. The four `LICENSE-BSL.md` files\nno longer name a carve-out path that does not exist.\n\nThird parties are pointed at `wakir-labs/wakir-verify` from\n`docs/offline-verifier-for-third-parties.md` and the root README, with\nthe post-PR-#6 claims — hashlist consistency, event binding, payload,\nroot authenticity — and with the copy''s \"four independently-maintained\nverifiers\" wording explicitly withdrawn.\n\nLocal: full suite 3644 passed / 310 skipped (baseline 2193ed8: 3707 /\n311; the delta is exactly the 64 tests of the deleted suite, the infra\ntest swap nets to zero). `reuse lint` 1358/1358. Archaeology lint clean.\nREUSE-wrap lint INTACT. No required status context references the\ndeleted paths.\n\nRefs: ADR-0074, ADR-0062 Cut-1, ADR-0023b\n\nCo-authored-by: Reza Tehrani <fabian@callandor.gmbh>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"a0af1145e48566e95213c794d575a28b77752ba2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/trees/a0af1145e48566e95213c794d575a28b77752ba2"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/commits/527508ee7eb8fa9ca267d5a66033bc2f03f19af2","verification":{"payload":"tree a0af1145e48566e95213c794d575a28b77752ba2\nparent 2193ed889e752580aeda5e19543dd0d3011ffe61\nauthor wakir-labs <xypu4r8e@addy.io> 1789421422 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789421422 +0200\n\nrefactor(wat): remove the duplicated four-pole verifier and `wat-verify` (ADR-0074) (#538)\n\n`wat/anchor/external_verifier/{poles,aggregator,types}.py` were\nbyte-identical, after namespace normalisation, to the `wakir-verify`\nstate before PR #6 — the state that returns a positive anchor quorum\nfor a file that is not a timestamp proof and for a swapped root\n(finding R3). They shipped as the console script `wat-verify`, so every\n`pip install wakir-runtime` handed a third party a verifier that judges\nfalse-positive. Nothing kept the two copies in sync; the tests that\ncovered this one ran only in `external-verifier-drift.yml`, which is\nnot a required context.\n\nADR-0074 removes the copy rather than porting the fix: two Apache-2.0\nimplementations of one contract plus a parity gate to hold them\ntogether is the drift surface the copy''s own README claimed to\neliminate. The role is already assigned — ADR-0062 Cut-1 gave \"offline,\nthird-party, no Wakir-runtime state required\" to `wakir-verify`, which\nhas fulfilled it correctly since PR #6.\n\nRemoved:\n  * `wat/anchor/external_verifier/**` and `tests/wat/external_verifier/**`\n  * `[project.scripts]` entry `wat-verify`\n  * the Apache-2.0 carve-out in `REUSE.toml` and its `LICENSING.md` row\n  * `.github/workflows/external-verifier-drift.yml` — the whole file: it\n    is the python-bitcoinlib matrix for the deleted package and touches\n    nothing under `tooling/external-verifier-*`. That matrix was ported\n    to `wakir-verify` first (PR #7); the ajv/hyperjump conformance\n    tooling and its driver `scripts/external_verifier_validation.py`\n    are untouched.\n\nKept deliberately:\n  * `wakir-wat-verify` — different contract (BUSL-1.1, operator-facing,\n    needs the manifest archive), not affected by R3.\n  * `tests/wat/test_external_verifier_real_tv2_sig.py` — despite the\n    name it drives `scripts/external_verifier_validation.py`, not the\n    deleted package. Its subject stays, so it stays.\n  * `tests/wat/test_external_verifier_parity.py` — ajv ↔ jsonschema\n    parity, unrelated to the deleted code.\n\nCoverage: everything substantial from the deleted tests that\n`wakir-verify` lacked went there first in PR #7 (the python-bitcoinlib\ndrift matrix, which was dead there because no lane installed the\nlibrary). Three test functions and one module symbol were deliberately\nnot carried over — each encodes the pre-R3 semantics and each has a\nreplacement; named individually in the PR body.\n\nFollow-on edits: `tests/wat/test_aggregator_side_signing_e2e.py`\nimported two constants from the deleted fixtures module behind a\n`try/except ImportError` fallback holding byte-identical literals; the\nliterals are now inline, since an import guard that can only ever fire\nis worse than the constant it hides. `test_brand_proof_verifier_remains_apache`\nlost its subject and is replaced by `test_merkle_read_half_remains_apache`\n— the Merkle Read-Half is now the only Apache-2.0 carve-out inside a BSL\nsub-tree and was previously unguarded. The four `LICENSE-BSL.md` files\nno longer name a carve-out path that does not exist.\n\nThird parties are pointed at `wakir-labs/wakir-verify` from\n`docs/offline-verifier-for-third-parties.md` and the root README, with\nthe post-PR-#6 claims — hashlist consistency, event binding, payload,\nroot authenticity — and with the copy''s \"four independently-maintained\nverifiers\" wording explicitly withdrawn.\n\nLocal: full suite 3644 passed / 310 skipped (baseline 2193ed8: 3707 /\n311; the delta is exactly the 64 tests of the deleted suite, the infra\ntest swap nets to zero). `reuse lint` 1358/1358. Archaeology lint clean.\nREUSE-wrap lint INTACT. No required status context references the\ndeleted paths.\n\nRefs: ADR-0074, ADR-0062 Cut-1, ADR-0023b\n\nCo-authored-by: Reza Tehrani <fabian@callandor.gmbh>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqGduCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAef4QADMrt3y/5JJxirPj/V7c25z1\nT3vBdVARk4pLXtyNqInK4iWhkwKBmKBqqvKymUxRp4sDvNVdUXcSo6W4VIk2tN3y\nk8vHz7QSpOO8SiB/9gktB/N5yC51mvNa+UHWatAIU6nRMavfhN9DGphuQTwBkgN5\ntFmc/I02Rdufoj3uIe0yW7RzKyiHkTJC+vIwwNsH8dFAVqBbXk7QXpNVIGxOEMyk\nHUJ5OuOIdrlZbU92F1LV+KDVT/kwv8k8TU3SnC++uUY5eyS1QLA+v/DZEJmtLeVX\nE76iOjYZIb/x1p0YoXJsiNukoaZT4lHnYxX2iTD3HL9ul3jLA05cgL+uUxbz5LRm\nPZBhbOPM+ZSO60a8a3pN0oCC2F+NrCt92uxBNf+lSLKuxdXahlkLAsBcE3zs0i2f\nFs65JRl/sOpfkWo/2rgSrhZ1KOXCYQTP5pQ3Jb+ggZK9LtK2Cyj3Nr6sExS/obo7\nQQmep8doXmQ6q+s1m2Pq+LjcQ3Q3pu44/EMbY9g1dt8ReTqyj2e//f7FneWPGxpt\nayIOvbE8I6zYQbsWgPGGIAilTGTjxspfno6mrcOwpv22kKHckFP94p6m0S4pUG9e\n4Lz009srS9ga+BoyX8V7uk5I9im4x3eqL2Wb02t9BnYZLFJgdFoPTlDhvrrFaLEM\nIRcp/rSODkE8forXrEtj\n=MZtv\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-14T21:30:23Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/527508ee7eb8fa9ca267d5a66033bc2f03f19af2","node_id":"C_kwDOSVv36NoAKDUyNzUwOGVlN2ViOGZhOWNhMjY3ZDVhNjYwMzNiYzJmMDNmMTlhZjI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commit/2193ed889e752580aeda5e19543dd0d3011ffe61","sha":"2193ed889e752580aeda5e19543dd0d3011ffe61","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/2193ed889e752580aeda5e19543dd0d3011ffe61"}],"sha":"527508ee7eb8fa9ca267d5a66033bc2f03f19af2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/527508ee7eb8fa9ca267d5a66033bc2f03f19af2"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/281068747?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/wakir-labs","id":281068747,"login":"wakir-labs","node_id":"U_kgDOEMDEyw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/wakir-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/commits/2193ed889e752580aeda5e19543dd0d3011ffe61/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-14T20:52:47Z","email":"xypu4r8e@addy.io","name":"wakir-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-14T20:52:47Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(proof-path): strict report semantics, no input repair, no fail-open aggregator (R1/R5/R6) (#537)\n\n* fix(ci): report validator is strict by default (R1)\n\nThe proof-path report validator used a deny-list (`FORBIDDEN_STATUSES\n= (\"failed\", \"not_run\")`) where its own docstring promised an\nallow-list (\"every step must have run and succeeded\"). Four classes of\nself-contradictory report passed the gate, reproduced against the\npinned baseline with the CI invocation:\n\n  protocol_event status=skipped exit_code=20   -> rc=0 accepted\n  protocol_event status=banana                 -> rc=0 accepted\n  merkle_manifest status=ok exit_code=999      -> rc=0 accepted\n  external_verify ok + proof_verified=false    -> rc=0 accepted\n\nAll four now exit 1.\n\n- Allow-list instead of deny-list: the strict profile accepts exactly\n  `ok`. Missing / unknown / skipped / failed / not_run all fail.\n- `exit_code` is checked against the status via the driver''s own\n  status->code map, not just `isinstance(int)`. Booleans stay out.\n- Step details are cross-checked against the status: a successful\n  `external_verify` must carry manifest_consistent / root_match /\n  leaf_present / proof_verified all true, `inclusion_proof` must carry\n  verified=true, `runtime_bridge` bridge_status=\"ok\". No successful\n  step may carry `fault_injected` or `envelope_projection_used`.\n- Leniency is now the explicit flag `--allow-skipped-external-verify`,\n  which CI does not set; the default is strict.\n  `--require-external-verify-ok` stays accepted (proof-path.yml and\n  QUICKSTART pass it) as a no-op that additionally cancels the\n  relaxation, so passing both is strict.\n- Docstring rewritten to the contract the code now enforces.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(demo-proof): the spool is the input of step 3, not a suggestion (R5)\n\n`_spool_jsonl_to_aggregator_events()` filled missing *or falsy* B1\nfields from `event.envelope.json` (`record.get(x) or envelope.get(x)`),\nso bridge-to-spool drift was repaired instead of reported. The\narchitecture map claims the opposite (`layers.md:227`).\n\nThis was not a dormant safety net. It was load-bearing on every green\nrun: the bridge audit writer emits `capability_token_hash: \"\"` — the\nleaf-projection spec''s no-capability sentinel — and the old code read\nthat as \"missing\" and substituted the demo envelope''s all-zero\nplaceholder. Every manifest, root and inclusion proof the proof path\nhas produced so far committed to a B1 tuple that no spool line ever\nheld. Detail in the PR body.\n\n- Strict by default: a B1 field that is absent, non-string or empty\n  fails the step with the spool file and line number.\n- `capability_token_hash` is exempt from the emptiness rule and passes\n  through verbatim, per `wirelang/specs/wat-leaf-projection.md` §3.4\n  and the `compute_leaf_hash` docstring. The aggregator input now\n  byte-matches the spool''s B1 tuple; the demo''s Merkle root changes\n  accordingly (no fixture pinned the old value).\n- The envelope fallback survives as `--allow-envelope-projection` /\n  `DEMO_PROOF_ALLOW_ENVELOPE_PROJECTION=1`, which the proof path never\n  sets. Field-name aliases (`event_time`) moved behind the same flag.\n- Every run writes `manifest-projection.json` and the driver carries\n  `envelope_projection_used` into the report, so a projected run is\n  visible rather than inferred. The strict report validator requires\n  that flag to be present and false.\n\nAlso in this commit: the validator tests move to the strict contract\nfrom the R1 fix. Two expectations were rewritten rather than added,\nboth because the old expectation *was* the defect\n(`test_skipped_external_verify_passes_without_require_flag` asserted\nthe permissive default). Nothing was deleted; the R1 negative matrix\nis appended as regression tests.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ci): required aggregator contexts must not fail open on a red dependency (R6)\n\n`runtime-acceptance-gates.yml`''s aggregator job carries the only\nbranch-protection context of its lane (`runtime acceptance gates`; the\nfive gate jobs are not required contexts — verified live on 2026-09-14,\n13 contexts, `enforce_admins: false`). It depended on those five jobs\nvia `needs` with no job-level `if: always()`, and its own comment said\nso: \"If any single-gate job is RED, the aggregator skips.\" GitHub does\nnot block a merge on a skipped required check.\n\n- `if: always()` on the aggregator job, plus a first step that turns\n  each `needs.<job>.result` into the job''s exit code. The result check\n  runs before the aggregator test, so a green aggregate can never mask\n  a red gate.\n- Same class, same fix in `tests.yml`: `drift-detection`\n  (`production-vs-sandbox drift envelope`) is also a required context\n  with `needs` and no `always()`. Found by the repository-wide sweep,\n  not by the review.\n- Sweep result for the other 39 workflows: four more jobs use `needs`\n  without `always()` (`e2e-bringup-ci.yml` x2, `selinux-hermetic-lane.yml`,\n  `wirelang-spec-ots-pre-anchor-probe.yml`). None of them is a required\n  context, so none of them can fail open into a merge. Listed in the PR,\n  left alone.\n- `tests/workflows/test_required_context_error_propagation.py` pins both\n  halves for every required context that uses `needs`, and carries its\n  own negative control against a vacuously-passing sweep.\n- `tests/ci/test_branch_protection_check_names_audit.py` pinned a stale\n  10-context set from 2026-09-11. Corrected to the live 13: the three\n  Phase-4 contexts are no longer pending, and `secret-scan` had been\n  required all along without ever being inventoried.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(ci): TEMPORARY R6 negative control — force gate-2-3 red\n\nReverted in the next commit. Proves the required context\n`runtime acceptance gates` reports RED rather than skipped when a\nsingle gate job fails.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(bridge): carry the audited capability digest into the spool leaf\n\nRevert of the temporary R6 negative control, plus the consequence of\nmaking the spool authoritative (R5).\n\nWith the envelope repair gone, the proof path went red in\n`cross-repo compatibility (protocol ↔ runtime ↔ verify)`:\n\n    [fail] manifest manifest.json violates protocol manifest schema:\n           events/0/capability_token_hash: '''' does not match\n           ''^[0-9a-f]{64}$''\n\nTwo separate defects were hiding behind each other:\n\n1. `wat/anchor/bridge_audit_writer.py` hardcoded\n   `capability_token_hash=\"\"` — the writer dropped the capability\n   binding of the event it was auditing, and had no parameter to\n   accept one. It now takes `capability_token_hash`, defaulting to the\n   no-capability sentinel so existing callers are unchanged, and the\n   demo passes the event''s declared digest from step 1 through step 2.\n   The manifest root is byte-identical to the pre-fix root — the\n   difference is that the value is now in the spool, where the proof\n   claims it came from.\n\n2. `capability_token_hash` has two contradictory definitions across\n   the repositories: `wirelang/specs/wat-leaf-projection.md` §3.4 and\n   `compute_leaf_hash` define `\"\"` as the normative no-capability\n   value; the wakir-protocol manifest schema requires\n   `^[0-9a-f]{64}$`. Not touched here — it is a wakir-protocol\n   question. Documented in `docs/architecture/layers.md` and in the\n   writer docstring, and a test pins that the sentinel passes through\n   rather than being repaired, with the cross-repo consequence spelled\n   out.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(wat): land the Zone-3 capability_token_hash follow-up\n\nRemoving the envelope repair (R5) turned `cross-repo compatibility\n(protocol ↔ runtime ↔ verify)` red and named an existing pin:\n\n    tests/compat/test_zone3_capability_token_hash.py\n    \"bridge_audit_writer no longer emits the empty sentinel — good:\n     now tighten aggregator_cli._validate_events and update this\n     Zone-3 pin module\"\n\nThe contradiction was already found and already decided (Cross-Review\nZone 3, ADR-0072 W4): inside an anchored manifest\n`capability_token_hash` is a fixed-width digest, the canonical schema''s\n`^[0-9a-f]{64}$` is right, and the pilot writer''s empty string becomes\nthe all-zero digest. The producer change was a tracked follow-up of\nPR #526 that nothing forced, because the demo''s envelope repair made\nthe manifest schema-valid while the spool said something else. With\nthe repair gone, the follow-up became load-bearing. Both halves land\nhere:\n\n- Producer: `wat/anchor/bridge_audit_writer.py` exports\n  `NO_CAPABILITY_DIGEST = \"0\" * 64` and defaults to it, and forwards a\n  caller-supplied `capability_token_hash` into the leaf tuple.\n- Consumer: `wat/cmd/aggregator_cli._validate_events` enforces the\n  schema pattern on `payload_hash` and `capability_token_hash`\n  (`HEX64_FIELDS`), so the aggregator refuses to build a manifest it\n  could not publish, with the event index in the message.\n- `compute_leaf_hash` stays permissive, as the Zone-3 note requires;\n  `tests/fixtures/jcs-leaf-vectors` still covers the empty tuple.\n- The pin module now pins the post-follow-up state: the two\n  \"…_today\" tests become real assertions (aggregator rejects the empty\n  string and four more non-hex64 shapes; writer defaults to and\n  forwards the digest), asserted against behaviour rather than a\n  source-code substring.\n- An empty capability in the spool is still not repaired by step 3: it\n  is passed through and rejected by the aggregator, which is where\n  that rule belongs. Test pins exactly that layering.\n\nAlso in this commit: the documentation corrections listed in the PR\nbody (STABILITY.md lines 55/56/80/83/103/109, layers.md rows 3/6/7 and\nthe honest-notes section, QUICKSTART.md validator invocation,\ndocs/ci/branch-protection-required-checks.md §1 inventory,\n`rust_backend_switch.py` module docstring).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: the OTS fixture evidence covers 8 of 10 receipts, not all of them\n\n`STABILITY.md` L72 cited `tests/fixtures/wat-tv*-real/*/root.bin.ots`\nas \"real receipts with real Bitcoin attestation\". The glob spans two\ncohorts and the claim only holds for one of them.\n\nCounted by attestation tag on 2026-09-14 (Bitcoin `0588960d73d71901`\nvs. pending `83dfe30d2ef90c8e`), all 10 `.ots` files in the tree:\n\n    bitcoin pending  file\n          4       4  wat-tv2-real/2026-05-27T00..03    (4 files)\n          4       4  wat-tv2-real-signed/2026-05-27T00..03 (4 files)\n          0       4  wat-tv3-real/2026-05-26T17\n          0       4  wat-real-manifest\n\n`wat-tv3-real` is inside the cited glob and has no Bitcoin binding at\nall — pending calendar attestations only, never upgraded.\n`wat-real-manifest` is in the same state and outside the glob.\n\nBoth the STABILITY row and the matching claim in\n`docs/architecture/layers.md` (the Bitcoin-time-binding honest note,\nsame glob) now name the two cohorts separately. No fixture touched —\nthis is a description correction, same class as the other maturity-\nmatrix corrections in this PR: claimed evidence that is only partly\nthere.\n\nFound by Reza in wakir-verify (he pins the two pending cases as\n`structural_ok` rather than configuring them away); tag counts\nre-verified independently here.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Fred <fabian@callandor.gmbh>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"723a7f1a62ee3b2de81c867a6dc34650fb888284","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/trees/723a7f1a62ee3b2de81c867a6dc34650fb888284"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/git/commits/2193ed889e752580aeda5e19543dd0d3011ffe61","verification":{"payload":"tree 723a7f1a62ee3b2de81c867a6dc34650fb888284\nparent 9bb5ab1f58d48850ca67db78ed7f5dc5b20a3daf\nauthor wakir-labs <xypu4r8e@addy.io> 1789419167 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789419167 +0200\n\nfix(proof-path): strict report semantics, no input repair, no fail-open aggregator (R1/R5/R6) (#537)\n\n* fix(ci): report validator is strict by default (R1)\n\nThe proof-path report validator used a deny-list (`FORBIDDEN_STATUSES\n= (\"failed\", \"not_run\")`) where its own docstring promised an\nallow-list (\"every step must have run and succeeded\"). Four classes of\nself-contradictory report passed the gate, reproduced against the\npinned baseline with the CI invocation:\n\n  protocol_event status=skipped exit_code=20   -> rc=0 accepted\n  protocol_event status=banana                 -> rc=0 accepted\n  merkle_manifest status=ok exit_code=999      -> rc=0 accepted\n  external_verify ok + proof_verified=false    -> rc=0 accepted\n\nAll four now exit 1.\n\n- Allow-list instead of deny-list: the strict profile accepts exactly\n  `ok`. Missing / unknown / skipped / failed / not_run all fail.\n- `exit_code` is checked against the status via the driver''s own\n  status->code map, not just `isinstance(int)`. Booleans stay out.\n- Step details are cross-checked against the status: a successful\n  `external_verify` must carry manifest_consistent / root_match /\n  leaf_present / proof_verified all true, `inclusion_proof` must carry\n  verified=true, `runtime_bridge` bridge_status=\"ok\". No successful\n  step may carry `fault_injected` or `envelope_projection_used`.\n- Leniency is now the explicit flag `--allow-skipped-external-verify`,\n  which CI does not set; the default is strict.\n  `--require-external-verify-ok` stays accepted (proof-path.yml and\n  QUICKSTART pass it) as a no-op that additionally cancels the\n  relaxation, so passing both is strict.\n- Docstring rewritten to the contract the code now enforces.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(demo-proof): the spool is the input of step 3, not a suggestion (R5)\n\n`_spool_jsonl_to_aggregator_events()` filled missing *or falsy* B1\nfields from `event.envelope.json` (`record.get(x) or envelope.get(x)`),\nso bridge-to-spool drift was repaired instead of reported. The\narchitecture map claims the opposite (`layers.md:227`).\n\nThis was not a dormant safety net. It was load-bearing on every green\nrun: the bridge audit writer emits `capability_token_hash: \"\"` — the\nleaf-projection spec''s no-capability sentinel — and the old code read\nthat as \"missing\" and substituted the demo envelope''s all-zero\nplaceholder. Every manifest, root and inclusion proof the proof path\nhas produced so far committed to a B1 tuple that no spool line ever\nheld. Detail in the PR body.\n\n- Strict by default: a B1 field that is absent, non-string or empty\n  fails the step with the spool file and line number.\n- `capability_token_hash` is exempt from the emptiness rule and passes\n  through verbatim, per `wirelang/specs/wat-leaf-projection.md` §3.4\n  and the `compute_leaf_hash` docstring. The aggregator input now\n  byte-matches the spool''s B1 tuple; the demo''s Merkle root changes\n  accordingly (no fixture pinned the old value).\n- The envelope fallback survives as `--allow-envelope-projection` /\n  `DEMO_PROOF_ALLOW_ENVELOPE_PROJECTION=1`, which the proof path never\n  sets. Field-name aliases (`event_time`) moved behind the same flag.\n- Every run writes `manifest-projection.json` and the driver carries\n  `envelope_projection_used` into the report, so a projected run is\n  visible rather than inferred. The strict report validator requires\n  that flag to be present and false.\n\nAlso in this commit: the validator tests move to the strict contract\nfrom the R1 fix. Two expectations were rewritten rather than added,\nboth because the old expectation *was* the defect\n(`test_skipped_external_verify_passes_without_require_flag` asserted\nthe permissive default). Nothing was deleted; the R1 negative matrix\nis appended as regression tests.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ci): required aggregator contexts must not fail open on a red dependency (R6)\n\n`runtime-acceptance-gates.yml`''s aggregator job carries the only\nbranch-protection context of its lane (`runtime acceptance gates`; the\nfive gate jobs are not required contexts — verified live on 2026-09-14,\n13 contexts, `enforce_admins: false`). It depended on those five jobs\nvia `needs` with no job-level `if: always()`, and its own comment said\nso: \"If any single-gate job is RED, the aggregator skips.\" GitHub does\nnot block a merge on a skipped required check.\n\n- `if: always()` on the aggregator job, plus a first step that turns\n  each `needs.<job>.result` into the job''s exit code. The result check\n  runs before the aggregator test, so a green aggregate can never mask\n  a red gate.\n- Same class, same fix in `tests.yml`: `drift-detection`\n  (`production-vs-sandbox drift envelope`) is also a required context\n  with `needs` and no `always()`. Found by the repository-wide sweep,\n  not by the review.\n- Sweep result for the other 39 workflows: four more jobs use `needs`\n  without `always()` (`e2e-bringup-ci.yml` x2, `selinux-hermetic-lane.yml`,\n  `wirelang-spec-ots-pre-anchor-probe.yml`). None of them is a required\n  context, so none of them can fail open into a merge. Listed in the PR,\n  left alone.\n- `tests/workflows/test_required_context_error_propagation.py` pins both\n  halves for every required context that uses `needs`, and carries its\n  own negative control against a vacuously-passing sweep.\n- `tests/ci/test_branch_protection_check_names_audit.py` pinned a stale\n  10-context set from 2026-09-11. Corrected to the live 13: the three\n  Phase-4 contexts are no longer pending, and `secret-scan` had been\n  required all along without ever being inventoried.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(ci): TEMPORARY R6 negative control — force gate-2-3 red\n\nReverted in the next commit. Proves the required context\n`runtime acceptance gates` reports RED rather than skipped when a\nsingle gate job fails.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(bridge): carry the audited capability digest into the spool leaf\n\nRevert of the temporary R6 negative control, plus the consequence of\nmaking the spool authoritative (R5).\n\nWith the envelope repair gone, the proof path went red in\n`cross-repo compatibility (protocol ↔ runtime ↔ verify)`:\n\n    [fail] manifest manifest.json violates protocol manifest schema:\n           events/0/capability_token_hash: '''' does not match\n           ''^[0-9a-f]{64}$''\n\nTwo separate defects were hiding behind each other:\n\n1. `wat/anchor/bridge_audit_writer.py` hardcoded\n   `capability_token_hash=\"\"` — the writer dropped the capability\n   binding of the event it was auditing, and had no parameter to\n   accept one. It now takes `capability_token_hash`, defaulting to the\n   no-capability sentinel so existing callers are unchanged, and the\n   demo passes the event''s declared digest from step 1 through step 2.\n   The manifest root is byte-identical to the pre-fix root — the\n   difference is that the value is now in the spool, where the proof\n   claims it came from.\n\n2. `capability_token_hash` has two contradictory definitions across\n   the repositories: `wirelang/specs/wat-leaf-projection.md` §3.4 and\n   `compute_leaf_hash` define `\"\"` as the normative no-capability\n   value; the wakir-protocol manifest schema requires\n   `^[0-9a-f]{64}$`. Not touched here — it is a wakir-protocol\n   question. Documented in `docs/architecture/layers.md` and in the\n   writer docstring, and a test pins that the sentinel passes through\n   rather than being repaired, with the cross-repo consequence spelled\n   out.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(wat): land the Zone-3 capability_token_hash follow-up\n\nRemoving the envelope repair (R5) turned `cross-repo compatibility\n(protocol ↔ runtime ↔ verify)` red and named an existing pin:\n\n    tests/compat/test_zone3_capability_token_hash.py\n    \"bridge_audit_writer no longer emits the empty sentinel — good:\n     now tighten aggregator_cli._validate_events and update this\n     Zone-3 pin module\"\n\nThe contradiction was already found and already decided (Cross-Review\nZone 3, ADR-0072 W4): inside an anchored manifest\n`capability_token_hash` is a fixed-width digest, the canonical schema''s\n`^[0-9a-f]{64}$` is right, and the pilot writer''s empty string becomes\nthe all-zero digest. The producer change was a tracked follow-up of\nPR #526 that nothing forced, because the demo''s envelope repair made\nthe manifest schema-valid while the spool said something else. With\nthe repair gone, the follow-up became load-bearing. Both halves land\nhere:\n\n- Producer: `wat/anchor/bridge_audit_writer.py` exports\n  `NO_CAPABILITY_DIGEST = \"0\" * 64` and defaults to it, and forwards a\n  caller-supplied `capability_token_hash` into the leaf tuple.\n- Consumer: `wat/cmd/aggregator_cli._validate_events` enforces the\n  schema pattern on `payload_hash` and `capability_token_hash`\n  (`HEX64_FIELDS`), so the aggregator refuses to build a manifest it\n  could not publish, with the event index in the message.\n- `compute_leaf_hash` stays permissive, as the Zone-3 note requires;\n  `tests/fixtures/jcs-leaf-vectors` still covers the empty tuple.\n- The pin module now pins the post-follow-up state: the two\n  \"…_today\" tests become real assertions (aggregator rejects the empty\n  string and four more non-hex64 shapes; writer defaults to and\n  forwards the digest), asserted against behaviour rather than a\n  source-code substring.\n- An empty capability in the spool is still not repaired by step 3: it\n  is passed through and rejected by the aggregator, which is where\n  that rule belongs. Test pins exactly that layering.\n\nAlso in this commit: the documentation corrections listed in the PR\nbody (STABILITY.md lines 55/56/80/83/103/109, layers.md rows 3/6/7 and\nthe honest-notes section, QUICKSTART.md validator invocation,\ndocs/ci/branch-protection-required-checks.md §1 inventory,\n`rust_backend_switch.py` module docstring).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: the OTS fixture evidence covers 8 of 10 receipts, not all of them\n\n`STABILITY.md` L72 cited `tests/fixtures/wat-tv*-real/*/root.bin.ots`\nas \"real receipts with real Bitcoin attestation\". The glob spans two\ncohorts and the claim only holds for one of them.\n\nCounted by attestation tag on 2026-09-14 (Bitcoin `0588960d73d71901`\nvs. pending `83dfe30d2ef90c8e`), all 10 `.ots` files in the tree:\n\n    bitcoin pending  file\n          4       4  wat-tv2-real/2026-05-27T00..03    (4 files)\n          4       4  wat-tv2-real-signed/2026-05-27T00..03 (4 files)\n          0       4  wat-tv3-real/2026-05-26T17\n          0       4  wat-real-manifest\n\n`wat-tv3-real` is inside the cited glob and has no Bitcoin binding at\nall — pending calendar attestations only, never upgraded.\n`wat-real-manifest` is in the same state and outside the glob.\n\nBoth the STABILITY row and the matching claim in\n`docs/architecture/layers.md` (the Bitcoin-time-binding honest note,\nsame glob) now name the two cohorts separately. No fixture touched —\nthis is a description correction, same class as the other maturity-\nmatrix corrections in this PR: claimed evidence that is only partly\nthere.\n\nFound by Reza in wakir-verify (he pins the two pending cases as\n`structural_ok` rather than configuring them away); tag counts\nre-verified independently here.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Fred <fabian@callandor.gmbh>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqF6fCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAQI8QAKgZBnbrxffmVPQg1Kc41B36\nA1Tt7RryAHsIkn8Vfvt0ztGcr5pd9jNoh3bk6T8ZbN95h9HnA9KufHhvTSC4GXuF\noGh5bhX1kKn1tbRtDLFpT7hzZecUVToirzrrR5YKyIg0do2fZ8AWYMxMyx7K863l\nbWwHFxyA0XUbX2L6TRpWDI6lUyLVpe19a/i/vCGKs9bjgQbmy33ePI8h8fEzwb6N\n2JcYuaytbBiNTl/PNaulq5r14pzVHEb9PNWmTsjLoZCizwDvb7frhXB7PBiRDjLR\nfFKwy7Tz3+isKA3GujugRQkyPDIkp4iVJpkO25k/H8902YP+i2YOdYikiD0AOhcF\nILvcyD/KzYjPrGovPn1YN5xVyk/j8Z5uJpSV5Gc1PTQuR9/+5iemw3NinUv3wOiI\nt9zmsMS4VJjtez6v0Woc6DXWycaM3SVns3bf6VqtjbHg0vOp/po7m6/FYgMic8IK\n6VYxXzDC/+gEHeWbZPRjlq6PCCcZQD/MT7NvETG6D/XCYo4eeF0BztICLjM4LZKh\noACS31GeBDKu/Y23XcTt9FhApFYCuZicyjC96CL+iQXXsa5Zbs9SVaoZbdyFF8Ns\nooqDUlskuXPa+ONYp8jW6GjB84VHof4LAuv8jmUHqDVp8Ed4eqRU5/GVZPDzKMMP\nkTxy3ShUQTidPP9uhiCJ\n=Q2Me\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-14T20:52:48Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_